// The Human Body, more articles: muscles, the lymphatic system, stress, rabies and measles.
ADDART({id:"muscles",t:"Muscles",c:"Human Body",i:"💪",w:"Muscle",r:["bones","heart","exercise","nervous","nutri"],
p:[["Moving the body","Muscles let us move, from running and lifting to blinking and smiling."],["Three kinds","The body has skeletal, smooth and cardiac muscle."],["Pulling power","Muscles can only pull, so they often work in pairs."]],
l:"Muscles are soft tissues that can contract, or shorten, to produce movement. The human body has more than 600 skeletal muscles, which are attached to bones and controlled by choice, as well as smooth muscle in the walls of organs such as the stomach and blood vessels, and cardiac muscle, which makes up the heart. Muscles make up a large share of body weight, about a third or more in many adults. Besides moving the body, they keep it upright, protect organs, move food through the gut and produce heat. Regular exercise and a healthy diet help keep muscles strong throughout life.",
s:[["Skeletal muscle","Skeletal muscles are attached to bones by tough cords called tendons. They are under voluntary control, meaning we decide when to move them. Under a microscope they look striped. They range from large muscles in the thighs and buttocks to tiny muscles that move the eyes."],["Smooth muscle","Smooth muscle lines hollow organs such as the stomach, intestines, bladder and blood vessels. It works automatically, without our thinking about it, squeezing food along the gut, controlling blood flow and adjusting the size of the pupils."],["Cardiac muscle","Cardiac muscle is found only in the heart. It contracts rhythmically about 60 to 100 times a minute at rest, every minute of a person’s life, without tiring. Its cells are linked so that electrical signals spread quickly and make the heart beat in a coordinated way."],["Working in pairs","A muscle can pull but cannot push. To bend and straighten a joint, muscles work in opposing pairs. When the biceps on the front of the upper arm contracts, the elbow bends; when the triceps on the back contracts, the arm straightens."]],
k:[["Skeletal muscles","More than 600"],["Types","Skeletal, smooth, cardiac"],["Largest muscle","Gluteus maximus (buttock)"],["Smallest muscle","Stapedius (in the ear)"],["Muscle proteins","Actin and myosin"]],
d:[["c. 170","The Greek physician Galen describes muscles and their actions"],["1543","Andreas Vesalius publishes detailed drawings of human muscles"],["1791","Luigi Galvani shows that electricity makes frog muscles twitch"],["1864","Wilhelm Kühne isolates the muscle protein myosin"],["1954","The sliding filament theory of muscle contraction is proposed"],["1986","Scientists identify the gene behind Duchenne muscular dystrophy"]],
s2:[["How a muscle contracts","Each muscle is made of bundles of long cells called muscle fibers. Inside them, thin strands of the proteins actin and myosin overlap. When a nerve signal arrives, myosin pulls on actin, sliding the strands past each other so the fiber shortens. This explanation, the sliding filament theory, was proposed in 1954 by two research teams in Britain."],["Fuel for muscles","Muscles run on a molecule called ATP, made from glucose and fat using oxygen. During short bursts of hard effort, muscles can make energy without enough oxygen, but this cannot last long. Fit muscles have more blood vessels and energy-producing mitochondria, so they tire less easily."],["Building strength","When muscles are worked harder than usual, as in lifting weights or climbing, tiny fibers are strained and then rebuilt thicker and stronger. Protein from food, such as fish, eggs, beans and meat, supplies the building blocks, while rest and sleep give muscles time to repair."],["Cramps and strains","A cramp is a sudden, painful tightening of a muscle that will not relax, often after exercise or in hot weather. A strain, or pulled muscle, is a tear in muscle fibers caused by overstretching. Gentle stretching, warming up before activity and drinking enough water help prevent both."],["Muscles and aging","After about the age of 30, people slowly lose muscle mass and strength unless they stay active. In older adults, severe muscle loss, called sarcopenia, can lead to falls and loss of independence. Strength exercises, even simple ones such as standing up from a chair, help older people stay strong."],["Muscle diseases","Muscular dystrophies are inherited diseases in which muscles gradually weaken. Duchenne muscular dystrophy, the most common severe form in children, mainly affects boys. Other conditions, such as myasthenia gravis, interfere with the signals between nerves and muscles. Researchers are testing gene therapies and new medicines for these diseases."]],
sp:["The muscles of a smile","Every expression on the human face is made by small muscles attached to the skin rather than to bones. A genuine smile uses muscles that lift the corners of the mouth and others that crinkle the skin around the eyes, while a polite, forced smile often moves only the mouth. The French doctor Guillaume Duchenne studied facial expressions in the 1800s by stimulating face muscles with electricity, and the true, eye-crinkling smile is still called the Duchenne smile. Facial muscles also help us chew, speak, blink and protect the eyes."],
g:[["Tendon","A tough cord that attaches muscle to bone"],["Muscle fiber","A long cell that makes up muscle"],["Contraction","The shortening of a muscle"],["Sarcopenia","Loss of muscle mass and strength with age"]],
f:["The body has more than 600 skeletal muscles.","The heart is made of cardiac muscle.","Muscles can only pull, never push."],
fun:"The smallest muscle in the body, the stapedius in the middle ear, is only about a millimeter long."});

ADDART({id:"lymphatic",t:"The Lymphatic System",c:"Human Body",i:"🫧",w:"Lymphatic system",r:["immune","blood","heart","cancer","digest"],
p:[["The body’s drainage","The lymphatic system collects extra fluid from tissues and returns it to the blood."],["Fighting germs","Its lymph nodes filter out germs and help the immune system."],["Nodes and organs","It includes lymph vessels, lymph nodes, the spleen, the thymus and the tonsils."]],
l:"The lymphatic system is a network of thin vessels, small organs called lymph nodes, and other tissues that work alongside the blood circulation. Each day, fluid leaks out of tiny blood vessels into the body’s tissues. The lymphatic system collects this fluid, now called lymph, filters it through lymph nodes, where white blood cells trap and destroy germs, and returns it to the bloodstream near the heart. The system also absorbs fats from the intestines and is a key part of the immune system. Its main parts include lymph vessels, several hundred lymph nodes, the spleen, the thymus, the tonsils and patches of tissue in the gut.",
s:[["Lymph and lymph vessels","Lymph is a clear or milky fluid containing water, proteins, fats and white blood cells. It travels through lymph vessels that branch through almost every part of the body. Unlike blood, lymph has no heart to pump it; it is pushed along by the squeezing of nearby muscles and by breathing, while tiny valves keep it flowing in one direction."],["Lymph nodes","Lymph nodes are small, bean-shaped organs found in clusters in the neck, armpits, groin, chest and abdomen. They act like filters. White blood cells inside them recognize germs and abnormal cells and begin an immune response. When fighting an infection, nearby nodes may swell and feel tender, often called swollen glands."],["The spleen and thymus","The spleen, under the ribs on the left side, filters blood, removes old red blood cells and stores white blood cells. The thymus, in the chest behind the breastbone, is where a type of white blood cell called the T cell matures; it is largest in childhood and shrinks after puberty."],["Absorbing fats","In the lining of the small intestine are tiny lymph vessels called lacteals. They absorb fats from digested food, which turn the lymph milky white. This fatty lymph is then carried to the bloodstream."]],
k:[["Fluid","Lymph"],["Main organs","Lymph nodes, spleen, thymus, tonsils"],["Number of lymph nodes","Several hundred"],["Key cells","Lymphocytes (white blood cells)"],["Returns lymph to blood","Near the heart"]],
d:[["1622","Gaspare Aselli describes milky lymph vessels in the gut of a dog"],["1651","Jean Pecquet describes the main lymph duct"],["1653","Olof Rudbeck and Thomas Bartholin publish descriptions of the lymphatic system"],["1832","Thomas Hodgkin describes the lymph node cancer later named after him"],["1961","The role of the thymus in immunity is discovered"],["2015","Researchers report lymph vessels in the coverings of the brain"]],
s2:[["Discovering the system","For centuries doctors knew about blood vessels but not lymph vessels. In 1622 the Italian physician Gaspare Aselli noticed white, milky threads in the intestines of a dog that had recently eaten. Over the following decades scientists including Jean Pecquet, Olof Rudbeck and Thomas Bartholin traced the network and described its connection to the blood."],["Swollen nodes","Lymph nodes often swell during common infections such as colds, sore throats and ear infections, and return to normal when the illness passes. Nodes that stay swollen for weeks, feel hard or grow without explanation should be checked by a doctor, because they can occasionally be a sign of more serious illness."],["Lymphoma","Lymphoma is a cancer of lymphocytes, the white blood cells of the lymphatic system. The two main groups are Hodgkin lymphoma and non-Hodgkin lymphoma. Many types can be treated successfully with chemotherapy, radiation and newer immune-based therapies."],["Lymphatic filariasis","Lymphatic filariasis, sometimes called elephantiasis, is caused by tiny worms spread by mosquitoes. The worms damage lymph vessels, which can lead to severe swelling of the legs and other body parts. The Philippines and many other countries have run mass drug programs to eliminate the disease, and many provinces have been declared free of it."],["Lymphedema","When lymph vessels or nodes are damaged or removed, for example after cancer surgery, lymph can build up and cause long-lasting swelling called lymphedema. Treatment includes special massage, compression garments, exercise and careful skin care."],["Brain drainage","Scientists long believed the brain had no lymph vessels. In 2015 researchers reported finding lymph vessels in the meninges, the protective layers around the brain. The discovery has led to new research on how the brain clears waste and how this might relate to diseases such as Alzheimer’s."]],
sp:["A hidden river","Most people know that blood circulates through the body, but few think about the second, quieter system flowing beside it. Every day, a few liters of fluid seep out of the blood into the spaces between cells. If it were not drained away, the body would swell like a sponge. The lymphatic system acts as a hidden river, gathering this fluid drop by drop, cleaning it as it passes through hundreds of lymph nodes, and pouring it back into large veins near the neck. Without it, the body could not keep its fluids balanced or fight off infections effectively."],
g:[["Lymph","Clear fluid that flows through lymph vessels"],["Lymph node","A small organ that filters lymph and helps fight infection"],["Lymphocyte","A type of white blood cell that fights infection"],["Lacteal","A lymph vessel in the small intestine that absorbs fat"]],
f:["The body has several hundred lymph nodes.","Swollen glands are usually lymph nodes fighting infection.","Lymph has no heart to pump it."],
fun:"Lymph turns milky white after a fatty meal because of the fat it carries from the intestines."});

ADDART({id:"stress",t:"Stress",c:"Health",i:"😮‍💨",w:"Stress (biology)",r:["mental","hormone","sleep","exercise","emotions"],
p:[["The body’s alarm","Stress is the body’s response to a challenge or threat."],["Helpful and harmful","Short bursts of stress can help us, but long-lasting stress can harm health."],["Coping","Sleep, exercise, talking to others and relaxing help people cope."]],
l:"Stress is the body’s natural response to pressure, danger or change. When a person faces a challenge, such as an exam, an argument or an emergency, the brain triggers the release of hormones such as adrenaline and cortisol. The heart beats faster, breathing quickens and muscles tense, preparing the body to act. This reaction, often called fight or flight, can be helpful in short bursts, sharpening attention and giving energy. When stress continues for a long time, however, it can affect sleep, mood, digestion, the immune system and the heart. Learning healthy ways to manage stress is an important part of physical and mental health.",
s:[["Fight or flight","When the brain senses a threat, a region called the hypothalamus signals the adrenal glands above the kidneys to release adrenaline. Within seconds the heart speeds up, blood pressure rises, breathing becomes faster and sugar is released into the blood for energy. The American physiologist Walter Cannon described this as the fight-or-flight response in 1915."],["Cortisol","If stress continues, the body releases cortisol, sometimes called the stress hormone. Cortisol helps keep energy available and controls inflammation. Over long periods, however, high cortisol levels are linked to poor sleep, weight gain, weaker immunity and difficulty concentrating."],["Good and bad stress","Not all stress is harmful. Moderate, short-term stress, sometimes called eustress, can motivate people to study, perform or meet a deadline. Stress becomes harmful when it is intense, constant or feels impossible to control, a condition called chronic stress."],["Signs of stress","Stress can show up as headaches, stomachaches, tight muscles, trouble sleeping, tiredness, irritability, worry, sadness or changes in appetite. Young people may notice difficulty concentrating in class or losing interest in activities they usually enjoy."]],
k:[["Main stress hormones","Adrenaline and cortisol"],["Fight-or-flight described","Walter Cannon, 1915"],["Stress research pioneer","Hans Selye"],["Helpful stress","Eustress"],["Long-lasting stress","Chronic stress"]],
d:[["1915","Walter Cannon describes the fight-or-flight response"],["1936","Hans Selye publishes his first work on the body’s response to stress"],["1967","The Holmes and Rahe scale ranks stressful life events"],["1980","Post-traumatic stress disorder is formally recognized"],["2018","The Philippines passes the Mental Health Act"],["2020","The COVID-19 pandemic raises stress levels worldwide"]],
s2:[["Hans Selye","The Hungarian-Canadian scientist Hans Selye spent decades studying how the body reacts to strain. In 1936 he described a pattern he later called the general adaptation syndrome, with stages of alarm, resistance and exhaustion. He helped make stress a common word in medicine and everyday language."],["Stress and the body","Long-term stress has been linked to high blood pressure, heart disease, stomach problems, headaches and a greater chance of catching infections. It can also make existing conditions, such as asthma or skin problems, worse. Researchers continue to study exactly how stress affects different organs."],["Stress and the mind","Ongoing stress can contribute to anxiety and depression. After very frightening events such as disasters, violence or accidents, some people develop post-traumatic stress disorder, with flashbacks, nightmares and strong fear. Support and treatment from trained professionals can help people recover."],["Healthy ways to cope","Helpful habits include getting enough sleep, being physically active, eating regular meals, spending time with friends and family, and taking breaks from screens. Slow breathing, prayer, meditation, music, hobbies and time outdoors can calm the body’s alarm system. Breaking big tasks into smaller steps can make them feel less overwhelming."],["Talking about it","Sharing worries with a trusted parent, teacher, friend, counselor or health worker can ease stress and help find solutions. In the Philippines, the Mental Health Act of 2018 aims to make mental health services more available, and the National Center for Mental Health runs a crisis hotline. Many schools also have guidance counselors."],["Stress in modern life","Surveys in many countries report high levels of stress linked to work, money, school, family responsibilities and constant digital connection. Long commutes, crowded cities and natural disasters add pressure in places such as Metro Manila. Some employers and schools now offer wellness programs, flexible hours or mental health days."]],
sp:["The five-minute calm-down","When stress builds up, simple techniques can help the body switch off its alarm. One popular method is box breathing: breathe in slowly for a count of four, hold for four, breathe out for four, and hold again for four, repeating several times. Another is the five senses exercise, in which a person names five things they can see, four they can touch, three they can hear, two they can smell and one they can taste. These techniques slow the heart rate and bring attention back to the present moment. They are used by students before exams, athletes before competitions and even soldiers and astronauts."],
g:[["Stress","The body’s response to pressure or threat"],["Adrenaline","A hormone that prepares the body for action"],["Cortisol","A hormone released during longer periods of stress"],["Chronic stress","Stress that lasts a long time"]],
f:["Adrenaline makes the heart beat faster.","Some stress can be helpful.","Talking to someone can reduce stress."],
fun:"The word stress was borrowed from engineering, where it describes the force acting on a material such as a metal beam."});

ADDART({id:"rabies",t:"Rabies",c:"Disease and Medicine",i:"🐕",w:"Rabies",r:["vacc","pasteur","dogs","immune","firstaid"],
p:[["A deadly virus","Rabies is a viral disease that attacks the brain and is almost always fatal once symptoms begin."],["Spread by bites","Most human cases come from the bites of infected dogs."],["Preventable","Rabies can be prevented with prompt wound washing and vaccines."]],
l:"Rabies is a disease caused by a virus that infects the brain and nervous system of mammals, including humans. It is usually spread through the saliva of an infected animal, most often by a bite or scratch. In countries such as the Philippines, dogs cause almost all human cases. Once symptoms such as fever, confusion, fear of water and paralysis appear, rabies is almost always fatal. Yet it is entirely preventable: washing a wound immediately with soap and water and getting vaccines quickly after exposure can save a person’s life. Vaccinating dogs is the most effective way to stop the disease at its source.",
s:[["How rabies spreads","The rabies virus lives in the saliva of infected animals. It enters the body through a bite, a scratch or contact with broken skin or the eyes, nose or mouth. It then travels slowly along nerves to the brain, a journey that can take weeks or months. This delay gives time for vaccines to work if treatment starts early."],["Symptoms","Early signs include fever, headache and tingling or pain at the bite site. As the virus reaches the brain, people may become anxious, confused and agitated, have difficulty swallowing and develop a fear of water, called hydrophobia. Paralysis and coma follow. Infected animals may become unusually aggressive or unusually quiet and drool heavily."],["What to do after a bite","Anyone bitten or scratched by a dog, cat or other mammal should immediately wash the wound with soap and running water for about 15 minutes and go to a health center or animal bite treatment center. Doctors decide whether a series of rabies vaccines, and sometimes rabies antibodies, is needed. Home remedies such as applying garlic or having the wound sucked do not prevent rabies."],["A worldwide problem","The World Health Organization estimates that rabies kills tens of thousands of people every year, most of them in Asia and Africa and many of them children. Nearly all deaths could be prevented with vaccination of dogs and timely treatment of bite victims."]],
k:[["Cause","Rabies virus"],["Main source in humans","Dog bites"],["Fatality","Almost always fatal once symptoms appear"],["First vaccine","Louis Pasteur, 1885"],["World Rabies Day","28 September"]],
d:[["c. 1930 BC","The Laws of Eshnunna in Mesopotamia mention dogs that spread a deadly disease"],["1885","Louis Pasteur gives the first rabies vaccine to Joseph Meister"],["2004","A teenager in the United States survives rabies without vaccination"],["2007","The Philippines passes the Anti-Rabies Act"],["2007","The first World Rabies Day is held"],["2015","Global partners set a goal of ending dog-transmitted rabies deaths by 2030"]],
s2:[["Pasteur’s gamble","In July 1885 a nine-year-old boy named Joseph Meister was badly bitten by a rabid dog in France. His mother brought him to the scientist Louis Pasteur, who had been developing a rabies vaccine using dried spinal cord tissue from infected rabbits. Pasteur, who was not a medical doctor, gave the boy a series of injections. Meister survived, and the treatment soon spread around the world."],["Rabies in the Philippines","The Philippines is among the countries with the most rabies deaths, with a few hundred people dying each year. The Anti-Rabies Act of 2007 requires dog owners to have their pets vaccinated and registered and declares March as Rabies Awareness Month. Hundreds of animal bite treatment centers offer vaccines across the country."],["Vaccinating dogs","Experts agree that vaccinating at least 70 percent of dogs in a community can stop rabies from spreading. Countries in Latin America greatly reduced human rabies deaths through mass dog vaccination. Responsible pet ownership, including keeping dogs from roaming, also helps."],["Other animals","Besides dogs, rabies can be carried by cats, bats, monkeys, foxes, raccoons and other mammals. In the Americas, bats are a major source of human cases. Birds, reptiles and fish do not carry rabies."],["Surviving rabies","Only a handful of people are known to have survived rabies after symptoms began. In 2004 an American teenager named Jeanna Giese survived after doctors put her in an induced coma, a method called the Milwaukee protocol. Later attempts using the same method have rarely succeeded, so prevention remains essential."],["Myths and facts","Some people wait to see whether the biting dog stays healthy before seeking treatment, or rely on traditional healers called tandok or tawak. Health authorities warn that such delays can be fatal and urge immediate wound washing and medical care, whether or not the animal appears sick."]],
sp:["Joseph Meister’s story","Joseph Meister never forgot the man who saved his life. As an adult he worked as a caretaker at the Pasteur Institute in Paris, the research center founded in Louis Pasteur’s honor. According to a popular account, when German forces occupied Paris in 1940, Meister was still at his post. He died that year. Today World Rabies Day is held every 28 September, the anniversary of Pasteur’s death in 1895, to remind people that rabies is still a threat and that it can be prevented."],
g:[["Rabies","A deadly viral disease that attacks the nervous system"],["Post-exposure prophylaxis","Vaccines and treatment given after a possible exposure"],["Hydrophobia","Fear of water, a symptom of rabies"],["Saliva","The liquid in the mouth that can carry the rabies virus"]],
f:["Dogs cause most human rabies cases.","Rabies can be prevented with vaccines.","Wash any animal bite with soap and water."],
fun:"Rabies gets its name from the Latin word rabies, meaning madness or rage."});

ADDART({id:"measles",t:"Measles",c:"Disease and Medicine",i:"🤒",w:"Measles",r:["vacc","immune","pandemic","pregnancy","hygiene"],
p:[["Very contagious","Measles is one of the most contagious diseases known."],["Fever and rash","It causes high fever, cough, red eyes and a spreading rash."],["Prevented by vaccine","Two doses of measles vaccine give strong, long-lasting protection."]],
l:"Measles is a highly contagious disease caused by a virus that spreads through the air when an infected person coughs, sneezes or breathes. It causes high fever, cough, runny nose, red eyes and a red, blotchy rash that spreads from the face to the rest of the body. Most people recover, but measles can lead to pneumonia, brain swelling, blindness and death, especially in young children and malnourished people. A safe and effective vaccine has been available since 1963, and vaccination has saved tens of millions of lives. Outbreaks still occur where too few people are vaccinated, including in the Philippines in 2019.",
s:[["How it spreads","The measles virus can float in the air and remain infectious for up to two hours after an infected person leaves a room. About nine out of ten unvaccinated people exposed to it will catch the disease. People are contagious from about four days before the rash appears until about four days after."],["Symptoms","Symptoms begin about 10 to 14 days after infection, with fever, cough, runny nose and red, watery eyes. Tiny white spots, called Koplik spots, may appear inside the mouth. A few days later a rash breaks out on the face and spreads down the body."],["Complications","Measles can cause ear infections, diarrhea, pneumonia and swelling of the brain. It also weakens the immune system for months or even years afterwards, making people more likely to catch other infections. Children under five, pregnant women and people with weak immune systems face the highest risk."],["The vaccine","The measles vaccine is usually given in two doses, often combined with vaccines against mumps and rubella, called MMR, or with rubella alone. Two doses protect about 97 percent of people. In the Philippines, measles vaccines are given free as part of the routine immunization program for infants."]],
k:[["Cause","Measles virus"],["Filipino name","Tigdas"],["Spread","Through the air"],["Vaccine available since","1963"],["Protection from two doses","About 97 percent"]],
d:[["c. 900","Persian physician al-Razi describes measles and smallpox"],["1757","Francis Home shows measles is caused by something in the blood of patients"],["1954","John Enders and Thomas Peebles isolate the measles virus"],["1963","The first measles vaccine is licensed"],["2019","The Philippines declares a measles outbreak"],["2019","Worldwide measles cases reach their highest level in more than 20 years"]],
s2:[["A disease of history","Measles has afflicted humans for well over a thousand years. The Persian physician al-Razi, around the year 900, was among the first to describe it clearly and tell it apart from smallpox. When Europeans brought measles to the Americas and the Pacific islands, it killed huge numbers of people who had never been exposed. In 1875 an epidemic in Fiji killed about a quarter of the population."],["Making the vaccine","In 1954 the American scientists John Enders and Thomas Peebles collected blood from a sick boy named David Edmonston and grew the measles virus in the laboratory. Weakened forms of this virus were used to create the first vaccines, licensed in 1963. Later improvements made the vaccine safer and more effective."],["The 2019 Philippine outbreak","In February 2019 the Philippine Department of Health declared a measles outbreak in Metro Manila and several regions. There were tens of thousands of cases and hundreds of deaths, mostly among young children who had not been vaccinated. Health workers held catch-up vaccination drives in schools, homes and evacuation centers."],["Vaccine confidence","Health experts linked falling vaccination rates in the Philippines partly to public fears after the controversy over the dengue vaccine Dengvaxia in 2017, which shook trust in vaccines generally. Globally, a 1998 study that claimed a link between the MMR vaccine and autism was found to be fraudulent and retracted in 2010; large studies have found no such link."],["Measles today","Thanks to vaccination, measles deaths worldwide have fallen sharply since 2000. However, gaps in vaccination, worsened by disruptions during the COVID-19 pandemic, have allowed outbreaks to return in many countries, including some that had previously eliminated the disease."],["Herd immunity","Because measles is so contagious, about 95 percent of a community must be vaccinated to stop it from spreading. This protects babies too young to be vaccinated and people who cannot receive vaccines for medical reasons. This shared protection is known as herd immunity or community immunity."]],
sp:["The disease that makes the immune system forget","In 2019 scientists published studies showing that measles does more than cause a rash and fever. The virus destroys many of the memory cells the immune system uses to recognize germs it has met before. After recovering, a child may lose part of their protection against diseases they had already fought off, a condition researchers called immune amnesia. This helps explain why, in the years after a measles epidemic, children often suffer more illnesses of other kinds. It is another reason doctors say preventing measles with vaccines protects children’s health far beyond the illness itself."],
g:[["Measles","A highly contagious viral disease with fever and rash"],["Koplik spots","Small white spots inside the mouth, an early sign of measles"],["MMR","A combined vaccine against measles, mumps and rubella"],["Herd immunity","Protection of a community when most people are immune"]],
f:["Measles spreads through the air.","Two vaccine doses protect about 97 percent of people.","In Filipino, measles is called tigdas."],
fun:"The measles virus can stay infectious in the air of a room for up to two hours after an infected person has left."});

