// Technology, more articles: elevators and escalators, high-speed rail, drones, the power grid and clocks.
ADDART({id:"elevators",t:"Elevators and Escalators",c:"Engineering",i:"🛗",w:"Elevator",r:["skyscrapers","machines","elec","bridges","rail"],
p:[["Going up","Elevators carry people and goods between the floors of buildings."],["Safety first","Elisha Otis’s safety brake made passenger elevators trusted in the 1850s."],["Moving stairs","Escalators are moving staircases that carry crowds smoothly."]],
l:"An elevator, called a lift in British English, is a platform or cabin that moves up and down inside a shaft to carry people or goods between floors. Simple hoists pulled by people, animals or water power have existed since ancient times, but modern passenger elevators became possible in the 1850s, when the American inventor Elisha Otis demonstrated a safety brake that stopped a cabin from falling if its rope broke. Together with steel frames, safe elevators made tall buildings and skyscrapers practical. Escalators, or moving staircases, followed in the late 1800s and now carry millions of people every day in malls, airports and train stations.",
s:[["How an elevator works","Most elevators in tall buildings hang from strong steel cables that pass over a wheel at the top of the shaft, driven by an electric motor. A heavy counterweight on the other side of the wheel balances the cabin, so the motor needs much less power. Shorter buildings often use hydraulic elevators, which are pushed up by a piston filled with oil."],["Safety systems","Modern elevators have several cables, each strong enough to hold the cabin alone. If the cabin moves too fast, a governor triggers safety brakes that grip the guide rails. Doors cannot open unless the cabin is level with a floor, and buffers at the bottom of the shaft cushion any fall. Elevators are among the safest forms of transport."],["Escalators","An escalator is a loop of linked steps that moves continuously, driven by a motor and chains, with a moving handrail kept at the same speed. The steps flatten at the top and bottom so riders can step on and off. Moving walkways work in a similar way on flat ground or gentle slopes, as in airports."],["Making cities taller","Before elevators, buildings rarely rose above five or six stories, because few people wanted to climb more stairs. Safe elevators made upper floors desirable and valuable, helping create the skylines of New York, Chicago, Hong Kong, Dubai and the business districts of Makati and Bonifacio Global City."]],
k:[["Safety brake demonstrated","Elisha Otis, 1854"],["First passenger safety elevator","New York, 1857"],["First electric elevator","Werner von Siemens, 1880"],["Escalator name","Trademarked by Otis, 1900"],["British name","Lift"]],
d:[["c. 236 BC","Archimedes is said to build a lifting device using ropes and pulleys"],["1743","A counterweighted personal lift is built for King Louis XV in Versailles"],["1854","Elisha Otis demonstrates his safety brake in New York"],["1857","The first passenger safety elevator is installed in New York"],["1880","Werner von Siemens builds the first electric elevator"],["1896","Jesse Reno’s inclined moving stairway carries riders at Coney Island"]],
s2:[["Otis’s famous show","At the 1854 world’s fair in New York, Elisha Otis stood on an open elevator platform raised high above a crowd. He then ordered the only rope holding it to be cut. The platform dropped only a few centimeters before his spring-loaded brake caught it. He is said to have called out, all safe, gentlemen. Orders for his elevators soon followed."],["Electric elevators","The German engineer Werner von Siemens built the first electric elevator in 1880. Electric motors were faster, quieter and cleaner than steam-powered machines. By the early 1900s electric elevators served most tall buildings, and push-button controls gradually replaced human elevator operators."],["The fastest elevators","Elevators in some of the world’s tallest buildings travel at more than 70 kilometers per hour. The Burj Khalifa in Dubai, the tallest building in the world, has elevators that climb more than 500 meters. Very tall buildings use sky lobbies, where passengers change from express elevators to local ones."],["Accessibility","Elevators, ramps and lifts make buildings and transport usable for wheelchair users, older people, parents with strollers and people carrying heavy loads. Many countries, including the Philippines through its Accessibility Law of 1982, require public buildings to provide accessible features."],["Escalator history","Several inventors designed moving stairs in the 1800s. In 1896 Jesse Reno installed an inclined moving belt at Coney Island in New York, while Charles Seeberger developed designs that the Otis company bought and sold under the name escalator, trademarked in 1900. The word later became generic."],["Elevators of the future","Engineers are developing lighter cables made of carbon fiber that could allow single elevator rides of a kilometer or more, and cabins that move sideways as well as up and down using magnetic systems. The idea of a space elevator, a cable stretching from Earth into orbit, first imagined by Konstantin Tsiolkovsky in 1895, remains beyond current materials."]],
sp:["Elevator etiquette","Riding an elevator comes with unwritten rules that many people follow around the world. Let passengers step out before stepping in, move to the back to make space, and hold the door for those hurrying to catch it if it is safe. In many Philippine malls and office towers, people stand quietly facing the doors, and in busy buildings the button for closing the door is pressed often during rush hour. On escalators in some cities, such as Tokyo or London, riders stand on one side to let others walk past on the other, though some transport authorities now ask everyone to stand still for safety."],
g:[["Elevator","A cabin that moves up and down between floors"],["Counterweight","A weight that balances the elevator cabin"],["Escalator","A moving staircase"],["Hydraulic","Powered by liquid pushed under pressure"]],
f:["Elisha Otis invented the elevator safety brake.","The first electric elevator was built in 1880.","Escalator was once a trademark."],
fun:"Escalator was originally a brand name owned by the Otis Elevator Company before it became an ordinary word."});

ADDART({id:"highspeedrail",t:"High-Speed Rail",c:"Transport",i:"🚄",w:"High-speed rail",r:["rail","japan","china","energytransition","megacities"],
p:[["Fast trains","High-speed trains travel at 250 kilometers per hour or more on special tracks."],["Started in Japan","Japan opened the first high-speed line, the Shinkansen, in 1964."],["Growing networks","China now has by far the largest high-speed network."]],
l:"High-speed rail is a type of passenger train service that runs much faster than ordinary trains, usually at 250 kilometers per hour or more on new lines, using specially built tracks, powerful electric trains and advanced signaling. The first high-speed line, Japan’s Tokaido Shinkansen between Tokyo and Osaka, opened in 1964. High-speed networks have since spread across Europe and Asia, and China has built the largest network in the world. Supporters say high-speed rail is fast, safe and produces less pollution than flying or driving, while critics point to its very high construction costs.",
s:[["How the trains go so fast","High-speed trains are streamlined to cut air resistance and draw electricity from overhead wires to power motors spread along the train. The tracks have gentle curves, no level crossings with roads, and continuous welded rails for a smooth ride. Signals are displayed inside the cab, because drivers cannot read trackside signals at such speeds."],["The Shinkansen","Japan’s Shinkansen, often called the bullet train, opened on 1 October 1964, just before the Tokyo Olympics, with trains running at up to 210 kilometers per hour. Today Shinkansen lines connect most of Japan’s major cities, with trains reaching 320 kilometers per hour. The network is famous for its punctuality and safety record."],["Europe’s fast trains","France opened its first TGV line between Paris and Lyon in 1981, followed by high-speed lines in Germany, Italy, Spain and other countries. The Eurostar, through the Channel Tunnel, links London with Paris and Brussels. Spain has one of the longest high-speed networks in Europe."],["China’s network","China opened its first major high-speed line in 2008 and has since built a network of tens of thousands of kilometers, more than the rest of the world combined. Trains on some lines run at 350 kilometers per hour, carrying billions of passenger trips a year and linking cities from Beijing to Guangzhou."]],
k:[["Usual top speed","250 to 350 km/h"],["First line","Tokaido Shinkansen, Japan, 1964"],["Largest network","China"],["Wheeled speed record","574.8 km/h, France, 2007"],["Maglev speed record","603 km/h, Japan, 2015"]],
d:[["1964","Japan opens the Tokaido Shinkansen between Tokyo and Osaka"],["1981","France opens its first TGV line"],["1994","Eurostar services begin through the Channel Tunnel"],["2004","The Shanghai Maglev begins commercial service"],["2007","A French TGV sets the wheeled train speed record of 574.8 km/h"],["2015","A Japanese maglev train reaches 603 km/h"]],
s2:[["Maglev trains","Maglev, short for magnetic levitation, trains float above a guideway using powerful magnets, so there is no friction between wheels and rails. The Shanghai Maglev, opened in 2004, links the city to its airport at speeds of up to about 300 to 430 kilometers per hour. Japan is building a maglev line between Tokyo and Nagoya designed for speeds of 500 kilometers per hour."],["Safety","High-speed rail has an excellent safety record. Japan’s Shinkansen has carried billions of passengers since 1964 without a passenger death caused by a derailment or collision, thanks to dedicated tracks, automatic train control and earthquake detection systems that stop trains when tremors are sensed."],["Benefits and costs","Supporters say high-speed rail cuts travel times between cities, reduces road congestion and produces far less carbon dioxide per passenger than flying. Critics note that building lines costs billions, some lines carry fewer passengers than predicted, and money might be better spent on local transport. Debates over cost have slowed projects in countries such as the United States and the United Kingdom."],["Trains versus planes","For journeys of a few hundred kilometers, high-speed trains often compete well with air travel, because stations are in city centers and boarding is quick. After high-speed lines opened between Madrid and Barcelona and between Paris and Lyon, many travelers switched from planes to trains."],["Rail in the Philippines","The Philippines does not yet have high-speed rail. The North–South Commuter Railway under construction in Luzon is designed for faster electric trains linking Clark, Metro Manila and Calamba, though at speeds below true high-speed rail. A Mindanao railway has also been planned. Supporters see modern rail as key to easing traffic, while critics point to delays and costs."],["Rail around the world","High-speed lines also operate or are being built in South Korea, Taiwan, Turkey, Morocco, Saudi Arabia, Indonesia and India. Indonesia opened Southeast Asia’s first high-speed railway, between Jakarta and Bandung, in 2023, built with Chinese partners."]],
sp:["Seven minutes to clean a bullet train","At Tokyo Station, Shinkansen trains often have only a short turnaround before departing again. In about seven minutes, teams of cleaners sweep through every carriage, collecting rubbish, wiping tables, turning all the seats to face the new direction of travel and checking the toilets. When they finish, the team lines up on the platform and bows to the waiting passengers. Visitors film the routine, sometimes called the seven-minute miracle, as an example of Japanese efficiency and pride in work, and transport companies from other countries have studied it."],
g:[["High-speed rail","Passenger trains running at 250 km/h or more"],["Shinkansen","Japan’s high-speed rail network"],["Maglev","A train that floats above its track using magnets"],["Streamlined","Shaped to move smoothly through air"]],
f:["Japan’s Shinkansen opened in 1964.","China has the largest high-speed network.","A Japanese maglev reached 603 km/h."],
fun:"The long nose of some Shinkansen trains was designed partly to reduce a loud boom when they rush out of tunnels; one design was inspired by the beak of the kingfisher."});

ADDART({id:"drones",t:"Drones",c:"Technology",i:"🛸",w:"Unmanned aerial vehicle",r:["flight","robot","gps","camera","aieveryday"],
p:[["Flying without a pilot","Drones are aircraft that fly without a pilot on board."],["Many uses","They take photos, map land, spray crops, deliver supplies and help in disasters."],["Rules and worries","Their use raises questions about safety, privacy and warfare."]],
l:"A drone, or unmanned aerial vehicle, is an aircraft that flies without a human pilot on board. It is controlled by a person on the ground using a remote control, or flies itself using computers, sensors and GPS. Drones range from tiny toys to large military aircraft with wingspans like those of airliners. Small camera drones with four rotors, called quadcopters, became popular in the 2010s for photography, filmmaking and hobby flying. Drones are now used in farming, mapping, construction, search and rescue, deliveries and disaster response. Their use in warfare and their effects on privacy and safety are widely debated.",
s:[["How a quadcopter flies","A quadcopter has four rotors that spin to create lift. By changing the speed of individual rotors, a flight computer tilts the drone to move forward, backward or sideways, or spins it around. Sensors such as gyroscopes, accelerometers and GPS keep it stable, and many drones can hover in place or return home automatically."],["Cameras in the sky","Camera drones let photographers and filmmakers capture aerial views that once required helicopters. They are used to film sports, weddings, tourism videos, news events and nature documentaries. Some carry thermal cameras that detect heat, useful for finding people or inspecting buildings."],["Drones at work","Farmers use drones to check the health of crops and spray fertilizer or pesticide. Engineers inspect bridges, power lines and wind turbines without climbing them. Surveyors make detailed maps and 3D models of land, and scientists use drones to count wildlife, study volcanoes and track forests."],["Drones in disasters","After typhoons, earthquakes and floods, drones can quickly survey damaged areas, find stranded people and help plan relief. In the Philippines, drones have been used to map damage after typhoons, monitor volcanoes such as Taal and Mayon and deliver supplies to areas cut off by landslides."]],
k:[["Also called","Unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV)"],["Common design","Quadcopter"],["Name origin","Queen Bee target aircraft, 1935"],["Philippine regulator","Civil Aviation Authority of the Philippines"],["Controlled by","Remote pilot or onboard computer"]],
d:[["1918","The Kettering Bug, an early pilotless flying bomb, is tested in the United States"],["1935","Britain flies the Queen Bee, a radio-controlled target plane"],["1995","The Predator drone first flies on military missions"],["2006","The drone company DJI is founded in China"],["2016","Drones begin delivering blood to hospitals in Rwanda"],["2022","Drones are used on a large scale in the war in Ukraine"]],
s2:[["Where the name came from","In the 1930s Britain built a radio-controlled target aircraft called the Queen Bee, used for gunnery practice. When the United States developed similar planes, they were nicknamed drones, after male bees. The name stuck and was later applied to all kinds of pilotless aircraft."],["Delivering medicine","In 2016 the company Zipline began using small fixed-wing drones to deliver blood supplies to hospitals in Rwanda, dropping packages by parachute. Similar services now deliver medicines, vaccines and lab samples in Ghana, Nigeria and other countries. Some companies also test drone delivery of food and parcels in cities."],["Drones in war","Militaries use drones for surveillance and attacks, and armed drones have played a major role in conflicts in the Middle East, Africa and Ukraine. Supporters say drones keep pilots out of danger and can strike precisely, while critics raise concerns about civilian casualties, accountability and the possibility of weapons that choose targets without human control. International discussions on rules for autonomous weapons continue."],["Safety and rules","Drones can be dangerous near airports, crowds and emergency operations. Many countries require drone owners to register their aircraft, keep them within sight, avoid flying near airports and stay below certain heights. In the Philippines, the Civil Aviation Authority of the Philippines sets rules and issues certificates for drone operators."],["Privacy","Because drones can carry cameras over homes and private property, they raise privacy concerns. Laws in many places limit filming people without permission, and some groups call for stronger rules. Drone hobbyists argue that responsible pilots should not be over-regulated."],["Light shows","Hundreds or thousands of drones carrying colored lights can be programmed to fly together, forming pictures, words and moving shapes in the night sky. Drone light shows have appeared at Olympic Games, New Year celebrations and festivals, including events in the Philippines, sometimes replacing fireworks because they are quieter and create no smoke."]],
sp:["Mapping after the storm","When a strong typhoon strikes, roads may be blocked, bridges washed away and phone lines down, so it is hard to know where help is needed most. Teams of volunteers and government workers now launch drones to fly over towns and photograph them from above. The pictures are stitched together into detailed maps showing destroyed houses, flooded fields and blocked roads. Relief agencies use these maps to decide where to send food, water and rescue teams. Some Filipino universities and local governments train students and staff to fly drones for disaster mapping."],
g:[["Drone","An aircraft that flies without a pilot on board"],["UAV","Unmanned aerial vehicle"],["Quadcopter","A drone with four rotors"],["Thermal camera","A camera that detects heat"]],
f:["Drones were named after male bees.","Quadcopters have four rotors.","Drones deliver medicine in parts of Africa."],
fun:"Drone light shows can use thousands of drones at once, each flying to its exact position in the sky."});

ADDART({id:"powergrid",t:"The Power Grid",c:"Energy",i:"🔌",w:"Electrical grid",r:["elec","phpower","renew","battery","nuclear"],
p:[["Electricity on the move","The power grid carries electricity from power plants to homes, schools and businesses."],["High voltage","Electricity travels long distances at very high voltage, then is stepped down for use."],["Keeping balance","Grid operators must match supply and demand every second."]],
l:"A power grid, or electrical grid, is the network that delivers electricity from where it is generated to where it is used. It includes power plants, high-voltage transmission lines, substations, transformers and the local distribution lines that bring power to homes and buildings. Because electricity is hard to store in large amounts, grid operators must balance the amount produced with the amount used at every moment. If the balance fails, blackouts can occur. Grids are changing quickly as more electricity comes from solar and wind power, which vary with the weather, and as batteries and smart technology help manage supply and demand.",
s:[["From plant to plug","Electricity is produced in power plants that burn coal, gas or oil, split atoms, or use water, wind, sunlight or underground heat. Step-up transformers raise its voltage to hundreds of thousands of volts for long-distance travel on transmission towers. At substations near towns, transformers lower the voltage, and distribution lines carry it along streets to homes, where it arrives at about 220 to 240 volts in the Philippines."],["Why high voltage","Sending electricity at high voltage means less current flows for the same amount of power, so less energy is wasted as heat in the wires. This is why transmission lines are kept high above the ground on tall towers and why substations are fenced off for safety."],["Balancing supply and demand","Demand for electricity rises and falls through the day, peaking on hot afternoons and evenings when air conditioners, lights and appliances are switched on. Control centers watch the grid constantly and tell power plants to increase or decrease output. The grid’s frequency, 60 hertz in the Philippines, must stay steady; if it drops, it signals that demand is outrunning supply."],["Blackouts","Blackouts happen when power plants fail, lines are damaged by storms or demand exceeds supply. To avoid a total collapse, operators sometimes cut power to some areas in turns, called rotational brownouts in the Philippines. Typhoons often topple power poles and lines, leaving communities without electricity for days."]],
k:[["Philippine grid frequency","60 hertz"],["Household voltage in the Philippines","About 220–230 volts"],["Philippine transmission operator","National Grid Corporation of the Philippines"],["Main grids","Luzon, Visayas, Mindanao"],["First central power station","Pearl Street, New York, 1882"]],
d:[["1882","Thomas Edison opens the Pearl Street power station in New York"],["1886","William Stanley demonstrates an AC power system with transformers"],["1895","The Niagara Falls power plant begins generating alternating current"],["1903","The Manila Electric Railway and Light Company, later Meralco, is founded"],["2003","A blackout leaves about 50 million people without power in North America"],["2025","A major blackout strikes Spain and Portugal"]],
s2:[["The war of the currents","In the 1880s Thomas Edison promoted direct current, or DC, which flows in one direction. George Westinghouse and Nikola Tesla promoted alternating current, or AC, which reverses direction many times a second and can easily be changed to higher or lower voltages with transformers. AC proved better for sending power over long distances and became the standard for grids worldwide."],["The Philippine grid","The Philippines has three main grids, serving Luzon, the Visayas and Mindanao, connected by undersea cables. The Mindanao–Visayas Interconnection Project, completed in the 2020s, linked Mindanao to the rest of the country for the first time. The National Grid Corporation of the Philippines operates the transmission network, while distribution companies such as Meralco and electric cooperatives deliver power to customers."],["Islands and microgrids","Many small islands in the Philippines are not connected to the main grids. They often rely on diesel generators, which are expensive and polluting. Solar panels, batteries and small local grids, called microgrids, are increasingly used to bring cheaper, cleaner and more reliable electricity to remote communities."],["Renewables on the grid","Solar and wind power produce electricity only when the Sun shines or the wind blows. Grid operators manage this by combining many sources across wide areas, using batteries and pumped hydropower to store energy, and building more transmission lines. Supporters say these tools allow very high shares of renewable power, while others warn that grids must be carefully planned to stay reliable."],["Smart grids","Smart grids use digital meters, sensors and communication networks to monitor electricity flows in real time. They can detect faults, reroute power, encourage people to use electricity when it is cheaper and connect electric vehicles and home solar panels. Smart meters also allow customers to track their own use."],["Grid security","Because modern life depends on electricity, power grids are considered critical infrastructure. Operators protect them against extreme weather, equipment failures and cyberattacks. In 2015 and 2016 hackers caused blackouts in parts of Ukraine, showing that grids can be targeted through computer networks."]],
sp:["The day Spain went dark","Shortly after midday on 28 April 2025, the power grid of Spain and Portugal suddenly collapsed. Within seconds, trains stopped in tunnels, traffic lights went dark, elevators halted between floors and mobile networks struggled. Millions of people walked home, gathered around battery radios and shared food in the streets. Engineers gradually restarted the grid, section by section, and most power returned by the next morning. The blackout led to investigations into its causes and wide debate across Europe about how to keep grids stable as they rely more on renewable energy."],
g:[["Power grid","The network that delivers electricity"],["Transmission line","A high-voltage line carrying power over long distances"],["Transformer","A device that raises or lowers voltage"],["Frequency","How many times per second alternating current changes direction"]],
f:["The Philippine grid runs at 60 hertz.","Electricity travels at high voltage over long distances.","Edison opened the first central power station in 1882."],
fun:"Electricity flowing through the grid must be produced at almost the exact moment it is used, because very little is stored along the way."});

ADDART({id:"clocks",t:"Clocks and Timekeeping",c:"Inventions",i:"🕰️",w:"Clock",r:["timephys","sun","machines","gps","compass"],
p:[["Measuring time","Clocks measure and show the passing of time."],["From shadows to atoms","People have used sundials, water clocks, pendulums, quartz crystals and atoms to keep time."],["Precise time","Modern atomic clocks are accurate to a tiny fraction of a second over millions of years."]],
l:"A clock is a device for measuring and showing time. People first told time by watching the Sun, Moon and stars, then built sundials and water clocks thousands of years ago. Mechanical clocks with gears appeared in medieval China and Europe, and the pendulum clock, invented in 1656, made timekeeping far more accurate. Quartz clocks, using vibrating crystals, became common in the 20th century, and atomic clocks, which count the vibrations of atoms, now define the second itself. Precise time is essential for navigation, satellites, the internet, power grids and banking, as well as for daily life.",
s:[["Sundials and water clocks","A sundial tells time by the shadow cast by a pointer as the Sun moves across the sky. The ancient Egyptians and Babylonians used shadow clocks over 3,000 years ago. Water clocks, which measured time by the steady flow of water into or out of a container, worked at night and on cloudy days and were used in Egypt, Greece, China and the Islamic world."],["Mechanical clocks","Mechanical clocks use a falling weight or a coiled spring to turn gears, controlled by a device called an escapement that releases the gears in small, regular steps. The first mechanical clocks in Europe appeared in church towers in the late 1200s and 1300s. They rang bells to mark the hours, which is where the word clock, from a word for bell, comes from."],["The pendulum","The Italian scientist Galileo Galilei noticed that a swinging pendulum keeps a steady rhythm. In 1656 the Dutch scientist Christiaan Huygens built the first pendulum clock, which was accurate to within about a minute a day, a huge improvement. Pendulum clocks remained the most accurate timekeepers for almost 300 years."],["Quartz and atomic clocks","A quartz crystal vibrates at a precise rate when electricity passes through it. The first quartz clock was built in 1927, and cheap quartz watches spread worldwide from the 1970s. Atomic clocks, first built in 1955, measure the natural vibrations of caesium or other atoms and are so accurate that they would lose less than a second in millions of years."]],
k:[["Pendulum clock invented","Christiaan Huygens, 1656"],["First quartz clock","1927"],["First caesium atomic clock","1955"],["The second defined by atoms","1967"],["Philippine time keeper","PAGASA"]],
d:[["c. 1500 BC","Egyptians use shadow clocks and water clocks"],["1092","Su Song completes a giant astronomical clock tower in China"],["1656","Christiaan Huygens builds the first pendulum clock"],["1761","John Harrison’s H4 marine timekeeper is tested at sea"],["1955","The first caesium atomic clock is built in England"],["2013","The Philippines passes the Philippine Standard Time Act"]],
s2:[["Su Song’s clock tower","In 1092 the Chinese scholar and engineer Su Song completed a huge water-driven astronomical clock tower in the capital, Kaifeng. More than ten meters tall, it turned a rotating globe of the stars and had figures that appeared to announce the hours. It used an early form of escapement, centuries before similar devices in Europe."],["Finding longitude","Sailors could find their latitude from the Sun and stars, but not their longitude, their east–west position, without knowing the exact time at home. The British government offered a prize for a solution. The carpenter and clockmaker John Harrison spent decades building sea clocks, and his H4 watch, tested in 1761, kept time accurately enough on a voyage to Jamaica to solve the problem."],["Time zones","Before railways, towns set their clocks by the local Sun, so noon differed from place to place. Railway timetables made this confusing, and in 1884 an international conference chose the line through Greenwich in England as the starting point for world time zones. The Philippines uses Philippine Standard Time, eight hours ahead of Greenwich."],["Juan Time","The Philippine Standard Time Act of 2013 requires government offices and broadcasters to synchronize their clocks with the official time kept by PAGASA. The campaign behind the law encouraged punctuality and challenged the habit, jokingly called Filipino time, of arriving late."],["Atomic time and GPS","GPS satellites carry atomic clocks and broadcast precise time signals. A receiver works out its position by comparing how long signals take to arrive from several satellites; an error of a millionth of a second would put the location off by about 300 meters. Atomic time also keeps mobile networks, the internet, stock markets and power grids in step."],["The leap second","Earth’s rotation is slightly irregular and gradually slowing, so atomic time and solar time drift apart. Since 1972 leap seconds have occasionally been added to keep them aligned, but they cause problems for computer systems. In 2022 international scientists agreed to stop adding leap seconds by 2035."]],
sp:["The clocks of old Manila","In Spanish colonial Manila, church bells and tower clocks marked the hours for city life, calling people to prayer, work and rest. Many old churches in the Philippines, such as those in Intramuros and in provincial towns, still have bell towers that once served as the community’s clock. In the 1930s the Manila City Hall was built with its famous clock tower, which became a landmark. Its four large faces still look out over the city, and its lights are sometimes changed to different colors to mark special occasions."],
g:[["Sundial","A device that tells time by a shadow cast by the Sun"],["Escapement","A mechanism that releases a clock’s gears in regular steps"],["Pendulum","A weight that swings back and forth at a steady rate"],["Atomic clock","A clock that keeps time using the vibrations of atoms"]],
f:["The pendulum clock was invented in 1656.","Atomic clocks define the second.","The word clock comes from a word for bell."],
fun:"Clocks run slightly faster on a mountaintop than at sea level because of gravity, an effect modern atomic clocks can measure."});

