// Science and Numbers, more articles: the periodic table, catalysts, neutrinos, the Higgs boson and the theory of evolution.
ADDART({id:"periodictable",t:"The Periodic Table",c:"Chemistry",i:"🧪",w:"Periodic table",r:["atom","metals","molecules","reactions","electron"],
p:[["Chart of the elements","The periodic table arranges all known chemical elements in order of atomic number."],["Rows and columns","Elements in the same column behave in similar ways."],["Mendeleev’s idea","Dmitri Mendeleev published the first widely used table in 1869."]],
l:"The periodic table is a chart that arranges the chemical elements, the basic substances from which all matter is made, in order of their atomic number, the number of protons in each atom’s nucleus. Elements are placed in rows, called periods, and columns, called groups, so that elements with similar chemical properties fall in the same column. The Russian chemist Dmitri Mendeleev published the first widely accepted version in 1869 and boldly left gaps for elements not yet discovered, correctly predicting their properties. Today the table contains 118 confirmed elements, from hydrogen, the lightest, to oganesson, the heaviest. It is one of the most important tools in science.",
s:[["Periods and groups","Each row, or period, begins a new layer of electrons around the nucleus. Each column, or group, contains elements with the same number of electrons in their outer layer, which largely decides how they react. For example, the alkali metals in group 1, such as sodium and potassium, are all soft and react strongly with water."],["Metals and nonmetals","Most elements are metals, found on the left and in the middle of the table. They are usually shiny, conduct electricity and can be bent or shaped. Nonmetals, such as carbon, oxygen and nitrogen, sit on the upper right. Between them lie metalloids, such as silicon, which have properties of both and are vital in electronics."],["Noble gases","The column on the far right holds the noble gases, including helium, neon and argon. Their outer electron layers are full, so they hardly react with other elements. Neon glows red-orange in signs, and helium, lighter than air, fills balloons."],["Reading a square","Each square usually shows an element’s symbol, such as H for hydrogen, Fe for iron from the Latin ferrum, or Au for gold from the Latin aurum. It also shows the atomic number and the atomic mass, the average weight of its atoms."]],
k:[["Confirmed elements","118"],["Arranged by","Atomic number"],["First widely used table","Dmitri Mendeleev, 1869"],["Lightest element","Hydrogen"],["Heaviest confirmed element","Oganesson"]],
d:[["1669","Hennig Brand discovers phosphorus, the first element found by a known person"],["1789","Antoine Lavoisier publishes a list of chemical elements"],["1869","Dmitri Mendeleev presents his periodic table"],["1875","Gallium, predicted by Mendeleev, is discovered"],["1913","Henry Moseley shows elements should be ordered by atomic number"],["2016","The names of the last four elements to complete the seventh row are approved"]],
s2:[["Mendeleev’s gaps","Mendeleev arranged the elements known in his time by atomic weight and grouped them by their properties. Where the pattern broke, he left gaps and predicted that undiscovered elements would fill them. He described their likely weights, densities and behavior. When gallium, scandium and germanium were found between 1875 and 1886, they matched his predictions remarkably well."],["Moseley and atomic number","In 1913 the young British physicist Henry Moseley used X-rays to show that each element has a unique number of positive charges in its nucleus, its atomic number. Ordering elements by this number fixed a few puzzling spots in Mendeleev’s table. Moseley was killed in the First World War in 1915, at the age of 27."],["Making new elements","Elements heavier than uranium, number 92, are very rare or absent in nature and have mostly been created in laboratories by smashing atoms together. Many exist for only fractions of a second. The four newest elements, nihonium, moscovium, tennessine and oganesson, were named in 2016, completing the seventh row of the table."],["Named after places and people","Element names come from many sources. Some honor scientists, such as einsteinium, curium and mendelevium. Others are named after places, such as americium, francium, polonium after Poland, and nihonium, from Nihon, a Japanese name for Japan. Some come from mythology, such as titanium, from the Titans of Greek myth."],["Elements in everyday life","Elements surround us. Carbon forms the backbone of living things, oxygen keeps us alive, silicon is used in computer chips, and lithium powers rechargeable batteries. The Philippines is a major producer of nickel, an element used in stainless steel and electric car batteries, and also mines copper and gold."],["A table that keeps growing","Scientists are trying to create element 119 and beyond, which would begin an eighth row. Some predict an island of stability, where certain very heavy elements might last longer than their neighbors. In 2019 the world celebrated the International Year of the Periodic Table, 150 years after Mendeleev’s work."]],
sp:["A dream of a table","A popular story says that Dmitri Mendeleev, after struggling for days to find a pattern in the elements, fell asleep at his desk and saw in a dream a table where all the elements fell into place. He woke and wrote it down. Historians doubt the dream story, noting that he had worked for years on the problem, writing the elements and their properties on cards and arranging them like a game of patience. Whatever the truth, the table he published in 1869 changed chemistry. Element 101, mendelevium, was named in his honor in 1955."],
g:[["Element","A pure substance made of only one kind of atom"],["Atomic number","The number of protons in an atom’s nucleus"],["Group","A column of the periodic table"],["Period","A row of the periodic table"]],
f:["The periodic table has 118 confirmed elements.","Mendeleev predicted elements before they were found.","Au, the symbol for gold, comes from Latin."],
fun:"Only two elements are liquid at room temperature: mercury and bromine."});

ADDART({id:"catalysts",t:"Catalysts",c:"Chemistry",i:"⚗️",w:"Catalysis",r:["reactions","digest","cars","molecules","foodsecurity"],
p:[["Speeding up reactions","A catalyst is a substance that speeds up a chemical reaction without being used up."],["Catalysts of life","Living things use catalysts called enzymes."],["In industry","Catalysts help make fertilizer, fuels, plastics and medicines."]],
l:"A catalyst is a substance that makes a chemical reaction happen faster, or at a lower temperature, without itself being permanently changed or used up. It works by providing an easier pathway for the reaction, lowering the amount of energy needed to get it started. Catalysts are essential to life: the enzymes in our bodies are biological catalysts that digest food, copy DNA and release energy. They are also vital to modern industry, where they help make fertilizers, fuels, plastics, medicines and countless other products, and in cars, where catalytic converters clean exhaust gases. Most of the world’s chemical products are made using catalysts at some stage.",
s:[["How catalysts work","For a reaction to happen, molecules must collide with enough energy to break old bonds and form new ones. This energy barrier is called the activation energy. A catalyst offers a different route with a lower barrier, so more collisions succeed and the reaction speeds up. At the end of the reaction, the catalyst is left unchanged and can be used again."],["Enzymes","Enzymes are proteins that act as catalysts in living cells. Each enzyme fits only certain molecules, like a lock and key. Amylase in saliva begins breaking down starch in rice and bread, while enzymes in the stomach and intestines digest proteins and fats. Without enzymes, many reactions in the body would be far too slow to sustain life."],["Types of catalysts","Some catalysts are mixed in with the reacting substances, often in a liquid. Others are solids, such as metals or minerals, on whose surfaces gases or liquids react. Metals such as platinum, palladium, nickel and iron are common industrial catalysts."],["Catalytic converters","Since the 1970s many cars have been fitted with catalytic converters in their exhaust systems. Inside, a honeycomb coated with platinum, palladium and rhodium changes poisonous carbon monoxide, unburned fuel and nitrogen oxides into less harmful carbon dioxide, water and nitrogen."]],
k:[["Definition","Speeds up a reaction without being used up"],["Term catalysis coined","Jöns Jacob Berzelius, 1835"],["Biological catalysts","Enzymes"],["Common metal catalysts","Platinum, palladium, nickel, iron"],["Car exhaust cleaner","Catalytic converter"]],
d:[["1835","Jöns Jacob Berzelius coins the word catalysis"],["1909","Wilhelm Ostwald wins the Nobel Prize for work on catalysis"],["1913","The Haber–Bosch process begins making ammonia on an industrial scale"],["1926","James Sumner shows that an enzyme is a protein"],["1975","Catalytic converters are widely fitted to new cars in the United States"],["2021","The Nobel Prize in Chemistry honors new tools for making molecules with organic catalysts"]],
s2:[["Feeding the world","The Haber–Bosch process, developed by Fritz Haber and Carl Bosch in the early 1900s, uses an iron-based catalyst to combine nitrogen from the air with hydrogen to make ammonia, the main ingredient of most fertilizers. Fertilizer made this way helps grow food for a large share of the world’s population. However, the process uses large amounts of energy, and overuse of fertilizer can pollute rivers and seas."],["Fuels and plastics","Oil refineries use catalysts made from minerals called zeolites to break large hydrocarbon molecules into smaller ones for gasoline, diesel and jet fuel. Other catalysts link small molecules into long chains to make plastics such as polyethylene, used in bags and bottles."],["Catalysts in the kitchen and home","Enzymes are added to laundry detergents to break down stains from food, blood and grass. Yeast uses its own enzymes to ferment sugar, making bread rise and producing alcohol in drinks. Meat tenderizers contain enzymes from papaya or pineapple that break down tough proteins."],["Green chemistry","Chemists design new catalysts to make reactions cleaner, using less energy and producing less waste. In 2021 Benjamin List and David MacMillan received the Nobel Prize in Chemistry for developing small organic molecules as catalysts, an approach that avoids some toxic metals and helps make medicines more efficiently."],["Catalysts and clean energy","Catalysts are central to many clean energy technologies. Fuel cells use platinum catalysts to combine hydrogen and oxygen into electricity and water, and electrolyzers use catalysts to split water into hydrogen. Researchers are searching for cheaper catalysts, because metals such as platinum are rare and expensive."],["Poisoning a catalyst","A catalyst can stop working if other substances stick to its surface and block it, a process called poisoning. For example, lead in old types of gasoline ruined catalytic converters, which is one reason leaded gasoline was phased out. The Philippines banned leaded gasoline in 2000."]],
sp:["The papaya that softens meat","For generations, cooks in the Philippines and other tropical countries have used unripe papaya to soften tough meat. In dishes such as tinola, green papaya is cooked with chicken, and some cooks wrap meat in papaya leaves or rub it with papaya juice before cooking. The secret is an enzyme called papain, found in the fruit’s milky sap. Papain breaks the long protein chains in meat into smaller pieces, making it more tender. Today papain is extracted and sold as commercial meat tenderizer, and is also used in medicines and cosmetics. It is a delicious example of a natural catalyst at work."],
g:[["Catalyst","A substance that speeds up a reaction without being used up"],["Enzyme","A protein that acts as a catalyst in living things"],["Activation energy","The energy needed to start a reaction"],["Catalytic converter","A device that cleans car exhaust gases"]],
f:["Enzymes are biological catalysts.","Catalysts are not used up in reactions.","Papaya contains a meat-softening enzyme."],
fun:"A single enzyme molecule can speed up millions of reactions every second."});

ADDART({id:"neutrinos",t:"Neutrinos",c:"Physics",i:"👻",w:"Neutrino",r:["particles","standardmodel","sun","supernova","radioact"],
p:[["Ghost particles","Neutrinos are tiny particles that pass through almost everything."],["Everywhere","Trillions of neutrinos from the Sun pass through your body every second."],["Hard to catch","Scientists need huge underground detectors to see them."]],
l:"A neutrino is a tiny, electrically neutral particle with an extremely small mass. Neutrinos are among the most abundant particles in the universe, produced in huge numbers by nuclear reactions in the Sun and other stars, by exploding stars, by radioactive decay and by nuclear reactors. Because they barely interact with other matter, they can pass straight through people, buildings and even the whole Earth without stopping, earning them the nickname ghost particles. Neutrinos were proposed by Wolfgang Pauli in 1930 and first detected in 1956. The discovery that they change from one type to another showed that they have mass, a finding that earned the 2015 Nobel Prize in Physics.",
s:[["What a neutrino is","Neutrinos belong to a family of particles called leptons, which also includes the electron. They carry no electric charge and are not affected by the strong force that holds atomic nuclei together. They interact only through gravity and the weak nuclear force, which is why they rarely collide with anything."],["Three flavors","There are three types, or flavors, of neutrino: the electron neutrino, the muon neutrino and the tau neutrino. Each is linked to a different charged particle. Experiments have shown that neutrinos can switch between these flavors as they travel, a process called neutrino oscillation."],["Neutrinos from the Sun","Nuclear fusion in the Sun’s core produces enormous numbers of neutrinos. They stream out into space at nearly the speed of light, and tens of billions pass through every square centimeter of Earth every second, day and night, since they travel through the planet as easily as through empty space."],["Catching neutrinos","To detect neutrinos, scientists build huge detectors deep underground, under mountains or in mines, to shield them from other particles. The detectors contain vast amounts of water, ice or special liquids. On the very rare occasions when a neutrino hits an atom, it produces a faint flash of light that sensitive instruments can record."]],
k:[["Proposed by","Wolfgang Pauli, 1930"],["Named by","Enrico Fermi"],["First detected","1956"],["Types","Electron, muon and tau neutrinos"],["Nobel Prize for oscillation","2015"]],
d:[["1930","Wolfgang Pauli proposes the neutrino"],["1934","Enrico Fermi develops a theory of beta decay including the neutrino"],["1956","Clyde Cowan and Frederick Reines detect neutrinos from a nuclear reactor"],["1987","Detectors record neutrinos from a supernova in a nearby galaxy"],["1998","Super-Kamiokande in Japan finds evidence that neutrinos change type"],["2013","The IceCube detector in Antarctica reports neutrinos from beyond the solar system"]],
s2:[["Pauli’s desperate remedy","In the 1920s physicists were puzzled by beta decay, a type of radioactivity in which energy seemed to disappear. In 1930 Wolfgang Pauli suggested that an unseen, neutral particle was carrying away the missing energy. He called his idea a desperate remedy and worried that the particle could never be detected. Enrico Fermi later named it the neutrino, Italian for little neutral one."],["The first detection","In 1956 the American physicists Clyde Cowan and Frederick Reines placed tanks of water and detectors next to a nuclear reactor in South Carolina, which produced huge numbers of neutrinos. They recorded the telltale signals of neutrinos striking protons and sent Pauli a telegram announcing their success. Reines shared the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1995."],["The solar neutrino problem","From the 1960s, experiments counting neutrinos from the Sun found only about a third to a half of the number expected. Scientists wondered whether their models of the Sun were wrong. The answer came around 2000: many electron neutrinos change into other flavors on their way to Earth, so early detectors, which saw only one flavor, missed them."],["Neutrinos have mass","The standard theory of particle physics originally assumed neutrinos had no mass. The discovery of oscillation, by the Super-Kamiokande experiment in Japan in 1998 and the Sudbury Neutrino Observatory in Canada in 2001, proved they must have a tiny mass. Takaaki Kajita and Arthur McDonald shared the 2015 Nobel Prize for this work. Their exact masses are still being measured."],["Messages from exploding stars","In February 1987 a star exploded as a supernova in the Large Magellanic Cloud, a small galaxy near the Milky Way. A few hours before its light was seen, detectors in Japan and the United States recorded a burst of about two dozen neutrinos. It was the first time neutrinos from beyond the solar system had been detected, opening a new way of studying the universe."],["Neutrino telescopes","IceCube, buried in a cubic kilometer of ice at the South Pole, watches for flashes of light from neutrinos crossing the ice. In 2018 it traced a high-energy neutrino to a distant galaxy with a giant black hole. Other huge detectors are being built in Japan, the United States and China to learn more about these elusive particles."]],
sp:["A telegram to Pauli","On 14 June 1956, Clyde Cowan and Frederick Reines sent a telegram to Wolfgang Pauli, who was attending a conference in Switzerland. It said that they had definitely detected neutrinos from a nuclear reactor. More than 25 years after Pauli had predicted the particle, worrying that he had proposed something no one could ever find, his ghostly particle had been caught. Pauli reportedly interrupted the conference to read the message aloud, and later replied that everything comes to him who knows how to wait. He is also said to have celebrated with friends by sharing a case of champagne."],
g:[["Neutrino","A tiny neutral particle that rarely interacts with matter"],["Lepton","A family of particles that includes electrons and neutrinos"],["Oscillation","The changing of a neutrino from one type to another"],["Weak nuclear force","One of the four fundamental forces, involved in radioactive decay"]],
f:["Neutrinos can pass through the whole Earth.","Neutrinos were first detected in 1956.","There are three types of neutrino."],
fun:"About 100 trillion neutrinos from the Sun pass through your body every second, and you never feel a thing."});

ADDART({id:"higgs",t:"The Higgs Boson",c:"Physics",i:"✨",w:"Higgs boson",r:["standardmodel","accelerators","particles","quantum","atom"],
p:[["A missing piece","The Higgs boson is a particle linked to the field that gives other particles mass."],["Found in 2012","Scientists at CERN announced its discovery on 4 July 2012."],["A Nobel Prize","Peter Higgs and François Englert received the 2013 Nobel Prize in Physics."]],
l:"The Higgs boson is an elementary particle connected to the Higgs field, an invisible field thought to fill the whole universe and give mass to fundamental particles such as electrons and quarks. Without it, these particles would have no mass, and atoms, stars and planets could not form. The field and its particle were proposed in 1964 by several physicists, including Peter Higgs, François Englert and Robert Brout. Nearly 50 years later, on 4 July 2012, scientists working with the Large Hadron Collider at CERN, near Geneva, announced that they had found it. The discovery completed the Standard Model, the theory describing the known fundamental particles and forces.",
s:[["What the Higgs field does","Physicists describe the Higgs field as something like an invisible substance spread through space. Particles that interact strongly with it behave as if they are heavy, while particles that interact weakly are light, and particles such as photons, which do not interact with it, have no mass at all. The Higgs boson is a ripple, or excitation, in this field."],["Why it mattered","The Standard Model successfully explained many experiments, but it needed a way for particles to have mass without breaking its mathematics. The Higgs mechanism provided that. Finding the Higgs boson was the final test of the theory’s last missing piece."],["The Large Hadron Collider","The Large Hadron Collider, or LHC, is the world’s largest and most powerful particle accelerator, a ring 27 kilometers around buried beneath the border of France and Switzerland. It smashes protons together at nearly the speed of light, briefly creating new particles, including, very rarely, Higgs bosons, which decay almost instantly into other particles."],["How it was found","Because a Higgs boson lasts only a tiny fraction of a second, scientists cannot see it directly. Instead, giant detectors called ATLAS and CMS recorded the particles it decays into. By analyzing billions of collisions, both teams found a small bump in the data at a mass of about 125 billion electronvolts, the signature of a new particle."]],
k:[["Proposed","1964"],["Discovery announced","4 July 2012"],["Where","CERN, Large Hadron Collider"],["Mass","About 125 GeV"],["Nobel Prize","Peter Higgs and François Englert, 2013"]],
d:[["1964","Higgs, Englert and Brout, and other physicists propose the mechanism"],["1993","Physicist Leon Lederman’s book popularizes the nickname God particle"],["2008","The Large Hadron Collider starts operating"],["2012","CERN announces the discovery of a Higgs-like particle"],["2013","Peter Higgs and François Englert receive the Nobel Prize in Physics"],["2024","Peter Higgs dies at the age of 94"]],
s2:[["Many discoverers","In 1964 three separate groups published papers on how particles could gain mass: Robert Brout and François Englert in Belgium, Peter Higgs in Scotland, and Gerald Guralnik, Carl Hagen and Tom Kibble in London. Higgs pointed out that the theory predicted a new particle, which came to bear his name. Brout died in 2011, before the Nobel Prize was awarded."],["The God particle","The physicist Leon Lederman nicknamed the Higgs boson the God particle in a 1993 book. The name caught on in newspapers but was disliked by many scientists, including Peter Higgs, who was not religious and felt it was misleading. Lederman later joked that his publisher would not let him call it the goddamn particle, his original complaint about how hard it was to find."],["The announcement","On 4 July 2012, scientists packed an auditorium at CERN, while others watched online around the world, as the ATLAS and CMS teams presented their results. When the evidence was shown, the audience broke into applause. Peter Higgs, then 83, was seen wiping away tears. CERN’s director, Rolf-Dieter Heuer, said, I think we have it."],["A global effort","Thousands of scientists and engineers from more than 100 countries worked on the LHC and its detectors, including researchers from Asia, Africa and Latin America. Physicists from the Philippines have also taken part in CERN collaborations and training programs, and CERN welcomes students from many nations."],["What remains unknown","The Higgs boson completed the Standard Model, but the model does not explain everything. It does not include gravity, dark matter or dark energy, and it cannot explain why the universe contains more matter than antimatter. Scientists study the Higgs boson’s properties closely, hoping to find small differences from predictions that might point to new physics."],["The future of colliders","The LHC is being upgraded to produce many more collisions from around 2030. Physicists have proposed even larger colliders, including a ring of about 90 kilometers at CERN and a large collider in China, to study the Higgs boson in greater detail. Supporters say these machines could reveal new particles, while critics question their very high cost."]],
sp:["A shy man and a famous particle","Peter Higgs was a quiet, modest physicist who disliked fame. He developed his idea in the summer of 1964 at the University of Edinburgh, and his paper predicting the new particle was at first rejected by one journal. For decades he watched as experiments searched without success. When the discovery was announced in 2012, he was overwhelmed, saying he had never expected it to happen in his lifetime. On the day the Nobel Prize was announced in 2013, he famously avoided reporters by going out for lunch, and only learned the news when a former neighbor congratulated him on the street."],
g:[["Higgs boson","A particle linked to the field that gives other particles mass"],["Higgs field","An invisible field thought to fill all of space"],["Standard Model","The theory describing known particles and forces"],["CERN","The European laboratory for particle physics near Geneva"]],
f:["The Higgs boson was found in 2012.","The LHC is 27 kilometers around.","It is nicknamed the God particle."],
fun:"A Higgs boson exists for only about a tenth of a billionth of a trillionth of a second before decaying."});

ADDART({id:"evolution",t:"Discovering Evolution",c:"History of Science",i:"🦎",w:"History of evolutionary thought",r:["evo","darwin","fossils","mendel","genome"],
p:[["Life changes","Evolution is the process by which living things change over many generations."],["How it works","Charles Darwin and Alfred Russel Wallace explained how it works through natural selection."],["Shared ancestry","All living things are related through common ancestors."]],
l:"The discovery of evolution, the process by which populations of living things change over many generations through changes in their inherited traits, is one of the great stories of science. The modern theory began with Charles Darwin and Alfred Russel Wallace, who in 1858 independently proposed natural selection as its main cause: individuals with traits that help them survive and reproduce tend to leave more offspring, so those traits become more common over time. Darwin’s book On the Origin of Species, published in 1859, presented the evidence in detail. Later discoveries in genetics, fossils and DNA have strongly supported the theory, which is the foundation of modern biology. It explains how the great diversity of life on Earth arose from common ancestors over billions of years.",
s:[["Natural selection","Members of a species vary in size, color, speed and many other traits, and some of these differences are inherited. More young are born than can survive, so individuals with helpful traits are more likely to live and reproduce. Over many generations this process, natural selection, can change a population and eventually produce new species."],["Evidence from fossils","Fossils preserved in layers of rock show that life has changed over time, from simple single-celled organisms to fish, amphibians, reptiles, mammals and birds. Transitional fossils, such as Archaeopteryx, which had features of both dinosaurs and birds, and Tiktaalik, a fish with limb-like fins, show stages between major groups."],["Evidence from genes","DNA allows scientists to compare species directly. Humans share most of their DNA with chimpanzees, and a large share with mice, fish and even plants, reflecting common ancestry. Genetic comparisons let scientists build family trees of life that match those based on anatomy and fossils."],["Evolution we can see","Evolution is usually slow, but some changes happen fast enough to observe. Bacteria evolve resistance to antibiotics, insects become resistant to pesticides, and scientists studying finches on the Galápagos Islands have measured changes in beak size after droughts within just a few generations."]],
k:[["Main mechanism","Natural selection"],["Proposed by","Charles Darwin and Alfred Russel Wallace"],["Key book","On the Origin of Species (1859)"],["Age of life on Earth","About 3.5 billion years or more"],["Modern humans appeared","About 300,000 years ago"]],
d:[["1809","Jean-Baptiste Lamarck proposes an early theory of evolution"],["1835","Charles Darwin visits the Galápagos Islands aboard HMS Beagle"],["1858","Darwin and Wallace present natural selection in London"],["1859","Darwin publishes On the Origin of Species"],["1866","Gregor Mendel publishes his laws of inheritance"],["1953","The structure of DNA is discovered"]],
s2:[["Darwin’s voyage","From 1831 to 1836 the young naturalist Charles Darwin sailed around the world aboard the British survey ship HMS Beagle. He collected fossils, plants and animals in South America, the Galápagos Islands and elsewhere. He noticed that animals on different islands differed slightly, and over the next 20 years he developed his ideas about how species change."],["Wallace in Southeast Asia","Alfred Russel Wallace reached the same idea while collecting animals in the Malay Archipelago, in what are now Malaysia and Indonesia. He also noticed a sharp boundary between Asian and Australian animals, now called the Wallace Line. In 1858 he sent his ideas to Darwin, and their work was presented together at a meeting in London."],["Genetics joins the theory","Darwin did not know how traits were passed on. The work of Gregor Mendel on pea plants, rediscovered in 1900, showed that traits are inherited through units now called genes. In the 1930s and 1940s scientists combined genetics and natural selection into the modern synthesis, and the discovery of DNA’s structure in 1953 revealed how genes are copied and changed."],["Evolution in the Philippines","The Philippines is a natural laboratory of evolution. Because its islands were separated for long periods, many species evolved there that are found nowhere else, such as the Philippine eagle, the tarsier and many kinds of cloud rats and frogs. In 2019 scientists described Homo luzonensis, an extinct human species, from fossils found in Callao Cave in Cagayan."],["Human evolution","Fossils and DNA show that humans and chimpanzees shared a common ancestor several million years ago. Many extinct human species lived in the past, including Homo erectus and the Neanderthals. Our own species, Homo sapiens, appeared in Africa about 300,000 years ago and later spread around the world."],["Evolution and belief","Evolution is accepted by the overwhelming majority of scientists. Some religious groups reject it, holding that species were created separately as described in literal readings of scripture, a view called creationism. Many other religious communities, including the Catholic Church, teach that evolution can be compatible with faith in a creator. Debates have arisen in some countries over how evolution is taught in schools."]],
sp:["The finches that changed","Since 1973 the biologists Peter and Rosemary Grant have spent part of nearly every year on the tiny Galápagos island of Daphne Major, catching, measuring and tagging its finches. In 1977 a severe drought killed most of the plants with small, soft seeds, leaving mainly large, hard seeds. Finches with bigger, stronger beaks survived better, and the next generation had noticeably larger beaks. Years later, when wet weather brought back small seeds, the trend reversed. The Grants showed that natural selection, which Darwin thought too slow to watch, could be measured in real time."],
g:[["Evolution","Change in the inherited traits of living things over generations"],["Natural selection","The process by which helpful traits become more common"],["Species","A group of living things that can breed together"],["Common ancestor","An ancestor shared by two or more species"]],
f:["Darwin published On the Origin of Species in 1859.","Wallace developed the same idea in Southeast Asia.","Bacteria can evolve resistance to antibiotics."],
fun:"Darwin spent eight years studying barnacles before publishing his theory, partly to build his reputation as a careful scientist."});

