// Arts and Culture, more articles: the sarswela, documentary film, comedy and street art.
ADDART({id:"zarzuela",t:"Sarswela",c:"Performing Arts",i:"🎭",w:"Sarsuwela",r:["theatre","opera","phcinema","opm","musicals"],
p:[["Musical theater","The sarswela is a form of musical theater with spoken dialogue, songs and dances."],["From Spain","It came from the Spanish zarzuela and became a beloved Filipino art form."],["A golden age","Tagalog sarswela flourished in the early 1900s."]],
l:"The sarswela, also spelled sarsuwela, is a form of musical theater that mixes spoken scenes with songs, dances and instrumental music. It developed from the zarzuela of Spain, a popular theater form that takes its name from a royal hunting lodge near Madrid where such entertainments were staged in the 1600s. Spanish troupes brought the zarzuela to Manila in the late 1800s, and Filipino writers and composers soon created their own in Tagalog and other Philippine languages, with local stories, characters and music such as the kundiman. Its golden age came in the first decades of the 1900s, before films and vaudeville drew audiences away. Today the sarswela is revived by theater companies and schools as part of the nation’s cultural heritage.",
s:[["The Spanish zarzuela","The zarzuela began in 17th-century Spain as court entertainment combining spoken drama with music. The playwright Pedro Calderón de la Barca wrote some of the earliest works. In the 1800s it became hugely popular with ordinary Spaniards, with comic, romantic stories set in everyday city and village life."],["Arrival in the Philippines","Spanish touring companies performed zarzuelas in Manila from the late 1870s. Filipino audiences loved the mix of drama, romance, comedy and music. Local artists began translating Spanish works and then writing their own, and by the end of the 1800s sarswela in Philippine languages was appearing on Manila stages."],["Stories and characters","Filipino sarswelas often told stories of love between young people from different social classes, family conflicts, greedy landlords, village fiestas and the problems of everyday life. Many included comic servants and lively crowd scenes, and they usually ended happily. Some carried messages about patriotism, freedom or social justice."],["Music of the sarswela","Composers wrote songs in styles audiences knew, including the kundiman, the slow and heartfelt Filipino love song, the danza and lively marches. Orchestras or small bands accompanied the singers. Many sarswela songs became popular hits that people sang at home and at gatherings long after the shows closed."]],
k:[["Also spelled","Sarsuwela"],["Origin","Spanish zarzuela"],["Golden age in the Philippines","About 1900 to 1930"],["Famous work","Walang Sugat (1902)"],["Famous star","Atang de la Rama"]],
d:[["1657","Calderón de la Barca writes one of the first Spanish zarzuelas"],["1878","Spanish zarzuela troupes begin performing regularly in Manila"],["1902","Walang Sugat by Severino Reyes premieres"],["1917","Dalagang Bukid by Hermogenes Ilagan and Leon Ignacio premieres"],["1919","A film version of Dalagang Bukid becomes the first Filipino-made feature film"],["1987","Atang de la Rama is named National Artist for Theater and Music"]],
s2:[["Walang Sugat","Walang Sugat, meaning not wounded, was written by Severino Reyes with music by Fulgencio Tolentino and first performed in 1902. Set during the revolution against Spain, it tells of Tenyong, a young man who joins the revolutionaries, and his beloved Julia, whose mother wants her to marry a rich man. It is one of the most famous and frequently revived Filipino sarswelas."],["Dalagang Bukid","Dalagang Bukid, meaning country maiden, by Hermogenes Ilagan with music by Leon Ignacio, premiered in 1917. It tells of a flower vendor whose parents push her to marry a wealthy old man. In 1919 José Nepomuceno turned it into a film, considered the first feature film made by a Filipino, starring Atang de la Rama."],["Theater and resistance","In the early years of American rule, some Filipino playwrights wrote dramas with hidden messages against colonial rule, using symbols such as the flag, which was then banned. Several writers were arrested for these so-called seditious plays. Sarswelas themselves were mostly romantic and comic, but some also expressed national pride."],["Atang de la Rama","Honorata de la Rama, known as Atang, began performing as a child and became the most celebrated sarswela star of her time, admired as the Queen of the Kundiman. She performed across the Philippines and abroad and later championed Filipino theater and music. In 1987 she was named a National Artist."],["Decline and revival","From the 1920s and 1930s, sarswela faced competition from movies, radio and bodabil, or vaudeville. Many theaters closed, and the sarswela became rare. From the 1970s onward, groups such as the Cultural Center of the Philippines, Tanghalang Pilipino, university theater companies and regional groups revived classic works and created new ones."],["Beyond Manila","Sarswela also thrived in other regions in local languages. In Iloilo and other parts of the Visayas, playwrights wrote sarswelas in Hiligaynon and Cebuano, and Ilocano and Pampango versions were performed in the north. These local traditions added regional music, humor and stories to the form."]],
sp:["An evening at the sarswela","In the early 1900s, a night at the sarswela was a major social event in Manila and provincial towns. Families dressed in their best clothes and filled wooden theaters such as the Teatro Zorrilla and the Manila Grand Opera House. Vendors sold snacks outside, and the audience cheered favorite singers, laughed at comic servants and wept during sad love songs. Popular performers became stars whose photographs were sold and whose songs were hummed in the streets. For many Filipinos it was the main form of entertainment before the coming of cinema."],
g:[["Sarswela","Filipino musical theater with spoken dialogue and songs"],["Zarzuela","The Spanish musical theater form from which the sarswela came"],["Kundiman","A slow, heartfelt Filipino love song"],["Bodabil","Filipino vaudeville, a variety show of songs, dances and comedy"]],
f:["The sarswela came from the Spanish zarzuela.","Walang Sugat premiered in 1902.","Atang de la Rama was named a National Artist."],
fun:"The word zarzuela comes from the Palacio de la Zarzuela, a royal lodge near Madrid whose name refers to the brambles, zarzas, that grew there."});

ADDART({id:"documentary",t:"Documentary Film",c:"Film",i:"🎥",w:"Documentary film",r:["cinema","phcinema","tv","streaming","photography"],
p:[["Real life on screen","A documentary is a nonfiction film about real people, places and events."],["Many kinds","Documentaries cover nature, history, science, politics, music and everyday life."],["Truth and storytelling","Filmmakers balance telling a story with showing the truth."]],
l:"A documentary is a nonfiction film or program that records real people, places and events in order to inform, explain, persuade or preserve a record. Documentaries can explore almost any subject, from wildlife and science to history, politics, sport, music and ordinary lives. The word was first used in this sense in 1926 by the Scottish filmmaker John Grierson, who described the documentary as the creative treatment of actuality. Early landmarks include Robert Flaherty’s Nanook of the North in 1922. Today documentaries are made for cinemas, television and streaming services, and range from huge nature series to small films shot on phones. Questions about truth, fairness and the filmmaker’s point of view are central to the art form.",
s:[["Beginnings","The first films ever shown, by the Lumière brothers in France in 1895, were short scenes of real life, such as workers leaving a factory and a train arriving at a station. Newsreels showing current events became common in cinemas in the early 1900s, informing audiences before television existed."],["Nanook of the North","In 1922 the American filmmaker Robert Flaherty released Nanook of the North, about an Inuit family in Arctic Canada. It was a great success and is often called the first feature-length documentary. Later it was revealed that Flaherty staged some scenes and asked people to use traditional methods they no longer used, starting long debates about how much documentaries may shape reality."],["Styles of documentary","Some documentaries use a narrator who explains what is shown. Others, in the style called observational or cinéma vérité, follow people with a camera and let events unfold. Some mix interviews, archive footage, animation and reenactments. Each approach gives a different sense of what is real."],["Nature documentaries","Wildlife films have become one of the most popular kinds of documentary. The British naturalist David Attenborough has presented landmark series such as Life on Earth in 1979 and Planet Earth in 2006, using new camera technology to show animal behavior never filmed before. Such films have increased public interest in conservation."]],
k:[["Term coined by","John Grierson, 1926"],["Early landmark","Nanook of the North (1922)"],["First films of real life","Lumière brothers, 1895"],["Famous presenter","David Attenborough"],["Also called","Docu, doc"]],
d:[["1895","The Lumière brothers show short films of real life in Paris"],["1922","Robert Flaherty releases Nanook of the North"],["1926","John Grierson uses the word documentary for film"],["1929","Dziga Vertov makes Man with a Movie Camera"],["1979","David Attenborough’s Life on Earth is broadcast"],["2006","Planet Earth is broadcast"]],
s2:[["Propaganda films","Documentary techniques can be used to persuade rather than inform. During the 1930s and the Second World War, governments made films to spread their political messages. Triumph of the Will, made by Leni Riefenstahl in 1935 for the Nazi regime, is often studied as a powerful but deeply troubling example of how film can be used as propaganda."],["Documentaries that changed things","Some documentaries have influenced public debate and policy, on subjects such as the environment, health, food and justice. Films about wrongful convictions have led to cases being reopened, while environmental documentaries have raised awareness of climate change, plastic pollution and endangered species. Critics note that films with a strong message may leave out other viewpoints."],["Documentaries in the Philippines","Filipino filmmakers have made documentaries about history, culture, Indigenous communities, migrant workers, disasters and politics. Television networks have long produced documentary programs and investigative reports, and film festivals such as Cinemalaya and those of the Cultural Center of the Philippines have featured documentaries. Some Filipino documentaries have won awards at international festivals."],["The ethics of filming real people","Documentary makers must think carefully about the people they film. They ask for consent, try to represent people fairly, and consider how a film might affect those shown, especially children or vulnerable people. Viewers and critics debate whether reenactments, music and editing can make a film misleading even when its facts are accurate."],["The streaming boom","Streaming services have greatly increased the number of documentaries made and watched. True crime series, sports documentaries, celebrity biographies and nature programs attract large global audiences. Some critics worry that the pressure to entertain encourages sensational stories, while others welcome the chance for more voices and subjects to reach viewers."],["Making a documentary today","Light digital cameras, drones and phones have made documentary filmmaking more affordable. Students, community groups and citizen journalists can record important events and share them online. Skills such as research, interviewing, fact-checking and careful editing remain essential to making a documentary that viewers can trust."]],
sp:["Filming the deep","Making a big nature documentary can take years. For a single sequence, a camera crew may wait weeks in a hide in the rainforest or spend months at sea to capture an animal behavior that lasts only a few minutes. Crews have used cameras that work in near darkness, tiny cameras hidden inside fake rocks or animals, and deep-sea submersibles to film creatures thousands of meters below the surface. When the BBC series Blue Planet II showed the effects of plastic waste on ocean life in 2017, it sparked public calls to reduce single-use plastics in several countries."],
g:[["Documentary","A nonfiction film about real people and events"],["Newsreel","A short film of news shown in cinemas"],["Cinéma vérité","A documentary style that observes events directly"],["Reenactment","Acting out real past events for a film"]],
f:["John Grierson coined the term documentary in 1926.","Nanook of the North was released in 1922.","The first films ever shown were of real life."],
fun:"The very first films shown to a paying audience, in Paris in 1895, were short documentaries less than a minute long."});

ADDART({id:"comedy",t:"Comedy",c:"Performing Arts",i:"😂",w:"Comedy",r:["theatre","tv","cinema","phcinema","memes"],
p:[["Making people laugh","Comedy is entertainment meant to make people laugh."],["Ancient roots","Comedy began as a form of theater in ancient Greece."],["Many forms","Today it includes stand-up, sitcoms, comedy films, sketches and online videos."]],
l:"Comedy is a kind of entertainment, in theater, film, television, writing and live performance, that aims to amuse people and make them laugh. As a form of drama, it began in ancient Greece, where comic plays were performed at festivals in Athens. Over the centuries comedy took many forms, including the masked clowns of Italian commedia dell’arte, the plays of Shakespeare and Molière, silent film slapstick, radio and television sitcoms, stand-up routines and short online videos. Comedy can be gentle or sharp, silly or clever, and often comments on daily life, society and politics. In the Philippines, comedians have been among the country’s most beloved entertainers.",
s:[["Greek and Roman comedy","Ancient Greek comedy was performed at festivals in honor of the god Dionysus. Aristophanes, who wrote in the 400s BC, mocked politicians, philosophers and the war of his time. Later Greek and Roman writers, such as Menander, Plautus and Terence, wrote comedies about mistaken identities, clever servants and young lovers, plots still used today."],["Commedia dell’arte","In 16th-century Italy, traveling troupes performed commedia dell’arte, improvised comedies with stock characters wearing masks, such as the foolish old merchant Pantalone and the tricky servant Arlecchino, or Harlequin. Their physical humor and comic routines influenced theater across Europe."],["Shakespeare and Molière","William Shakespeare wrote comedies such as A Midsummer Night’s Dream and Twelfth Night, full of disguises, misunderstandings and happy endings. In France, Molière wrote sharp comedies mocking hypocrisy, greed and snobbery, such as Tartuffe and The Miser."],["Comedy on screen","Silent film comedians such as Charlie Chaplin and Buster Keaton became world stars with slapstick, comic chases and stunts. With radio and television came situation comedies, or sitcoms, built around the same characters in everyday settings, along with sketch shows, comedy variety programs and talk shows."]],
k:[["Origin","Ancient Greece"],["Famous Greek comic playwright","Aristophanes"],["Italian masked comedy","Commedia dell’arte"],["Silent film stars","Charlie Chaplin, Buster Keaton"],["Philippine King of Comedy","Dolphy"]],
d:[["c. 425 BC","Aristophanes’ comedies are performed in Athens"],["1550s","Commedia dell’arte troupes perform across Italy"],["1664","Molière’s Tartuffe is first performed"],["1914","Charlie Chaplin’s Little Tramp character first appears on film"],["1979","Eat Bulaga! premieres on Philippine television"],["2010","Dolphy receives the Grand Collar of the Order of the Golden Heart"]],
s2:[["Stand-up comedy","In stand-up comedy, a single performer speaks directly to an audience, telling jokes and stories, often about their own life and observations. It grew from music hall and vaudeville acts and became especially popular in the United States and Britain in the 20th century. Comedy clubs, television specials and online videos have spread it worldwide, including to comedy bars in Metro Manila."],["Comedy in the Philippines","Filipino comedy grew from bodabil, the variety shows of the early 1900s, and from radio programs. Comedians such as Dolphy, often called the King of Comedy, Panchito, Chiquito and Babalu starred in hugely popular films and television shows. Later stars included Vic Sotto, Joey de Leon, Tito Sotto, Vice Ganda and many others, whose humor often relies on wordplay, timing and playful teasing."],["Why we laugh","Scientists and philosophers have offered several explanations for laughter. Some say we laugh at surprise, when an expected pattern is suddenly broken; others say laughter releases tension or shows a sense of superiority. Laughter also bonds people together, which may be why we laugh much more when we are with others than when alone."],["Satire","Satire uses humor to criticize people, governments or society. It has a long history, from Aristophanes to the writer Jonathan Swift, and today appears in political cartoons, comedy news shows, parody songs and online memes. In many countries satire is valued as a form of free expression, while some governments have punished comedians for mocking leaders."],["Comedy and offense","Comedy often pushes boundaries, and audiences disagree about what is acceptable. Some argue that comedians should be free to joke about any subject, while others say jokes that rely on stereotypes about race, religion, gender, disability or appearance can hurt people. Debates about where to draw the line continue among comedians and audiences."],["Comedy goes online","Social media and video platforms allow anyone to share comic sketches, parodies, reaction videos and memes, reaching millions of viewers without a television network or film studio. Many Filipino comedians and content creators first became famous online before moving to television and film."]],
sp:["The longest-running noontime show","On 30 July 1979 a variety program called Eat Bulaga! premiered on Philippine television, hosted by the comic trio Tito Sotto, Vic Sotto and Joey de Leon. Mixing games, songs, comedy segments and giveaways, it became a lunchtime habit for millions of Filipino families and one of the longest-running television shows of its kind in the world. Its comic segments, catchphrases and segments featuring ordinary people became part of popular culture. In 2023 the hosts left the program’s original production company and continued on another channel, leading to a legal dispute over the show’s name."],
g:[["Comedy","Entertainment meant to make people laugh"],["Satire","Humor that criticizes people or society"],["Slapstick","Comedy based on physical action, such as falls and chases"],["Sitcom","A situation comedy, usually on television"]],
f:["Comedy began in ancient Greece.","Charlie Chaplin was a silent film star.","Dolphy is called the King of Comedy."],
fun:"People are about 30 times more likely to laugh when they are with others than when they are alone, according to some studies."});

ADDART({id:"streetart",t:"Street Art",c:"Art",i:"🎨",w:"Street art",r:["modernart","jeepney","photography","architecture","caveart"],
p:[["Art in public","Street art is art made in public places such as walls, streets and bridges."],["Graffiti and murals","It includes graffiti, painted murals, stencils, stickers and installations."],["Art or vandalism","Whether it is art or vandalism depends on permission and point of view."]],
l:"Street art is visual art created in public spaces, such as walls, buildings, bridges, sidewalks and vehicles, where anyone can see it. It includes graffiti, large painted murals, stencil art, posters, stickers, mosaics and sculptures. People have written and drawn on public walls since ancient times, but modern street art grew from the graffiti that spread across New York City in the 1970s. Some street art is painted illegally, while much is now commissioned by cities, businesses and communities to brighten neighborhoods. Street artists such as Banksy have become world-famous, and their work sells for millions. Debates continue over when street art is a public gift and when it is vandalism.",
s:[["Ancient graffiti","The word graffiti comes from the Italian for scratched. Ancient Romans scratched messages, jokes, election slogans and drawings on walls, many of which survived at Pompeii, the city buried by the eruption of Mount Vesuvius in AD 79. Even earlier, people painted and carved images on cave walls and rocks."],["New York graffiti","In the late 1960s and 1970s, young people in Philadelphia and New York began writing their nicknames, called tags, on walls and subway trains. Writers competed to create bigger, more colorful and more stylish pieces. Graffiti became one of the four elements of hip-hop culture, along with rapping, DJing and breakdancing."],["Murals","Murals are large paintings made directly on walls. The Mexican muralists of the 1920s and 1930s, such as Diego Rivera, José Clemente Orozco and David Alfaro Siqueiros, painted huge public works about history and the lives of workers. Many modern street artists paint murals with permission, often several stories high."],["Stencils and stickers","Some street artists use stencils, cut-out designs that let them spray an image quickly and repeat it in many places. Others paste up posters or stick small printed stickers. The British artist Banksy is famous for stencil works with humorous or political messages, while the American artist Shepard Fairey became known for his sticker and poster campaigns."]],
k:[["Word graffiti from","Italian, meaning scratched"],["Modern graffiti began","Philadelphia and New York, late 1960s and 1970s"],["Famous street artist","Banksy"],["Mexican muralists","Rivera, Orozco, Siqueiros"],["Philippine example","Painted jeepneys"]],
d:[["AD 79","The eruption of Vesuvius preserves graffiti on the walls of Pompeii"],["1920s","Mexican muralists begin painting large public murals"],["1970s","Graffiti spreads across New York City’s subway trains"],["1990","Artists paint the East Side Gallery on a remaining stretch of the Berlin Wall"],["2018","A Banksy painting partly shreds itself just after being sold at auction"],["2021","The shredded Banksy work sells again for a record price"]],
s2:[["Art or vandalism?","Painting on property without the owner’s permission is illegal in most places, and cleaning graffiti costs cities large sums. Critics see unauthorized graffiti as vandalism that makes neighborhoods look neglected. Supporters argue that street art brings color, creativity and important messages to dull or forgotten spaces. Many cities now offer legal walls where artists may paint freely."],["Banksy","Banksy is a British street artist whose real identity has been kept secret. Since the 1990s, Banksy’s stencil works have appeared in Bristol, London, New York, Bethlehem and elsewhere, often commenting on war, money, power and consumerism. In 2018, moments after his painting Girl with Balloon sold at auction, a hidden shredder in the frame cut it partly into strips. Renamed Love is in the Bin, it later sold for a record price."],["The Berlin Wall","While the Berlin Wall divided the city during the Cold War, its western side became covered in graffiti and paintings. After the wall fell in 1989, artists from many countries painted a long remaining section, now called the East Side Gallery. It is one of the largest open-air galleries in the world."],["Street art in the Philippines","Street art in the Philippines ranges from the brightly painted jeepneys, decorated with pictures, slogans and chrome, to murals on walls, bridges and schools. Cities and communities have commissioned murals on subjects such as Philippine heroes, nature, local culture and Baybayin script. Art festivals and groups of muralists have painted large works in Manila, Cebu, Davao, Baguio and other cities."],["Murals that help communities","Mural projects often involve local residents, including children, in choosing subjects and painting together. Such projects can make neighborhoods more welcoming, attract visitors and support local businesses. Some murals honor community members or mark important events, while others share messages about health, the environment or disaster preparedness."],["Street art and the art world","Once seen only as rebellion, street art is now shown in galleries and museums, and leading artists sell works to collectors. Some fans worry that commercial success goes against the spirit of free public art, and that neighborhoods made popular by murals can become more expensive for the people who live there."]],
sp:["Art on wheels","The Philippine jeepney is sometimes called a moving work of street art. After the Second World War, Filipinos rebuilt leftover American military jeeps into longer passenger vehicles and decorated them to attract riders. Owners and painters added bright colors, chrome horses on the hood, religious images, landscapes, family names and witty sayings. Workshops such as those in Las Piñas became famous for their craftsmanship. As older jeepneys are replaced under a government modernization program, many artists and residents hope to preserve their decorations as part of the nation’s visual culture."],
g:[["Street art","Art created in public places"],["Graffiti","Writing or drawings on walls or other surfaces in public"],["Mural","A large painting made directly on a wall"],["Stencil","A cut-out design used to paint the same image many times"]],
f:["Graffiti means scratched in Italian.","Graffiti survives on the walls of ancient Pompeii.","Jeepneys are a form of Philippine street art."],
fun:"Graffiti scratched by ancient Romans in Pompeii includes jokes, love notes, insults and even election campaign slogans."});

