/* data/modern6.js */
// The Modern World, part 5: biodiversity loss, extreme heat, antimicrobial resistance, wearable technology, gene therapy, influencers and podcasts.
ADDART({id:"biodivloss",t:"Biodiversity Loss",c:"Environment",i:"🦋",w:"Biodiversity loss",r:["extinction","conservation","deforestation","eagle","climchange"],
p:[["Life in decline","Biodiversity loss is the decline in the variety of living things on Earth."],["Main causes","Habitat loss, overuse of wildlife, climate change, pollution and invasive species are the main causes."],["Protecting nature","Countries have agreed to protect 30 percent of land and sea by 2030."]],
l:"Biodiversity, short for biological diversity, is the variety of life on Earth, from genes and species to whole ecosystems such as forests, coral reefs and wetlands. Biodiversity loss is the reduction of this variety, through the extinction of species, the shrinking of wildlife populations and the destruction of habitats. Scientists report that wildlife populations have declined sharply since 1970 and that around a million species may be at risk of extinction. The main causes are the conversion of forests and other natural areas to farms and cities, overfishing and hunting, climate change, pollution and invasive species. In 2022 nearly 200 countries adopted a global agreement to halt and reverse the loss of nature by 2030.",
s:[["Why biodiversity matters","Living things provide food, clean water, medicines, building materials and fertile soil. Bees and other insects pollinate many crops, forests store carbon and protect watersheds, and mangroves and reefs shield coasts from storms. Many people also value nature for its beauty and for cultural and spiritual reasons, and believe other species have a right to exist."],["Measuring the decline","In 2019 an international panel of scientists, IPBES, estimated that around one million animal and plant species are threatened with extinction, many within decades. The Living Planet Index, published by WWF and the Zoological Society of London, reported in 2024 an average decline of about 73 percent in monitored wildlife populations since 1970."],["The main drivers","The biggest cause of biodiversity loss is changes in how land and sea are used, especially clearing forests and draining wetlands for agriculture. Other major causes are the direct harvesting of plants and animals, climate change, pollution from plastics, chemicals and fertilizers, and invasive species that outcompete native ones."],["A sixth mass extinction?","Earth has experienced five great mass extinctions, including the one that ended the age of dinosaurs. Some scientists warn that human activities may be causing a sixth. Others argue that current losses, though serious and rapid, have not yet reached the scale of past mass extinctions, and that there is still time to prevent one."]],
k:[["Species at risk","Around 1 million (IPBES, 2019)"],["Wildlife decline since 1970","About 73 percent on average (Living Planet Index, 2024)"],["Global agreement","Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework, 2022"],["Key target","Protect 30 percent of land and sea by 2030"],["Philippine status","Megadiverse country"]],
d:[["1948","The International Union for Conservation of Nature is founded"],["1964","The first Red List of threatened species is started"],["1992","The Convention on Biological Diversity is signed at the Rio Earth Summit"],["2019","IPBES warns that a million species face extinction"],["2022","Countries adopt the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework"],["2024","The Living Planet Index reports a 73 percent average decline in wildlife populations"]],
s2:[["The Philippines, a megadiverse country","The Philippines is one of about 17 megadiverse countries, which together hold most of the world’s species. A large share of its mammals, birds, reptiles, amphibians and plants are found nowhere else. It is also considered a biodiversity hotspot, meaning that its rich wildlife is under serious threat, especially from the loss of forests."],["Threatened Philippine species","The Philippine eagle, the national bird, is critically endangered, with only a few hundred pairs estimated to remain in the wild. Other threatened species include the tamaraw of Mindoro, the Philippine cockatoo, the Visayan warty pig and many frogs, corals and plants. Conservation groups and government agencies run breeding programs and protect key forests."],["30 by 30","The Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework, agreed in December 2022, set targets including protecting at least 30 percent of the world’s land and oceans by 2030, restoring degraded ecosystems and reducing harmful subsidies. Supporters call it a landmark deal for nature, while some Indigenous groups and experts stress that protected areas must respect the rights of local communities."],["Indigenous peoples and nature","Lands managed by Indigenous peoples often contain high levels of biodiversity. In the Philippines, Indigenous communities such as the Ifugao, the Lumad of Mindanao and the Palaw’an have long traditions of caring for forests and rice terraces. Many experts argue that recognizing Indigenous land rights is an effective way to protect nature."],["Balancing people and nature","Protecting biodiversity can conflict with other needs, such as growing food, building roads and creating jobs. Debates continue over how to share the costs and benefits, especially between rich and poor countries. Approaches include paying communities to protect forests, sustainable fishing rules, wildlife-friendly farming and ecotourism."],["What people can do","Individuals can help by supporting protected areas, planting native trees, reducing waste and plastic use, avoiding products from endangered species, choosing sustainably caught seafood and joining citizen science projects that count birds, butterflies and other wildlife."]],
sp:["The return of the mangroves","Mangrove forests once lined much of the Philippine coastline, but large areas were cut down for fishponds, firewood and coastal development during the 20th century. Mangroves shelter young fish and crabs, filter water and protect villages from storm surges. After Typhoon Haiyan struck in 2013, studies found that places with healthy mangroves suffered less damage. Communities, schools and conservation groups have since planted mangrove seedlings along many coasts. Scientists stress that the right species must be planted in the right places, because poorly planned planting can fail, but well-managed projects have helped bring back fish, birds and protection from the sea."],
g:[["Biodiversity","The variety of life on Earth"],["Extinction","The disappearance of a species forever"],["Habitat","The natural home of a plant or animal"],["Invasive species","A species introduced to a new area where it causes harm"]],
f:["Around a million species may be at risk of extinction.","The Philippines is a megadiverse country.","The Philippine eagle is critically endangered."],
fun:"Scientists have described only a fraction of the species thought to live on Earth; many insects, fungi and deep-sea animals have yet to be named."});

ADDART({id:"extremeheat",t:"Extreme Heat",c:"Environment",i:"🥵",w:"Heat wave",r:["climchange","climate","water","megacities","firstaid"],
p:[["Dangerous temperatures","Extreme heat is a period of unusually and dangerously hot weather."],["Getting hotter","Heat waves are becoming more frequent and intense as the climate warms."],["Staying safe","Drinking water, staying in the shade and checking on others save lives."]],
l:"Extreme heat refers to periods of unusually high temperatures, often combined with high humidity, that can be dangerous to people, animals, crops and infrastructure. Long stretches of such weather are called heat waves. Extreme heat is one of the deadliest weather hazards, causing heat exhaustion, heatstroke and the worsening of heart and lung diseases, especially among older people, young children, outdoor workers and the poor. Scientists have found that heat waves are becoming more frequent, longer and more intense as Earth’s climate warms. The years 2023 and 2024 were the hottest on record worldwide. Cities, schools and workplaces are adapting with warning systems, cooling centers, shade and new building designs.",
s:[["What makes heat dangerous","The human body cools itself mainly by sweating. When the air is very hot and humid, sweat evaporates slowly, and the body struggles to lose heat. Weather services use measures such as the heat index, which combines temperature and humidity to show how hot it actually feels, to warn the public."],["Heat illness","Heat exhaustion causes heavy sweating, weakness, dizziness, headache and nausea. If the body keeps heating up, it can lead to heatstroke, a medical emergency in which body temperature rises above about 40°C and a person may become confused or collapse. Heatstroke requires immediate cooling and medical help."],["A warming world","As greenhouse gases warm the planet, hot days have become hotter and more common. Studies by climate scientists have found that many recent heat waves would have been extremely unlikely without climate change. The World Meteorological Organization confirmed that 2024 was the warmest year on record, about 1.5°C above preindustrial levels."],["Heat in cities","Cities are often several degrees hotter than the surrounding countryside, an effect called the urban heat island. Concrete, asphalt and buildings absorb heat during the day and release it at night, while traffic and air conditioners add more. Trees, parks, water features and light-colored roofs can help cool urban areas."]],
k:[["Measure used in the Philippines","Heat index"],["Danger level in the Philippines","Heat index of 42 to 51°C"],["Hottest year on record","2024"],["Most serious heat illness","Heatstroke"],["City effect","Urban heat island"]],
d:[["2003","A heat wave in Europe causes tens of thousands of deaths"],["2010","A severe heat wave and wildfires strike Russia"],["2021","A heat dome brings record temperatures to western Canada and the United States"],["2023","The year becomes the hottest on record at the time"],["2024","Extreme heat leads to class suspensions across the Philippines"],["2025","The Philippines returns the start of the school year to June, partly because of the heat"]],
s2:[["Heat in the Philippines","The hottest months in the Philippines are usually April and May, before the rainy season. In 2024 the heat index reached dangerous levels in many places, and schools across the country suspended in-person classes or shifted to online learning. PAGASA, the national weather agency, issues daily heat index forecasts and warnings."],["Changing the school calendar","For several years the Philippine school year began in August or later, meaning classes continued through the hot months of April and May. After widespread concern about students and teachers suffering in hot classrooms, the Department of Education moved the opening of classes back to June beginning in 2025, so that the hottest period would fall during the school break."],["Workers and heat","Construction workers, farmers, street vendors, traffic enforcers and delivery riders are especially exposed to extreme heat. Some countries require rest breaks, shade and water for outdoor workers, or ban heavy outdoor work during the hottest hours. Labor groups in many places have called for stronger heat protections."],["Who is most at risk","Older people, babies and young children, pregnant women, people with chronic illnesses and those living alone or without cooling are most at risk. Poorer households often live in crowded, poorly ventilated homes with metal roofs that trap heat, making them more vulnerable."],["Keeping cool safely","Health authorities advise drinking plenty of water, avoiding the midday sun, wearing light, loose clothing and a hat, resting in shade or cool rooms and never leaving children or pets in parked cars. Checking on elderly neighbors and relatives can save lives. Anyone showing signs of heatstroke should be moved to a cool place, cooled with water and taken to a doctor."],["Adapting to a hotter future","Cities and countries are adapting by planting trees, creating cooling centers, designing buildings with better ventilation and shade, and setting up heat warning systems. Air conditioning saves lives but uses large amounts of electricity, so engineers are working on more efficient cooling and passive designs that keep buildings cool naturally."]],
sp:["The traditional cool house","Long before air conditioning, Filipinos built homes designed for a hot, wet climate. The bahay kubo, a small house of bamboo and nipa palm, stands on stilts that let air flow underneath, with woven walls that breathe and a steep thatched roof that sheds rain and keeps out the Sun’s heat. The larger bahay na bato of the Spanish era had thick stone lower walls and an upper floor of wood with wide sliding windows made of capiz shells and ventanillas, small openings below the windows, to let breezes through. Architects today study these designs for ideas on keeping modern buildings cool without using so much energy."],
g:[["Heat wave","A long period of unusually hot weather"],["Heat index","How hot it feels when humidity is combined with temperature"],["Heatstroke","A dangerous rise in body temperature"],["Urban heat island","A city that is warmer than the surrounding countryside"]],
f:["2024 was the hottest year on record.","Cities are hotter than the countryside.","Heatstroke is a medical emergency."],
fun:"A city can be several degrees warmer than the nearby countryside at night because buildings and roads release heat stored during the day."});

ADDART({id:"amr",t:"Antimicrobial Resistance",c:"Health",i:"🦠",w:"Antimicrobial resistance",r:["antibiotics","penicil","evolution","pandemic","hygiene"],
p:[["When medicines stop working","Antimicrobial resistance happens when germs evolve to survive the medicines meant to kill them."],["Hard-to-treat germs","Germs resistant to many medicines are called superbugs."],["A global threat","Resistant infections kill more than a million people each year."]],
l:"Antimicrobial resistance, often shortened to AMR, happens when bacteria, viruses, fungi and parasites change over time so that medicines such as antibiotics no longer kill them or stop their growth. Infections then become harder or impossible to treat, raising the risk of severe illness, spread of disease and death. Germs that resist many medicines are often called superbugs. Resistance develops naturally through evolution, but it is greatly sped up by the overuse and misuse of antibiotics in people and animals. Researchers estimate that resistant bacterial infections directly cause more than a million deaths a year worldwide, and health organizations rank AMR among the greatest threats to global health.",
s:[["How resistance develops","When antibiotics are used, they kill most of the bacteria causing an infection, but a few may survive because of random genetic changes. These survivors multiply and pass on their resistance. Bacteria can also share resistance genes with each other, spreading resistance quickly between different kinds of germs."],["Misuse of antibiotics","Taking antibiotics for illnesses they cannot cure, such as colds and flu, which are caused by viruses, gives bacteria more chances to become resistant. So does stopping a course of antibiotics early, sharing leftover medicine or buying antibiotics without a prescription. In many countries antibiotics are also used in large amounts in livestock and fish farming."],["Superbugs","Some bacteria have become resistant to several antibiotics at once. MRSA, a resistant form of Staphylococcus aureus, can cause serious skin, blood and lung infections. Drug-resistant forms of tuberculosis, gonorrhea and bacteria found in hospitals are especially worrying because few medicines remain that can treat them."],["The scale of the problem","A major study published in 2024 estimated that resistant bacterial infections directly caused about 1.14 million deaths in 2021 and played a part in nearly 5 million. Without action, deaths could rise substantially by 2050. The burden is heaviest in low- and middle-income countries."]],
k:[["Short name","AMR"],["Nickname for resistant germs","Superbugs"],["Deaths directly caused (2021)","About 1.14 million"],["First warning","Alexander Fleming, 1945"],["Main driver","Overuse and misuse of antimicrobials"]],
d:[["1928","Alexander Fleming discovers penicillin"],["1945","Fleming warns that misuse of penicillin could make bacteria resistant"],["1961","MRSA is first reported in Britain"],["2015","The World Health Organization adopts a global action plan on AMR"],["2016","The United Nations holds its first high-level meeting on AMR"],["2024","World leaders agree a new declaration on AMR at the United Nations"]],
s2:[["Fleming’s warning","When Alexander Fleming received the Nobel Prize in 1945 for discovering penicillin, he warned in his speech that people who took too little of the drug, or took it carelessly, could make bacteria resistant. Within a few years, resistant strains of bacteria were indeed found in hospitals. His warning is often quoted today."],["The empty pipeline","Few new types of antibiotics have been discovered since the 1980s. Developing them is expensive, and because new antibiotics are kept in reserve for resistant infections, companies earn little from them. Governments and charities are testing new ways to reward companies for developing antibiotics."],["New discoveries","Scientists are searching for new antibiotics in soil, oceans and even the human nose. In 2020 researchers used artificial intelligence to identify a new antibiotic compound, named halicin. Other approaches include viruses that attack bacteria, called phages, and vaccines that prevent infections in the first place."],["One Health","Resistance spreads among people, animals and the environment, so experts call for a One Health approach that links human medicine, veterinary care, farming and environmental protection. Many countries have restricted the use of antibiotics to make farm animals grow faster."],["AMR in the Philippines","The Philippines has a national action plan on antimicrobial resistance and a surveillance program that tracks resistant bacteria in hospitals. Health authorities remind the public that antibiotics should be bought only with a doctor’s prescription and taken exactly as directed, and campaigns during World AMR Awareness Week each November spread the message."],["What everyone can do","People can help slow resistance by using antibiotics only when prescribed, completing the full course as directed, never sharing or saving leftover antibiotics, washing hands, keeping vaccinations up to date and preparing food safely to prevent infections."]],
sp:["The pill that cannot cure a cold","Many people believe antibiotics will help them recover from a cold, cough or flu, and some ask doctors for them or buy them from a pharmacy. But colds and flu are caused by viruses, and antibiotics work only against bacteria. Taking them for a viral illness does not speed recovery; it may cause side effects such as diarrhea and allergic reactions, and it helps resistant bacteria spread. Doctors usually recommend rest, fluids and simple remedies for colds. Public health campaigns in many countries now use slogans reminding people that antibiotics are not for every illness."],
g:[["Antimicrobial","A medicine that kills or stops the growth of microbes"],["Antibiotic","A medicine that fights bacteria"],["Resistance","The ability of a germ to survive a medicine"],["Superbug","A germ resistant to several medicines"]],
f:["Antibiotics do not work on viruses.","MRSA is a well-known superbug.","Fleming warned about resistance in 1945."],
fun:"Some bacteria can share resistance genes with completely different kinds of bacteria, passing them around like recipes."});

ADDART({id:"wearables",t:"Wearable Technology",c:"Technology",i:"⌚",w:"Wearable technology",r:["smartphone","exercise","dataprivacy","aimedicine","sleep"],
p:[["Tech you wear","Wearable technology includes smartwatches, fitness trackers and other devices worn on the body."],["Tracking health","Wearables count steps, measure heart rate and track sleep."],["Benefits and worries","They can encourage healthy habits but raise questions about accuracy and privacy."]],
l:"Wearable technology, or wearables, means electronic devices worn on the body, such as smartwatches, fitness bands, smart rings, wireless earbuds and smart glasses. Most contain sensors that measure movement, heart rate, sleep and other body signals, and connect to smartphones to show the data in apps. Fitness trackers became popular in the late 2000s and smartwatches in the 2010s, and hundreds of millions of people now use them. Medical wearables, such as continuous glucose monitors for people with diabetes and devices that check heart rhythms, can help manage health conditions. Supporters say wearables encourage exercise and early detection of problems, while critics raise concerns about accuracy, privacy and constant self-monitoring.",
s:[["How wearables work","Wearables use tiny sensors. Accelerometers and gyroscopes detect movement to count steps and recognize activities. Optical sensors shine green or red light into the skin and measure the reflected light to estimate heart rate and blood oxygen. Data is sent by Bluetooth to a phone, where apps turn it into charts, goals and reminders."],["Fitness trackers","Clip-on and wristband trackers, such as those made by Fitbit from 2009, made counting steps popular. Many people set a goal of 10,000 steps a day, a number that came from a Japanese pedometer marketed in the 1960s. Research suggests that health benefits increase with more daily steps, with large gains even at around 7,000 steps."],["Smartwatches","Smartwatches combine fitness tracking with features of a phone, such as notifications, messages, maps and payments. The Apple Watch, launched in 2015, became the best-selling smartwatch, and companies such as Samsung, Garmin, Huawei and Xiaomi make many others. Some watches can record an electrocardiogram or detect a fall and call for help."],["Medical wearables","Continuous glucose monitors attach a tiny sensor under the skin to measure blood sugar every few minutes, helping people with diabetes avoid dangerous highs and lows. Other medical wearables monitor heart rhythm, blood pressure or breathing, allowing doctors to follow patients at home."]],
k:[["Examples","Smartwatches, fitness bands, smart rings"],["Early fitness tracker","Fitbit, 2009"],["Apple Watch launched","2015"],["Origin of 10,000 steps","A 1960s Japanese pedometer"],["Key sensors","Accelerometer, optical heart sensor"]],
d:[["1965","A Japanese pedometer called Manpo-kei, meaning 10,000 steps meter, goes on sale"],["2009","The first Fitbit tracker is released"],["2013","Pebble’s smartwatch raises millions through crowdfunding"],["2015","The Apple Watch is launched"],["2018","A smartwatch electrocardiogram feature is cleared by United States regulators"],["2020s","Smart rings and medical wearables grow in popularity"]],
s2:[["Do they make people healthier?","Studies show that wearables can motivate some people to walk more and sit less, at least for a time. Others lose interest after a few months, and the devices work best when combined with personal goals, social support or advice from health workers. Researchers continue to study long-term effects."],["Accuracy","Wearables are good at counting steps and measuring heart rate during rest, but less accurate for calories burned, sleep stages and some activities. Readings can be affected by skin tone, tattoos, movement and how tightly the device is worn. Experts advise treating the numbers as rough guides rather than medical measurements."],["Saving lives","Some smartwatch users have been alerted to irregular heart rhythms, such as atrial fibrillation, that they did not know they had, leading them to see a doctor. Fall detection has helped older people get help after accidents. Doctors note that such alerts can also cause false alarms and unnecessary worry."],["Privacy and data","Wearables collect personal information about health, location and daily routines. This data may be stored by companies, shared with partners or used for advertising and research. Privacy advocates warn about how such data could be used by employers, insurers or governments, and many countries have data protection laws, including the Philippines’ Data Privacy Act of 2012."],["Wearables at work and school","Some companies give workers wearables to track safety or productivity, and some schools use them in physical education. Supporters say this can prevent injuries and encourage fitness, while critics argue it can feel like surveillance and put pressure on people to meet targets."],["The future of wearables","Companies are developing smart glasses that show information in front of the eyes, clothing with built-in sensors, patches that analyze sweat, and devices that could measure blood pressure or blood sugar without needles. Artificial intelligence is being used to spot patterns in wearable data that may warn of illness early."]],
sp:["Where 10,000 steps came from","Many fitness apps suggest a goal of 10,000 steps a day, but the number did not come from a scientific study. In 1965, ahead of a wave of interest in fitness after the 1964 Tokyo Olympics, a Japanese company sold a pedometer called the Manpo-kei, which means 10,000 steps meter. The round number was easy to remember and became popular around the world. Decades later, scientists studied the link between daily steps and health. They found that walking more is linked to lower risks of heart disease and early death, with benefits beginning well below 10,000 steps, so every extra walk counts."],
g:[["Wearable","An electronic device worn on the body"],["Accelerometer","A sensor that detects movement"],["Smartwatch","A wristwatch with computer and phone features"],["Glucose monitor","A device that measures blood sugar"]],
f:["The 10,000-step goal began with a 1965 pedometer.","Smartwatches can record heart rhythms.","Wearables use light to measure heart rate."],
fun:"The green lights on the back of many smartwatches measure your pulse by detecting how much light is absorbed by blood flowing under your skin."});

ADDART({id:"genetherapy",t:"Gene Therapy",c:"Medicine",i:"🧬",w:"Gene therapy",r:["crispr","dna","genome","mrna","cancer"],
p:[["Fixing genes","Gene therapy treats disease by adding, replacing or editing genes inside a patient’s cells."],["New treatments","It has been approved for some inherited diseases, blindness and cancers."],["High costs and hard questions","Gene therapies can be very expensive and raise ethical debates."]],
l:"Gene therapy is a medical approach that treats or prevents disease by changing the genes inside a person’s cells. It may add a working copy of a faulty gene, switch off a harmful gene, or edit DNA directly using tools such as CRISPR. Genes are usually delivered using modified, harmless viruses that carry them into cells, or cells are taken from the patient, changed in a laboratory and put back. After decades of research and setbacks, gene therapies have been approved since the 2010s for certain inherited diseases, a form of inherited blindness, spinal muscular atrophy, sickle cell disease and some blood cancers. These treatments can transform lives but often cost millions of dollars, and editing genes raises important ethical questions.",
s:[["How gene therapy works","Many diseases are caused by a faulty gene that fails to make a needed protein. Gene therapy aims to fix the problem at its source. Scientists often use viruses that have been emptied of their harmful parts as delivery vehicles, called vectors, to carry a healthy gene into cells, where it begins producing the missing protein."],["Two main approaches","In one approach, the gene is delivered directly into the body, for example by an injection into the eye, the blood or the spinal fluid. In the other, doctors collect a patient’s own cells, such as blood stem cells or immune cells, change them in a laboratory, and return them to the patient."],["Gene editing","Newer techniques such as CRISPR let scientists cut and change DNA at precise locations. In 2023 regulators in Britain and the United States approved Casgevy, the first CRISPR-based treatment, for sickle cell disease and a related blood disorder. Newer methods called base editing and prime editing can change single letters of DNA."],["Cancer therapies","CAR-T therapy is a kind of gene therapy in which a patient’s immune cells, called T cells, are given a new gene that helps them recognize and attack cancer cells. Since its first approval in 2017, it has helped some patients with leukemia and lymphoma who had run out of other options."]],
k:[["First approved trial","1990, United States"],["First CRISPR therapy approved","Casgevy, 2023"],["Delivery method","Modified viruses (vectors)"],["Cancer gene therapy","CAR-T"],["Main concerns","Cost, safety, ethics"]],
d:[["1990","Ashanti DeSilva becomes the first person treated in an approved gene therapy trial"],["1999","The death of Jesse Gelsinger in a trial sets back the field"],["2012","The first gene therapy is approved in Europe"],["2017","A gene therapy for inherited blindness and the first CAR-T therapy are approved in the United States"],["2023","Casgevy becomes the first approved CRISPR therapy"],["2025","A baby receives the first personalized gene-editing treatment"]],
s2:[["The first patient","In September 1990 a four-year-old girl named Ashanti DeSilva, who had an inherited disease that left her almost without an immune system, became the first person treated in an approved gene therapy trial in the United States. Doctors added working copies of the missing gene to her white blood cells. Her condition improved, though she also continued other treatment."],["Setbacks","In 1999 an 18-year-old volunteer, Jesse Gelsinger, died from a severe immune reaction to the virus used in a gene therapy trial. In the early 2000s some children treated for an immune disorder developed leukemia. These tragedies led to stricter safety rules and slowed the field for years, before safer vectors brought new success."],["Restoring sight and strength","In 2017 United States regulators approved Luxturna, which treats a rare form of inherited blindness by delivering a gene to cells in the retina. Zolgensma, approved in 2019, treats spinal muscular atrophy, a disease that weakens the muscles of babies, with a single infusion; children treated early have learned to sit, crawl and walk."],["A personalized treatment","In 2025 doctors in the United States reported treating a baby named KJ Muldoon, born with a rare and dangerous liver disorder, using a base-editing therapy designed specifically for his genetic mutation. It was developed in a matter of months. Scientists hope such personalized treatments may one day help other patients with very rare diseases."],["The cost problem","Gene therapies are among the most expensive medicines ever sold, some costing two million dollars or more for a single treatment. Companies say prices reflect high development costs and the value of a one-time cure, while health systems and patient groups worry that few people, especially in poorer countries, can afford them. Ways to lower costs and share access are widely debated."],["Ethics and editing embryos","Most gene therapies change only the cells of the patient being treated, so changes are not passed to future children. Editing embryos, sperm or eggs would create changes that could be inherited. In 2018 the Chinese scientist He Jiankui announced he had edited the genes of twin babies, sparking worldwide condemnation; he was later jailed. Most countries ban or strictly limit inheritable gene editing."]],
sp:["Walking after Zolgensma","Spinal muscular atrophy, or SMA, is caused by a missing or faulty gene needed by the nerve cells that control muscles. In its most severe form, babies lose the ability to move, swallow and breathe, and many once died before the age of two. Zolgensma delivers a working copy of the gene with a single infusion into the bloodstream. When given early, many treated babies have reached milestones that would once have been impossible, such as sitting up, crawling and even walking. Some countries now test newborns for SMA so treatment can begin before symptoms appear, though the treatment’s very high price remains a challenge."],
g:[["Gene therapy","Treating disease by changing genes in a patient’s cells"],["Vector","A harmless virus or other carrier used to deliver genes"],["CAR-T","A therapy that changes immune cells to fight cancer"],["Germline editing","Gene changes that can be passed to future generations"]],
f:["The first approved gene therapy trial was in 1990.","Casgevy was the first approved CRISPR therapy.","Some gene therapies cost millions of dollars."],
fun:"Scientists turn viruses, usually known for causing illness, into tiny delivery trucks that carry healing genes into cells."});

ADDART({id:"influencers",t:"Social Media Influencers",c:"Media and Culture",i:"🤳",w:"Influencer marketing",r:["socialmedia","shortvideo","memes","misinformation","ecommerce"],
p:[["Online personalities","Influencers are people with large followings on social media."],["A new kind of job","Many earn money from advertising, brand deals and sponsorships."],["Influence and responsibility","Their power raises questions about honesty, misinformation and young audiences."]],
l:"A social media influencer is a person who has built a large following on platforms such as YouTube, Instagram, TikTok and Facebook, and who can shape the opinions and buying decisions of their audience. Influencers create content about topics such as beauty, fashion, food, travel, gaming, fitness, comedy, parenting, finance and everyday life. Many earn income from advertising revenue, sponsored posts, brand partnerships, live-stream gifts and selling their own products, making up a large part of what is called the creator economy. Influencers have become important in marketing, entertainment and politics. Their rise has also prompted debate about honesty in advertising, the spread of misinformation, pressure on young people and the wellbeing of creators themselves.",
s:[["From bloggers to influencers","In the 2000s, bloggers and early YouTubers gained followings by sharing opinions, tutorials and daily life online. As smartphones and platforms such as Instagram, launched in 2010, and TikTok, which spread internationally from 2017, grew, ordinary people could reach millions of viewers. Brands began paying popular creators to promote products."],["Types of influencers","Influencers are often grouped by audience size, from mega-influencers with millions of followers to micro- and nano-influencers with smaller but closely engaged communities. Some are celebrities who became influential online; others became famous only through social media. Virtual influencers, computer-generated characters run by companies, also exist."],["How influencers earn","Influencers may earn money from platform payments for views, sponsored posts, affiliate links that pay a share of sales, merchandise, paid subscriptions, live-stream gifts and appearances at events. Income varies hugely; a small number earn millions, while most creators earn little or nothing."],["Influencers in the Philippines","Filipinos are among the world’s heaviest users of social media, and the country has a large and lively creator scene. Filipino vloggers, content creators and live sellers promote food, travel, beauty and products, often mixing English and Filipino. Brands, businesses and even government agencies work with influencers to reach audiences."]],
k:[["Main platforms","YouTube, Instagram, TikTok, Facebook"],["Industry name","Creator economy"],["Smaller influencers","Micro- and nano-influencers"],["Common income","Brand deals and ad revenue"],["Disclosure tag","#ad or Paid partnership"]],
d:[["2005","YouTube is launched"],["2010","Instagram is launched"],["2017","TikTok begins spreading worldwide"],["2019","The United States Federal Trade Commission issues a guide for influencers on disclosure"],["2022","Influencers play a visible role in Philippine election campaigns"],["2024","MrBeast becomes the most-subscribed YouTube channel"]],
s2:[["Honest advertising","When influencers are paid to promote products, many countries require them to say so clearly, using labels such as #ad or paid partnership. Regulators worry that audiences, especially young people, may not realize they are watching advertising. Some influencers have been fined or criticized for promoting harmful or misleading products, including risky investments and unproven health remedies."],["Influencers and politics","Political campaigns around the world, including in the Philippines, have worked with influencers and vloggers to reach voters, especially young people. Supporters say this brings politics closer to ordinary citizens. Critics and researchers have raised concerns about undisclosed paid political content and the spread of false or misleading information, and have called for clearer rules."],["Effects on young people","Influencers can inspire young people to learn new skills, discover interests and feel part of a community. But constant exposure to edited images and seemingly perfect lives may affect self-esteem and body image for some. Many young people say they want to become influencers, and parents and teachers encourage them to think critically about what they see online."],["Kidfluencers","Some influencers are children, whose parents film and share their lives online. These channels can earn large sums, raising questions about children’s privacy, consent and earnings. Some places have passed laws requiring that part of a child influencer’s income be set aside for them."],["Behind the screen","Creating content constantly, responding to followers and keeping up with changing algorithms can be stressful. Many influencers have spoken about burnout, online harassment and the pressure to share their private lives. Some have taken breaks or left social media to protect their mental health."],["Live selling","Live selling, in which creators sell products during live video streams, is popular in the Philippines and other parts of Asia. Sellers demonstrate clothes, gadgets or food, chat with viewers and announce discounts in real time, and buyers comment to claim items. It has become an important source of income for many small businesses and individuals."]],
sp:["A day in the life of a creator","Behind a few minutes of polished video, an influencer may spend many hours planning ideas, filming, editing, writing captions, answering messages and studying which posts perform best. Some work alone with a phone and a ring light in a bedroom, while successful creators may hire editors, managers and camera operators. Many creators juggle content creation with school or another job. Because platform algorithms decide which posts are shown widely, income can rise and fall suddenly. For many young Filipinos, becoming a content creator has become a dream career, though only a few make it a full-time living."],
g:[["Influencer","A person who can affect the opinions of many followers online"],["Creator economy","The businesses and income built around online content creators"],["Sponsored post","Content that a brand pays a creator to make"],["Algorithm","The rules a platform uses to decide what content people see"]],
f:["Instagram launched in 2010.","Paid posts should be labeled as advertising.","Live selling is popular in the Philippines."],
fun:"Some popular influencers are not real people at all but computer-generated characters managed by companies."});

ADDART({id:"podcasts",t:"Podcasts",c:"Media and Culture",i:"🎙️",w:"Podcast",r:["radio","streaming","smartphone","internet","shortvideo"],
p:[["Shows on demand","A podcast is a series of audio or video episodes that people can listen to whenever they like."],["Anyone can make one","With a microphone and an internet connection, almost anyone can start a podcast."],["Huge variety","There are podcasts about news, true crime, comedy, history, science, sports and much more."]],
l:"A podcast is a series of spoken audio episodes, and sometimes video episodes, made available online so that listeners can download or stream them whenever they want. The word combines iPod, Apple’s portable music player, and broadcast, and was first suggested in 2004. Podcasts grew slowly at first, then boomed in the 2010s as smartphones made listening easy and hit shows attracted millions of fans. Today there are millions of podcasts covering every imaginable subject, from news and politics to comedy, true crime, education, religion, health and personal stories. Because they are cheap to make, podcasts allow independent creators as well as major media companies to reach audiences around the world.",
s:[["How podcasts work","Podcasts are published online with a special web feed, called RSS, that lists each new episode. Listeners subscribe using podcast apps, such as Apple Podcasts or Spotify, which automatically show new episodes. Many podcasts are also posted as videos on YouTube, where hosts are filmed while they talk."],["Popular formats","Some podcasts feature one or two hosts chatting about a topic, while others interview guests. Narrative podcasts tell a story over many episodes, like a radio documentary or drama. News podcasts summarize the day’s events, and educational podcasts teach languages, science or history."],["Making a podcast","A simple podcast needs a microphone, recording software and a place to publish episodes. Many creators start at home, recording with friends. Larger productions involve researchers, sound designers and producers. Podcasts earn money through advertisements read by the hosts, sponsorships, listener donations and paid subscriptions."],["Why people listen","People listen to podcasts while commuting, exercising, cooking, doing chores or working. Many say they enjoy the feeling of a personal conversation with hosts they trust, and the chance to explore subjects in more depth than short videos allow."]],
k:[["Name from","iPod and broadcast"],["Word first suggested","2004"],["Added to iTunes","2005"],["Boom began","Mid-2010s"],["Main platforms","Spotify, Apple Podcasts, YouTube"]],
d:[["2004","The word podcasting is suggested in a British newspaper"],["2005","Apple adds podcasts to its iTunes software"],["2005","New Oxford American Dictionary names podcast its word of the year"],["2014","The true crime podcast Serial becomes a global hit"],["2019","Spotify invests heavily in podcasts"],["2020s","Video podcasts on YouTube become one of the most popular ways to listen"]],
s2:[["Radio roots","Podcasts carry on a long tradition of radio storytelling, talk shows and drama. In the Philippines, radio dramas, comedy programs and commentary shows were hugely popular for decades, and many families gathered around the radio each evening. Some radio programs are now released as podcasts too."],["The Serial effect","In 2014 Serial, a podcast that reinvestigated a 1999 murder case in the United States over many episodes, became one of the first podcasts heard by millions. Its success inspired a wave of true crime and narrative podcasts and drew major media companies into podcasting."],["Podcasts in the Philippines","Filipino podcasts cover topics such as comedy, relationships, horror stories, business, faith, history and current affairs, often in Filipino, English or Taglish. Many are recorded on video and shared on YouTube and Facebook. Listening has grown with cheaper smartphones and mobile data."],["Video podcasts","Many podcasts are now filmed as well as recorded, with hosts and guests sitting around microphones. Clips from these shows spread widely on social media. This has blurred the line between podcasts, talk shows and online video."],["Influence and responsibility","Some podcast hosts have audiences larger than those of television networks and have interviewed leading politicians and celebrities. Supporters praise podcasts for allowing long, open conversations. Critics note that some podcasts spread misleading claims without fact-checking, and debates continue over whether platforms should remove harmful content."],["Learning by listening","Teachers and students use podcasts to learn languages, practice listening skills and explore subjects such as history and science. Some schools ask students to create their own podcasts as projects, building research, writing and speaking skills."]],
sp:["How the podcast got its name","In February 2004 the British journalist Ben Hammersley wrote an article in The Guardian newspaper about a new way of sharing online radio shows that people could download automatically to their computers and music players. Wondering what to call it, he listed several possibilities, including audioblogging, guerrilla media and podcasting. The last one stuck. Within a year, Apple added podcasts to iTunes, and in 2005 podcast was named word of the year by the New Oxford American Dictionary. Ironically, although the name came from the iPod, most people today listen to podcasts on smartphones."],
g:[["Podcast","A series of audio or video episodes available online"],["Episode","One part of a podcast series"],["RSS feed","A web feed that lists new episodes automatically"],["Host","The person who presents a podcast"]],
f:["The word podcast comes from iPod and broadcast.","Serial became a hit in 2014.","Podcasts can be audio or video."],
fun:"Although the word podcast comes from Apple’s iPod, the iPod itself was discontinued in 2022, while podcasts kept growing."});

;
/* data/space9.js */
// Space, more articles: Titan, Europa, light pollution, India's Chandrayaan missions and the Philippine Space Agency.
ADDART({id:"titan",t:"Titan",c:"Astronomy",i:"🟠",w:"Titan (moon)",r:["saturn","moons","astrobio","voyager","atmosphere"],
p:[["Saturn’s giant moon","Titan is the largest moon of Saturn and the second-largest moon in the solar system."],["A thick atmosphere","It is the only moon with a thick atmosphere."],["Lakes of methane","Titan has rivers, lakes and seas of liquid methane and ethane."]],
l:"Titan is the largest moon of Saturn and the second-largest moon in the solar system, after Jupiter’s Ganymede. It is bigger than the planet Mercury. Titan is the only moon known to have a thick atmosphere, made mostly of nitrogen, and the only world besides Earth known to have stable bodies of liquid on its surface. But Titan’s lakes and seas are filled with liquid methane and ethane rather than water, because its surface is about minus 180°C. Discovered in 1655 by Christiaan Huygens, Titan was explored by the Cassini spacecraft, which dropped the Huygens probe onto its surface in 2005. NASA’s Dragonfly mission plans to fly a drone-like lander there.",
s:[["Size and orbit","Titan is about 5,150 kilometers across, roughly 1.5 times the width of Earth’s Moon. It orbits Saturn once every 16 days, always keeping the same side facing the planet. It is made of a mixture of rock and water ice."],["A hazy atmosphere","Titan’s atmosphere is thicker than Earth’s, with a surface pressure about one and a half times as great. Sunlight breaks down methane high in the atmosphere, creating an orange smog of complex chemicals that hides the surface from ordinary telescopes."],["Methane weather","On Titan, methane plays the role that water plays on Earth. It forms clouds, falls as rain, carves river channels and collects in lakes and seas near the poles. The largest, Kraken Mare, is bigger than the Caspian Sea."],["A hidden ocean","Measurements by Cassini suggest that beneath Titan’s icy crust lies an ocean of salty liquid water. Scientists are interested in whether this ocean, or the chemistry on Titan’s surface, could support any form of life."]],
k:[["Planet","Saturn"],["Diameter","About 5,150 km"],["Discovered","1655, by Christiaan Huygens"],["Surface temperature","About −180°C"],["Atmosphere","Mostly nitrogen"]],
d:[["1655","Christiaan Huygens discovers Titan"],["1944","Gerard Kuiper detects methane in Titan’s atmosphere"],["1980","Voyager 1 flies past Titan"],["2004","The Cassini spacecraft arrives at Saturn"],["2005","The Huygens probe lands on Titan"],["2017","Cassini’s mission ends after many Titan flybys"]],
s2:[["The Huygens landing","On 14 January 2005, the European Huygens probe parachuted through Titan’s atmosphere for about two and a half hours and landed on a damp plain scattered with rounded pebbles of ice. It sent back pictures and measurements for over an hour. It was the first landing on a world in the outer solar system and remains the most distant landing ever made."],["Cassini’s discoveries","During 13 years orbiting Saturn, the Cassini spacecraft flew past Titan more than 120 times. Its radar mapped dunes, mountains, river valleys and the methane seas near the north pole. It also detected signs of the hidden underground ocean."],["Dunes and mountains","Wide belts of dunes, made not of sand but of grains of solid organic material, cover large areas near Titan’s equator. Mountains of water ice rise from the surface, and some scientists think features on Titan may be cryovolcanoes, which erupt icy slush instead of lava."],["Could Titan host life?","Titan has many of the ingredients that scientists think led to life on Earth, including complex carbon chemistry and liquid on the surface and below. But it is extremely cold. Some researchers have imagined strange life forms that could live in liquid methane, while others focus on the underground water ocean."],["The Dragonfly mission","NASA’s Dragonfly mission, planned for launch in 2028, will send a nuclear-powered rotorcraft, like a large drone, to Titan. Titan’s thick air and low gravity make flying easier than on Earth. Dragonfly is designed to hop between sites, studying the chemistry of the surface and searching for signs of the processes that may lead to life."],["Titan in imagination","Writers have long been fascinated by Titan. It has appeared in science fiction novels, films and games as a place for future human settlements. With its thick atmosphere, people there would not need pressure suits, only warm clothing and oxygen masks, though the extreme cold would be a great challenge."]],
sp:["Postcards from Titan","As the Huygens probe drifted down under its parachute in 2005, its cameras revealed a landscape that looked surprisingly familiar: dark channels branching like riverbeds down to what appeared to be a shoreline. After landing, its pictures showed rounded stones on the ground, smoothed by flowing liquid just like pebbles in an Earth stream. But the stones were made of water ice as hard as rock, and the liquid that shaped them was methane. Scientists described Titan as a strange mirror of Earth, with familiar landforms built from completely different materials."],
g:[["Moon","A natural satellite that orbits a planet"],["Methane","A gas, also called natural gas, which is liquid on Titan"],["Cryovolcano","A volcano that erupts icy materials instead of molten rock"],["Rotorcraft","An aircraft lifted by spinning blades, such as a helicopter"]],
f:["Titan is bigger than Mercury.","Its lakes are filled with liquid methane.","The Huygens probe landed there in 2005."],
fun:"Because Titan’s air is thick and its gravity is weak, a person there could fly by flapping wings strapped to their arms."});

ADDART({id:"europa",t:"Europa",c:"Astronomy",i:"🧊",w:"Europa (moon)",r:["jupiter","moons","astrobio","galileo","water"],
p:[["An icy moon","Europa is one of Jupiter’s four largest moons, covered by a shell of ice."],["A hidden ocean","Beneath the ice lies a vast ocean of salty water."],["Searching for life","Scientists consider it one of the best places to look for life beyond Earth."]],
l:"Europa is the fourth-largest moon of Jupiter and one of the four Galilean moons discovered by Galileo Galilei in 1610. Slightly smaller than Earth’s Moon, it has a bright, smooth surface of water ice crossed by long cracks and ridges. Strong evidence suggests that beneath this icy shell lies a global ocean of salty liquid water, possibly containing twice as much water as all of Earth’s oceans. Because life on Earth depends on liquid water, Europa is considered one of the most promising places in the solar system to search for life. NASA’s Europa Clipper spacecraft, launched in 2024, is on its way to study the moon in detail.",
s:[["Surface of ice","Europa’s surface is among the smoothest in the solar system, with few craters, which means it is geologically young. It is covered with a web of dark lines and ridges where the ice has cracked and shifted, and areas of jumbled ice blocks called chaos terrain, which may form where warmer water rises close to the surface."],["The ocean below","Measurements by the Galileo spacecraft showed that Europa disturbs Jupiter’s magnetic field in a way best explained by a layer of salty, electrically conducting water beneath the ice. The ice shell may be 15 to 25 kilometers thick, with an ocean perhaps 60 to 150 kilometers deep."],["Heated by tides","Europa is far from the Sun, but Jupiter’s strong gravity stretches and squeezes it as it orbits, heating its interior through friction. This tidal heating keeps the ocean from freezing and may power hydrothermal vents on the seafloor, like those that support life in Earth’s deep oceans."],["Ingredients for life","Life as we know it needs liquid water, a source of energy and certain chemical elements. Europa appears to have water and energy, and scientists hope to learn whether it also has the right chemistry. No evidence of life has been found, but Europa is a top target in the search."]],
k:[["Planet","Jupiter"],["Discovered","1610, by Galileo Galilei"],["Diameter","About 3,100 km"],["Surface","Water ice"],["Mission","Europa Clipper, launched 2024"]],
d:[["1610","Galileo Galilei discovers Europa and three other moons of Jupiter"],["1979","Voyager 1 and 2 photograph Europa’s cracked surface"],["1995","The Galileo spacecraft begins orbiting Jupiter"],["2000","Galileo data strongly suggest an ocean beneath the ice"],["2023","Europe launches the JUICE mission to Jupiter’s icy moons"],["2024","NASA launches Europa Clipper"]],
s2:[["Galileo’s moons","In January 1610 Galileo pointed his telescope at Jupiter and saw four small points of light moving around it. They were the first moons discovered orbiting another planet, and they showed that not everything in the sky circles Earth. The moons, Io, Europa, Ganymede and Callisto, are now called the Galilean moons."],["Water plumes?","Observations by the Hubble Space Telescope in 2012 and 2016 hinted that plumes of water vapor might sometimes burst from cracks in Europa’s ice. If so, a spacecraft flying through them could sample the ocean without drilling. The evidence remains uncertain, and Europa Clipper will search for plumes."],["Europa Clipper","Launched in October 2024, Europa Clipper is the largest spacecraft NASA has built for a planetary mission. Expected to reach Jupiter in 2030, it will fly past Europa dozens of times, using cameras, radar and other instruments to measure the ice shell, study the ocean and analyze the surface chemistry."],["JUICE","The European Space Agency’s Jupiter Icy Moons Explorer, called JUICE, launched in 2023. It will study Ganymede, Callisto and Europa and become the first spacecraft to orbit a moon of another planet, Ganymede. The two missions will complement each other."],["Protecting Europa","Because Europa might host life, space agencies take great care to avoid contaminating it with microbes from Earth. Spacecraft are carefully cleaned, and at the end of its mission the Galileo spacecraft was deliberately crashed into Jupiter in 2003 so that it could never hit Europa."],["A future lander","Scientists have proposed sending a lander to Europa to scoop up surface ice and test it for chemical signs of life. Further in the future, some have imagined robotic probes that could melt through the ice and explore the ocean below, though such missions would be extremely difficult."]],
sp:["An ocean bigger than Earth’s","Europa is smaller than our Moon, yet its hidden ocean may hold about twice as much water as all the oceans on Earth combined. On Earth, the deep ocean floor has hydrothermal vents, cracks where hot, mineral-rich water gushes out. There, in complete darkness, communities of tubeworms, clams, shrimp and bacteria thrive on chemical energy rather than sunlight. Scientists wonder whether similar vents might exist on Europa’s seafloor. If life could arise in such a place on Earth, perhaps it could also arise in the dark ocean beneath Europa’s ice."],
g:[["Galilean moons","The four largest moons of Jupiter, discovered by Galileo"],["Tidal heating","Heat produced inside a moon by a planet’s gravity squeezing it"],["Chaos terrain","Areas of broken, jumbled ice on Europa’s surface"],["Hydrothermal vent","A crack in the seafloor where heated water flows out"]],
f:["Europa was discovered in 1610.","It may have more water than Earth’s oceans.","Europa Clipper launched in 2024."],
fun:"Europa’s surface is so smooth that its tallest features rise only a few hundred meters."});

ADDART({id:"lightpollution",t:"Light Pollution",c:"Astronomy",i:"🌃",w:"Light pollution",r:["stars","constellations","megacities","seaturtle","sleep"],
p:[["Too much light","Light pollution is excessive or badly aimed artificial light at night."],["Losing the stars","It hides the stars and the Milky Way from most people on Earth."],["Effects on life","It disturbs animals, plants and human sleep, and wastes energy."]],
l:"Light pollution is the excessive, misdirected or unnecessary use of artificial light at night. Streetlights, buildings, billboards and vehicles send light up into the sky, where it scatters and creates a glow over cities called skyglow. As a result, most people in the world can no longer see a truly dark night sky, and about a third of humanity cannot see the Milky Way from where they live. Besides hiding the stars, light pollution confuses migrating birds and sea turtle hatchlings, disrupts insects, wastes energy and may affect human sleep and health. Unlike many pollutants, it can be reduced quickly with better lighting design.",
s:[["Types of light pollution","Skyglow is the brightening of the night sky over populated areas. Glare is excessive brightness that causes discomfort and makes it harder to see. Light trespass is light falling where it is not wanted, such as into bedrooms. Clutter is a confusing mass of bright lights, often seen along busy roads."],["Losing the night sky","A 2016 world atlas of artificial sky brightness found that about 80 percent of the world’s people live under light-polluted skies. In cities, only the brightest stars and planets are visible. Studies using observations by volunteers suggest the night sky has been getting brighter by several percent each year."],["Wildlife in danger","Many animals rely on natural darkness. Newly hatched sea turtles head toward the brightest horizon, normally the moonlit sea, but bright beachfront lights can lead them inland. Migrating birds are attracted to lit buildings and may collide with them, while artificial light draws insects away from their normal activities."],["People and health","Human bodies follow a daily rhythm controlled by light and darkness. Bright light at night, especially blue-rich light, can reduce the hormone melatonin and disturb sleep. Researchers are studying possible links between long-term light at night and health problems."]],
k:[["Main effect","Skyglow"],["People under light-polluted skies","About 80 percent"],["Cannot see the Milky Way","About a third of humanity"],["Key solution","Shielded, dimmer, warmer lighting"],["Protected dark places","Dark sky parks and reserves"]],
d:[["1879","Thomas Edison demonstrates a practical light bulb"],["1958","Flagstaff in the United States passes an early law to limit outdoor lighting"],["1988","The International Dark-Sky Association is founded"],["2001","The first world atlas of night sky brightness is published"],["2016","A new atlas finds 80 percent of people live under light-polluted skies"],["2023","A study finds the night sky brightening by nearly 10 percent a year"]],
s2:[["Better lighting","Light pollution can be reduced by using fixtures that point light downward rather than into the sky, lighting only what needs to be lit, using timers and motion sensors, and choosing warmer, less blue light. Such changes save energy and money while keeping streets safe."],["LEDs: a mixed blessing","Many cities have replaced older streetlights with LEDs, which use much less electricity. However, bright white LEDs contain more blue light, which scatters more in the atmosphere, and lower costs sometimes lead to more lighting overall. Experts recommend warmer LEDs and careful design."],["Dark sky places","Around the world, parks, reserves and communities have been certified as dark sky places for protecting their natural night skies. They attract astronomers and tourists who want to see the Milky Way, meteor showers and countless stars."],["Satellites in the sky","A newer concern is the growing number of satellites in low orbit, such as those providing internet service. Sunlight reflecting off them creates moving streaks in telescope images. Astronomers are working with companies to make satellites less reflective, and the issue is debated as satellite networks grow."],["Light pollution in the Philippines","Metro Manila and other large cities glow brightly at night, and few residents can see more than a handful of stars. Astronomy groups organize stargazing trips to darker provinces and islands, and conservation groups ask beach resorts to limit lights during sea turtle nesting seasons to protect hatchlings."],["Seeing the stars again","During large blackouts, such as the one after an earthquake in Los Angeles in 1994, many city residents saw the Milky Way for the first time. Some reportedly called emergency services, alarmed by the strange glowing cloud in the sky. Such stories show how much of the night sky city dwellers have lost."]],
sp:["The turtles that follow the light","On beaches across the tropics, including those in the Philippines, sea turtles crawl ashore at night to lay their eggs in the sand. About two months later, the hatchlings dig their way out, usually after dark, and scramble toward the sea. They find it by heading for the brightest, lowest horizon, which on a natural beach is the open sea reflecting moonlight and starlight. Bright lights from hotels, roads and houses can confuse them, drawing them inland, where they may be eaten or die from exhaustion. Conservation volunteers turn off lights, use turtle-friendly amber lamps and guide lost hatchlings back toward the waves."],
g:[["Light pollution","Excessive or misdirected artificial light at night"],["Skyglow","The glow of the night sky over populated areas"],["Glare","Excessive brightness that causes discomfort"],["Melatonin","A hormone that helps regulate sleep"]],
f:["About 80 percent of people live under light-polluted skies.","Bright lights confuse sea turtle hatchlings.","Downward-facing lights reduce skyglow."],
fun:"From a truly dark place, the human eye can see a few thousand stars at once; from a big city, often only a few dozen."});

ADDART({id:"chandrayaan",t:"Chandrayaan Missions",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🚀",w:"Chandrayaan programme",r:["moon","indiacountry","spaceagencies","artemis","marsrovers"],
p:[["India to the Moon","Chandrayaan is the name of India’s missions to explore the Moon."],["Water discovered","Chandrayaan-1 helped discover water molecules on the Moon in 2009."],["A historic landing","In 2023 Chandrayaan-3 made India the first country to land near the Moon’s south pole."]],
l:"The Chandrayaan program is a series of lunar exploration missions by the Indian Space Research Organisation, ISRO. The name means moon craft in Sanskrit. Chandrayaan-1, launched in 2008, orbited the Moon and carried a NASA instrument that helped confirm the presence of water molecules on the lunar surface. Chandrayaan-2, launched in 2019, placed an orbiter around the Moon, but its lander crashed during its descent. On 23 August 2023, Chandrayaan-3 successfully landed near the Moon’s south pole, making India the fourth country to soft-land on the Moon and the first to land in that region. The missions were achieved at relatively low cost and became a source of great national pride.",
s:[["Chandrayaan-1","Launched on 22 October 2008, Chandrayaan-1 orbited the Moon for about ten months. It released an impact probe that crashed near the south pole, and its instruments mapped the surface. NASA’s Moon Mineralogy Mapper on board detected signs of water and hydroxyl molecules spread across the Moon, a major discovery announced in 2009."],["Chandrayaan-2","Chandrayaan-2 launched on 22 July 2019 with an orbiter, a lander named Vikram and a small rover. In September 2019, contact with Vikram was lost moments before touchdown and it crashed. The orbiter, however, continued to work successfully, mapping the Moon in high detail."],["Chandrayaan-3","Chandrayaan-3 launched on 14 July 2023 with an improved lander, again named Vikram, and a rover called Pragyan. On 23 August it touched down near the south pole. The six-wheeled rover drove about 100 meters, measuring the soil and confirming the presence of sulfur and other elements."],["Why the south pole","The Moon’s south pole has craters where sunlight never reaches, which may contain ice that has survived for billions of years. This ice could be studied for clues to the history of the solar system and might one day supply water and fuel for astronauts. Several countries are planning missions there."]],
k:[["Agency","Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO)"],["Name meaning","Moon craft"],["First mission","Chandrayaan-1, 2008"],["Historic landing","23 August 2023"],["Lander and rover","Vikram and Pragyan"]],
d:[["1969","The Indian Space Research Organisation is founded"],["2008","Chandrayaan-1 is launched"],["2009","Chandrayaan-1 data help confirm water molecules on the Moon"],["2019","Chandrayaan-2’s lander crashes, but its orbiter succeeds"],["2023","Chandrayaan-3 lands near the Moon’s south pole"],["2024","India celebrates its first National Space Day on 23 August"]],
s2:[["India’s space program","India began its space program in the 1960s, launching small rockets from a fishing village in Kerala. Its pioneers, including Vikram Sarabhai, after whom the landers are named, aimed to use space technology for national development, such as weather forecasting, communications and mapping. India launched its first satellite, Aryabhata, in 1975 with Soviet help."],["Low cost, high achievement","Chandrayaan-3 was reported to cost about 75 million US dollars, less than the budget of some Hollywood space films. ISRO is known for designing missions efficiently, reusing proven technology and using clever flight paths. Supporters see this as a model for developing countries, while critics debate whether space spending should take priority over other needs."],["A nation watches","The Chandrayaan-3 landing was broadcast live, and millions of people across India watched in homes, schools and public squares. When the lander touched down, celebrations broke out across the country. The landing site was named Shiv Shakti Point, and 23 August was declared National Space Day."],["Learning from failure","After Chandrayaan-2’s lander crashed, ISRO studied what had gone wrong and redesigned the lander for Chandrayaan-3 with stronger legs, more fuel, extra sensors and a larger landing area. The success four years later showed the value of learning from setbacks."],["Mars and beyond","In 2014 India’s Mars Orbiter Mission, known as Mangalyaan, reached Mars on its first attempt, making India the first Asian nation to do so. In 2023 India launched Aditya-L1 to study the Sun. India is also developing Gaganyaan, a program to send its own astronauts into space."],["Future Moon missions","India has announced plans for further lunar missions, including one to bring Moon samples back to Earth and a joint mission with Japan to explore the south pole. These missions form part of a wider international return to the Moon involving the United States, China, Japan and others."]],
sp:["Fifteen minutes of terror","Landing on the Moon is extremely difficult, because there is no air to slow a spacecraft with parachutes and every move must be controlled by rocket engines and onboard computers. ISRO engineers called the final descent the fifteen minutes of terror. On 23 August 2023, as Vikram slowed from thousands of kilometers per hour, scanned the ground for hazards and gently touched down, engineers in the control room in Bengaluru burst into cheers and hugs. Prime Minister Narendra Modi, watching from South Africa, congratulated the team live, saying India was now on the Moon."],
g:[["ISRO","Indian Space Research Organisation, India’s space agency"],["Lander","A spacecraft designed to touch down on a surface"],["Rover","A vehicle that moves across the surface of another world"],["Soft landing","A controlled landing without crashing"]],
f:["Chandrayaan means moon craft.","Chandrayaan-3 landed in 2023.","India was the first to land near the Moon’s south pole."],
fun:"The landers on Chandrayaan-2 and Chandrayaan-3 were both named Vikram, after Vikram Sarabhai, the father of India’s space program."});

ADDART({id:"philsa",t:"The Philippine Space Agency",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🛰️",w:"Philippine Space Agency",r:["sat","spaceagencies","phtyph","gps","disasterprep"],
p:[["The country’s space agency","The Philippine Space Agency, or PhilSA, leads the country’s space program."],["Filipino-made satellites","Filipino engineers have built satellites such as Diwata-1 and Maya-1."],["Space for development","Satellites help with disaster response, farming, mapping and protecting seas."]],
l:"The Philippine Space Agency, known as PhilSA, is the government agency responsible for the country’s space science and technology programs. It was created by the Philippine Space Act, signed into law on 8 August 2019. Even before PhilSA was formed, Filipino scientists and engineers, working with universities in Japan through a government program, built the country’s first microsatellite, Diwata-1, launched in 2016, followed by Diwata-2 and the tiny cube satellites of the Maya series. PhilSA uses satellite data to help monitor typhoons, floods and crops, map land and seas, and support national security. It also trains young scientists and promotes public interest in space.",
s:[["Creating PhilSA","For decades the Philippines relied on foreign satellites for weather images, communication and mapping. Scientists argued that a national space agency would help the country make better use of space technology. The Philippine Space Act of 2019 created PhilSA and set out a national space policy covering security, disaster management, research, industry and education."],["Diwata-1","Diwata-1, whose name comes from the Filipino word for a fairy or spirit, was built by Filipino engineers with Tohoku and Hokkaido universities in Japan. About the size of a small suitcase, it was released into orbit from the International Space Station in April 2016. Its cameras photographed the Philippines to help monitor weather, vegetation and coasts."],["The Maya cubesats","Maya-1, a cube satellite only 10 centimeters on each side, was built by Filipino students in Japan and deployed in 2018. It was followed by Maya-2 and by Maya-5 and Maya-6, the first cube satellites built entirely in the Philippines, deployed in 2023. These small satellites give students and engineers hands-on experience in building spacecraft."],["Using satellite data","PhilSA processes images from Filipino and foreign satellites to map floods after typhoons, track oil spills, monitor ships in Philippine waters, measure crop growth and detect changes in forests and coastlines. This information is shared with disaster agencies, local governments and researchers."]],
k:[["Created by","Philippine Space Act, 2019"],["Headquarters set by law","New Clark City, Tarlac"],["First microsatellite","Diwata-1, 2016"],["First Philippine-built cubesats","Maya-5 and Maya-6, 2023"],["Major planned satellite","MULA"]],
d:[["1997","Agila-2, the first satellite owned by a Philippine company, is launched"],["2016","Diwata-1 is deployed from the International Space Station"],["2018","Diwata-2 is launched and Maya-1 is deployed"],["2019","The Philippine Space Act creates PhilSA"],["2021","Maya-2 is deployed"],["2023","Maya-5 and Maya-6, built in the Philippines, are deployed"]],
s2:[["Satellites before PhilSA","In 1997 a Philippine telecommunications company launched Agila-2, a large communications satellite, from China. It carried television and telephone signals across Asia. For the next two decades, however, the country had no satellites of its own for observing the Earth."],["MULA","PhilSA has been developing a larger Earth-observation satellite called MULA, short for Multispectral Unit for Land Assessment, built with a British company. It is designed to take detailed images in many colors of light to help monitor farms, forests, coasts and disasters. Its launch has been postponed several times and, as of 2026, was planned for 2027."],["Space and national security","Satellite images help the Philippines monitor its large maritime territory, including the West Philippine Sea. PhilSA has used satellite data to track ships and to document changes to reefs. Supporters say this improves the country’s awareness of its seas, while the issue is part of wider regional tensions."],["Training the next generation","PhilSA and partner universities run scholarships and training programs in space science and engineering. Filipino students have built satellites in Japan, joined international space competitions and worked with foreign space agencies. Programs in schools aim to inspire young Filipinos to pursue careers in science and technology."],["International cooperation","The Philippines works with space agencies and organizations in Japan, the United States, Europe, the United Kingdom and Southeast Asia. Cooperation includes sharing satellite data, training, joint satellite projects and participation in regional disaster-monitoring networks."],["Why space matters for an archipelago","As a country of thousands of islands in the path of typhoons, the Philippines benefits greatly from space technology. Satellites provide weather forecasts, communication to remote islands, navigation for ships and fishermen, and quick maps after disasters. Supporters argue that investing in space helps the country solve problems on the ground."]],
sp:["A satellite from the Space Station","On 27 April 2016, astronauts aboard the International Space Station released Diwata-1 into orbit using a special launcher attached to the station’s Japanese module. As it drifted away, Filipino engineers on the ground celebrated a milestone years in the making. The young team had spent many months in Japan learning to design and build every part of the satellite. Over its life in orbit, Diwata-1 took thousands of images of the Philippines before it re-entered the atmosphere in 2020. Many of its builders went on to lead the country’s growing space program."],
g:[["PhilSA","Philippine Space Agency"],["Microsatellite","A small satellite, usually weighing between 10 and 100 kg"],["Cubesat","A tiny satellite built from cubes about 10 cm on each side"],["Earth observation","Studying Earth from space using satellites"]],
f:["PhilSA was created in 2019.","Diwata-1 was the first Philippine microsatellite.","Maya-5 and Maya-6 were built in the Philippines."],
fun:"Diwata-1 was launched from the International Space Station, released by astronauts using a special launcher on the station’s Japanese module."});

