/* data/body6.js */
// The Human Body, more articles: touch, reflexes, sun safety, stroke and asthma.
ADDART({id:"touch",t:"The Sense of Touch",c:"Human Body",i:"✋",w:"Somatosensory system",r:["skin","nervous","brain","pain","smelltaste"],
p:[["Feeling the world","Touch lets us feel pressure, vibration, texture, heat, cold and pain."],["Tiny sensors","Millions of tiny sensors in the skin send signals to the brain."],["Fingertips and lips","Some body parts, such as the fingertips and lips, are especially sensitive."]],
l:"Touch is the sense that allows us to feel pressure, vibration, texture, temperature and pain through the skin. It is part of a larger system, called the somatosensory system, that also tells us where our body parts are and how they are moving. The skin contains millions of sensory receptors of different kinds, each responding to particular sensations. Their signals travel along nerves to the spinal cord and brain, which interpret them as a gentle stroke, a sharp pinch, a hot cup or a rough surface. Touch helps us explore and handle objects, protects us from injury and plays a vital role in emotional bonds between people.",
s:[["Receptors in the skin","Different receptors detect different things. Some respond to light touch and fine texture, others to deep pressure or vibration, and others to stretching of the skin. Separate receptors sense warmth and cold, and nociceptors detect potential harm, producing the feeling of pain."],["Sensitive places","Receptors are not spread evenly. The fingertips, lips and tongue have many receptors close together, so they can detect tiny details. The back has far fewer. A simple test shows this: two pencil points pressed close together feel like one point on the back but two separate points on a fingertip."],["From skin to brain","Touch signals travel along nerve fibers into the spinal cord and up to the brain. They arrive in a strip of the brain called the somatosensory cortex, where each part of the body has its own area. Body parts with more receptors, such as the hands and lips, have larger areas."],["Knowing where your body is","Besides touch on the skin, sensors in muscles, tendons and joints tell the brain where the arms and legs are and how they are moving, a sense called proprioception. It lets people touch their nose with their eyes closed or walk without looking at their feet."]],
k:[["Sense organ","Skin"],["Brain area","Somatosensory cortex"],["Most sensitive areas","Fingertips, lips, tongue"],["Pain sensors","Nociceptors"],["Nobel Prize for touch and temperature sensors","2021"]],
d:[["1824","Louis Braille creates his raised-dot reading system"],["1834","Ernst Weber begins measuring the sensitivity of the skin"],["1937","Wilder Penfield maps body areas in the brain"],["1950s","Studies by Harry Harlow show the importance of comforting touch for young monkeys"],["1997","The heat and chili sensor TRPV1 is identified"],["2021","David Julius and Ardem Patapoutian win the Nobel Prize for discovering touch and temperature receptors"]],
s2:[["Reading with fingers","Because fingertips are so sensitive, they can read. In 1824 a blind French teenager, Louis Braille, developed a system of raised dots that represent letters and numbers. Braille is now used around the world, including in Filipino and other Philippine languages, allowing blind people to read books, signs and elevator buttons."],["The map in the brain","In the 1930s the Canadian neurosurgeon Wilder Penfield gently stimulated the brains of awake patients during surgery and asked what they felt. He mapped which part of the somatosensory cortex corresponds to each body part. Drawings of a little person with huge hands and lips, called a homunculus, show how much brain space each part receives."],["Hot peppers and cool mint","In 1997 the scientist David Julius and his team found a receptor in nerve cells, now called TRPV1, that responds both to heat and to capsaicin, the chemical that makes chili peppers taste hot. A related receptor responds to cold and menthol, which is why mint feels cool. Ardem Patapoutian discovered the Piezo receptors that sense pressure. The two shared the 2021 Nobel Prize."],["The power of a hug","Gentle, caring touch, such as a hug, holding hands or a parent cradling a baby, can calm the body, lower stress and strengthen bonds. Special nerve fibers in hairy skin respond best to slow stroking. Hospitals encourage skin-to-skin contact between newborn babies and their parents, sometimes called kangaroo care."],["When touch goes wrong","Damage to nerves from diabetes, injury or disease can cause numbness, tingling or burning pain. People who lose feeling in their feet may not notice cuts or blisters, so doctors advise them to check their feet daily. Some people are born unable to feel pain, which is dangerous because they cannot sense injuries."],["Touch and technology","Engineers are building robots and prosthetic hands with artificial skin that can sense pressure, so that people using them can feel what they grip. Phones and game controllers use vibrations, called haptic feedback, to make touchscreens and games feel more realistic."]],
sp:["The two-point test","Try this with a friend: close your eyes while your friend gently touches your skin with either one or two pencil tips held close together. On your fingertip you can probably tell when there are two points even if they are only a few millimeters apart. On your forearm they need to be much farther apart, and on your back they may need to be several centimeters apart before you can feel two separate points. The test, first used by the German scientist Ernst Weber in the 1830s, shows how densely packed touch receptors are in different parts of the body."],
g:[["Somatosensory system","The body system that senses touch, temperature, pain and position"],["Receptor","A sensor that responds to a particular stimulus"],["Proprioception","The sense of the position and movement of body parts"],["Haptic","Relating to the sense of touch"]],
f:["Fingertips are among the most sensitive parts of the body.","Braille lets people read with their fingers.","A chili receptor also senses heat."],
fun:"Chili peppers feel hot because their chemical capsaicin triggers the same receptor that senses real heat."});

ADDART({id:"reflexes",t:"Reflexes",c:"Human Body",i:"🦵",w:"Reflex",r:["nervous","spine","brain","muscles","eye"],
p:[["Automatic actions","A reflex is a fast, automatic response to something, without thinking."],["Protecting the body","Reflexes such as blinking and pulling away from heat protect us from harm."],["Baby reflexes","Newborn babies have special reflexes that fade as they grow."]],
l:"A reflex is an automatic, rapid response to a stimulus that happens without conscious thought. Examples include blinking when something flies toward the eyes, pulling a hand away from a hot stove, coughing when something irritates the throat and the knee-jerk that occurs when a doctor taps just below the kneecap. Many reflexes are controlled by the spinal cord or brainstem rather than the thinking parts of the brain, which makes them extremely fast. Reflexes protect the body from injury, help keep balance and posture, and control vital processes such as breathing and swallowing. Doctors test reflexes to check how well the nervous system is working.",
s:[["The reflex arc","The pathway of a simple reflex is called a reflex arc. A sensor detects a stimulus, such as heat on the skin, and sends a signal along a sensory nerve to the spinal cord. There, the signal passes, sometimes through a connecting nerve cell, to a motor nerve that makes a muscle contract. The message reaches the brain only afterward, which is why we often feel pain a moment after pulling away."],["Protective reflexes","Many reflexes protect us. Blinking shields the eyes, coughing and sneezing clear the airways, the gag reflex prevents choking, and the withdrawal reflex pulls a limb away from pain. The pupils of the eyes shrink automatically in bright light to protect the retina."],["The knee-jerk","When a doctor taps the tendon below the kneecap, the thigh muscle is stretched slightly. Sensors in the muscle send a signal to the spinal cord, which immediately tells the muscle to contract, so the lower leg kicks forward. This stretch reflex helps us keep our posture and balance without thinking."],["Reflexes and reactions","A reflex is different from a reaction. Catching a ball or braking a bicycle requires the brain to decide what to do, which takes longer. Reflexes are faster because they skip conscious thought, often taking only a few hundredths of a second."]],
k:[["Pathway","Reflex arc"],["Controlled mainly by","Spinal cord and brainstem"],["Famous example","Knee-jerk reflex"],["Nobel Prize for reflex research","Charles Sherrington, 1932"],["Learned reflexes studied by","Ivan Pavlov"]],
d:[["1664","René Descartes’s description of an automatic withdrawal from fire is published"],["1875","Doctors begin using the knee-jerk test to check the nervous system"],["1897","Ivan Pavlov begins studying digestive reflexes in dogs"],["1904","Pavlov receives the Nobel Prize for work on digestion"],["1906","Charles Sherrington publishes his landmark book on reflexes"],["1932","Sherrington shares the Nobel Prize for his work on nerve cells and reflexes"]],
s2:[["Descartes and the fire","The French philosopher René Descartes, in the 1600s, described how a person automatically pulls a foot away from fire, imagining that the heat tugged a thread running to the brain, which opened a valve to move the muscles. His explanation was wrong in its details, but the idea of an automatic pathway was an early version of the reflex."],["Sherrington’s discoveries","The British scientist Charles Sherrington studied reflexes in detail around 1900. He showed how muscles work in coordinated pairs during reflexes, one contracting while its partner relaxes, and introduced the word synapse for the junction between nerve cells. He shared the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 1932."],["Pavlov’s dogs","The Russian scientist Ivan Pavlov noticed that dogs began to drool not only when food arrived but when they heard sounds that usually came before feeding. He showed that animals can learn new reflexes, called conditioned reflexes, by linking a signal with a reward. His work greatly influenced psychology and the study of learning."],["Newborn reflexes","Babies are born with reflexes that help them survive. The rooting reflex makes a baby turn toward a touch on the cheek and search for milk, and the grasping reflex makes it grip a finger tightly. The startle, or Moro, reflex makes a baby fling out its arms at a sudden noise. Most of these fade within months, and doctors check them to monitor development."],["Testing reflexes","Doctors use a small rubber hammer to test reflexes at the knee, ankle and elbow, and shine a light to test the pupils. Reflexes that are too weak, too strong or uneven between the two sides can be signs of nerve damage or other problems that need further investigation."],["Reflexes in sport","Athletes such as goalkeepers, badminton players and boxers seem to have lightning reflexes, but much of their speed comes from training their brains to recognize patterns and predict what will happen. Practice can make reactions faster and more automatic, though true reflexes stay largely the same."]],
sp:["The yawn and the sneeze","Some reflexes are surprising. Sneezing can be triggered not only by dust or pepper but, in some people, by suddenly looking at bright sunlight, an inherited trait called the photic sneeze reflex. Yawning is partly a reflex, too, and it is famously contagious: seeing, hearing or even reading about yawning can make people yawn. Scientists are still not sure why we yawn or why it spreads, though theories include cooling the brain and signaling tiredness to others. Did you yawn while reading this?"],
g:[["Reflex","A fast, automatic response to a stimulus"],["Reflex arc","The nerve pathway of a reflex"],["Stimulus","Something that causes a response"],["Conditioned reflex","A learned response to a signal"]],
f:["Many reflexes are controlled by the spinal cord.","Blinking is a protective reflex.","Babies grip fingers by reflex."],
fun:"Some people sneeze when they suddenly look at bright sunlight, an inherited reflex nicknamed the ACHOO syndrome."});

ADDART({id:"sunsafety",t:"Sun Safety",c:"Health",i:"🧴",w:"Sunscreen",r:["skin","cancer","extremeheat","light","eye"],
p:[["Sunlight and health","Sunlight helps the body make vitamin D, but too much can harm the skin and eyes."],["Ultraviolet rays","Invisible ultraviolet, or UV, rays cause sunburn and skin damage."],["Staying protected","Shade, clothing, hats, sunglasses and sunscreen protect against UV."]],
l:"Sun safety means protecting the skin and eyes from harmful ultraviolet, or UV, radiation from the Sun. UV rays cannot be seen or felt, but they can burn the skin within minutes on a sunny day, cause premature aging and wrinkles, damage the eyes and raise the risk of skin cancer. Some sunlight is helpful, because it lets the skin make vitamin D, which keeps bones healthy. In tropical countries such as the Philippines, UV levels are often very high or extreme for much of the year. Simple habits, such as seeking shade at midday, wearing hats and protective clothing, using sunglasses and applying sunscreen, greatly reduce the risks.",
s:[["Types of UV rays","Sunlight contains two main types of UV that reach the ground: UVA, which penetrates deep into the skin and causes aging, and UVB, which mainly causes sunburn. Both can damage the DNA in skin cells. A third type, UVC, is blocked by the ozone layer."],["The UV Index","The UV Index is a scale that shows how strong the Sun’s UV rays are on a given day. Values of 1 to 2 are low, while 11 or more are extreme. In the Philippines, the UV Index often reaches very high or extreme levels around midday, and weather services publish daily forecasts."],["Sunburn","Sunburn happens when UV rays damage skin cells, causing redness, pain and sometimes blisters. It can occur even on cloudy days, because most UV passes through clouds. Water, sand and concrete reflect UV and increase exposure, which is why people often burn at the beach or pool."],["Skin cancer","Repeated sun exposure and sunburns raise the risk of skin cancer, including melanoma, the most dangerous type. People with fair skin are at greater risk, but people with darker skin can also get skin cancer and suffer sun damage. Changes in moles or new spots on the skin should be checked by a doctor."]],
k:[["Harmful rays","Ultraviolet (UVA and UVB)"],["Extreme UV Index","11 or higher"],["Sunscreen strength measure","SPF"],["Strongest sun","About 10 a.m. to 4 p.m."],["Benefit of sunlight","Vitamin D"]],
d:[["1930s","Early sunscreens are developed"],["1974","The sun protection factor, or SPF, becomes widely used"],["1985","Scientists discover the ozone hole over Antarctica"],["1987","The Montreal Protocol is agreed to protect the ozone layer"],["1992","Canada introduces the UV Index"],["2002","The World Health Organization publishes a global guide to the UV Index"]],
s2:[["How sunscreen works","Sunscreens contain ingredients that absorb or reflect UV rays before they damage the skin. SPF measures protection against UVB; SPF 30 blocks about 97 percent of UVB rays when applied correctly. Broad-spectrum sunscreens also protect against UVA. Experts recommend applying enough, about a teaspoon for the face and neck, and reapplying every two hours and after swimming or sweating."],["The payong habit","In the Philippines, many people carry a payong, or umbrella, not only for rain but for shade from the Sun, a sensible habit in a tropical climate. Hats, long sleeves and shaded walkways also help. Health authorities recommend staying in the shade during the hours of strongest sunlight, roughly 10 in the morning to 4 in the afternoon."],["Protecting the eyes","UV rays can damage the eyes, contributing to cataracts, which cloud the lens, and growths on the eye’s surface called pterygium, common among people who work outdoors in sunny places such as fishermen and farmers. Sunglasses labeled as blocking UV and wide-brimmed hats protect the eyes."],["Vitamin D","Sunlight on the skin helps the body make vitamin D, needed for strong bones and teeth. Short periods of sun exposure are usually enough, especially in the tropics. Vitamin D is also found in fish, eggs and fortified foods. Health experts advise balancing the benefits of some sunlight with the need to avoid sunburn."],["Children and the sun","Children’s skin is more easily damaged by the Sun, and sunburns in childhood raise the risk of skin cancer later in life. Schools in some countries have no hat, no play rules for outdoor breaks, and many encourage shade structures in playgrounds. Babies under six months should be kept out of direct sun."],["The ozone layer","The ozone layer high in the atmosphere absorbs most harmful UV rays. In the 1980s scientists discovered that chemicals used in refrigerators and spray cans were thinning it. The Montreal Protocol of 1987 phased out these chemicals, and the ozone layer is slowly recovering, a success story often cited for international cooperation."]],
sp:["Slip, slop, slap","In 1981 Australia, which has one of the highest skin cancer rates in the world, launched a famous public health campaign featuring a cartoon seagull named Sid. Its catchy slogan urged people to slip on a shirt, slop on sunscreen and slap on a hat. Later versions added seek shade and slide on sunglasses. The campaign became part of everyday life in Australia, changing habits at beaches and schools. Studies later found that skin cancer rates among younger Australians began to fall, and other countries have adapted the idea for their own sun safety campaigns."],
g:[["Ultraviolet","Invisible rays from the Sun that can harm skin and eyes"],["UV Index","A scale showing the strength of UV rays"],["SPF","Sun protection factor, a measure of sunscreen strength against UVB"],["Melanoma","A dangerous type of skin cancer"]],
f:["UV rays pass through clouds.","SPF 30 blocks about 97 percent of UVB.","Sunlight helps the body make vitamin D."],
fun:"Snow, sand and water reflect UV rays, so you can get sunburned even while sitting in the shade at the beach."});

ADDART({id:"stroke",t:"Stroke",c:"Disease and Medicine",i:"🧠",w:"Stroke",r:["brain","heartdisease","blood","exercise","firstaid"],
p:[["A brain attack","A stroke happens when blood flow to part of the brain is blocked or a blood vessel bursts."],["Act fast","Quick treatment can save brain cells and lives."],["Preventable","Controlling blood pressure and healthy habits greatly reduce the risk."]],
l:"A stroke occurs when the blood supply to part of the brain is suddenly cut off, either because a blood vessel is blocked by a clot, called an ischemic stroke, or because a blood vessel bursts and bleeds into or around the brain, called a hemorrhagic stroke. Without oxygen, brain cells begin to die within minutes. Strokes can cause sudden weakness or numbness on one side of the body, trouble speaking or seeing, confusion and loss of balance. Stroke is one of the leading causes of death and disability in the world and in the Philippines. Fast emergency treatment can greatly improve recovery, and most strokes can be prevented by controlling high blood pressure and living a healthy lifestyle.",
s:[["Types of stroke","Most strokes are ischemic, caused by a clot blocking an artery that supplies the brain. Hemorrhagic strokes, caused by bleeding, are less common but often more severe. A transient ischemic attack, or mini-stroke, causes brief symptoms that go away, but it is a serious warning sign that a major stroke may follow."],["Warning signs","The acronym BE FAST helps people recognize a stroke: Balance loss, Eyes losing vision, Face drooping on one side, Arm weakness, Speech difficulty, and Time to call emergency services. In the Philippines, campaigns also teach the signs in Filipino so families can act quickly."],["Why speed matters","During a stroke, millions of brain cells can die every minute. Doctors say time is brain. For ischemic strokes, clot-dissolving medicine given within a few hours, or a procedure to pull out the clot, can restore blood flow and reduce lasting damage. Patients should be taken to hospital immediately."],["Risk factors","The biggest risk factor is high blood pressure, or hypertension. Others include smoking, diabetes, high cholesterol, being overweight, heavy drinking, physical inactivity, an irregular heartbeat called atrial fibrillation, and older age. Many of these can be controlled."]],
k:[["Main types","Ischemic and hemorrhagic"],["Biggest risk factor","High blood pressure"],["Warning signs","BE FAST"],["Clot-dissolving drug approved","1996"],["World Stroke Day","29 October"]],
d:[["c. 400 BC","Hippocrates describes sudden paralysis, called apoplexy"],["1658","Johann Jakob Wepfer links apoplexy to bleeding in the brain"],["1996","A clot-dissolving drug is approved for stroke treatment in the United States"],["2006","The first World Stroke Day is held"],["2015","Trials show that removing clots mechanically improves recovery"],["2020s","Mobile stroke units and telemedicine spread to more hospitals"]],
s2:[["Stroke in the Philippines","Stroke and other diseases of the heart and blood vessels are among the leading causes of death in the Philippines. High blood pressure is very common and often goes undiagnosed or untreated. Health programs encourage people to have their blood pressure checked regularly, take prescribed medicines and reduce salt in their diet."],["Salt and blood pressure","Eating too much salt raises blood pressure in many people. Filipino diets often include salty foods such as dried fish, instant noodles, processed meats and condiments such as patis, bagoong and soy sauce. Health experts recommend tasting food before adding salt, cooking with herbs and spices and choosing fresh foods."],["Recovering from stroke","Many stroke survivors need rehabilitation to relearn skills such as walking, speaking, eating and dressing. Physical, occupational and speech therapists help them regain abilities. The brain can partly rewire itself, a process called neuroplasticity, especially with early and regular therapy. Family support is very important."],["Stroke in younger people","Although most strokes occur in older adults, they can happen at any age, even in children. Rising rates of high blood pressure, diabetes and obesity among younger adults worry doctors. Anyone with sudden stroke symptoms, whatever their age, should get emergency help."],["Modern treatments","A clot-dissolving medicine was approved for stroke in the United States in 1996, transforming treatment. In 2015 major trials showed that removing large clots with a thin tube threaded through the arteries greatly improves recovery. Some cities use ambulances with CT scanners, called mobile stroke units, to begin treatment before reaching hospital."],["Preventing stroke","Up to about 80 percent of strokes may be preventable. Measures include checking and controlling blood pressure, not smoking, being physically active, eating plenty of vegetables and fruit, limiting salt and alcohol, keeping a healthy weight and managing diabetes. Treating an irregular heartbeat with medicine can also greatly reduce stroke risk."]],
sp:["Time is brain","One morning, a woman notices that her father’s smile looks lopsided and he cannot lift his right arm. His words come out slurred. Remembering a television campaign about stroke, she calls for an ambulance instead of waiting to see whether he gets better. At the hospital, a scan shows a clot blocking an artery in his brain, and doctors quickly give him clot-dissolving medicine. Within hours his arm begins to move again. Doctors often share stories like this to show how recognizing the signs and acting fast can make the difference between recovery and lifelong disability."],
g:[["Stroke","Sudden loss of blood flow to part of the brain"],["Ischemic stroke","A stroke caused by a blocked blood vessel"],["Hemorrhagic stroke","A stroke caused by bleeding in the brain"],["Hypertension","High blood pressure"]],
f:["High blood pressure is the biggest stroke risk factor.","BE FAST helps people spot a stroke.","Many strokes can be prevented."],
fun:"The old name for stroke, apoplexy, comes from a Greek word meaning struck down suddenly."});

ADDART({id:"asthma",t:"Asthma",c:"Disease and Medicine",i:"🫁",w:"Asthma",r:["lungs","allergy","immune","hygiene","exercise"],
p:[["Narrowed airways","Asthma is a long-term condition in which the airways in the lungs become swollen and narrow."],["Wheezing and coughing","It causes coughing, wheezing, chest tightness and shortness of breath."],["Can be controlled","With the right medicines and care, most people with asthma live full, active lives."]],
l:"Asthma is a common long-term disease of the lungs in which the airways become inflamed, swollen and narrowed, making it hard to breathe. Symptoms include coughing, wheezing, chest tightness and shortness of breath, which may come and go and can suddenly worsen in an asthma attack. Attacks are often triggered by things such as dust mites, smoke, air pollution, pollen, pet dander, colds, cold air or exercise. Asthma affects hundreds of millions of people worldwide, often beginning in childhood. There is no cure, but inhaled medicines can control symptoms and prevent attacks, allowing most people with asthma to study, work and play sports normally.",
s:[["What happens in the lungs","Air reaches the lungs through branching tubes called bronchi. In asthma, the lining of these tubes is inflamed and sensitive. When triggered, the muscles around them tighten, the lining swells and extra mucus is produced, so the airways narrow and air struggles to flow in and out."],["Common triggers","Triggers vary from person to person. They include house dust mites, cockroaches, mold, pollen, animal fur, tobacco and wood smoke, vehicle and factory pollution, strong smells, respiratory infections, cold or humid air, exercise and strong emotions. Learning and avoiding personal triggers helps prevent attacks."],["Inhalers","Most asthma medicines are breathed in using inhalers, which deliver medicine directly to the lungs. Reliever inhalers quickly relax the airway muscles during symptoms. Preventer inhalers, usually containing steroids, reduce inflammation over time and are taken regularly. Spacers attached to inhalers help children and others get more medicine into the lungs."],["An asthma attack","During a severe attack, a person may struggle to breathe or speak, and their lips may turn blue. They should use their reliever inhaler and seek emergency help if they do not improve quickly. Having a written asthma action plan helps families know what to do."]],
k:[["Affected organ","Lungs (airways)"],["Main symptoms","Cough, wheeze, chest tightness, breathlessness"],["Main treatment","Inhaled medicines"],["Metered-dose inhaler invented","1956"],["World Asthma Day","First Tuesday of May"]],
d:[["c. 400 BC","Greek physicians use the word asthma, meaning panting"],["1190","Maimonides writes a treatise on asthma"],["1956","The pressurized metered-dose inhaler is invented"],["1970s","Inhaled steroid medicines are introduced"],["1993","The Global Initiative for Asthma is launched"],["1998","The first World Asthma Day is held"]],
s2:[["Who gets asthma","Asthma often starts in childhood and is one of the most common chronic diseases in children. It tends to run in families and is more common in people with allergies or eczema. Some children grow out of it, while others have it throughout life. Adults can also develop asthma."],["Air pollution and asthma","Polluted air, from vehicles, factories, burning garbage and cooking with wood or charcoal indoors, can trigger asthma and may contribute to its development. In crowded cities such as Metro Manila, traffic fumes are a concern. Cleaner fuels, better ventilation and reducing smoke help protect people’s lungs."],["The inhaler’s invention","In 1955 a 13-year-old girl with asthma asked her father, who worked at a drug company in the United States, why her medicine could not come in a spray can like hairspray. The company developed the pressurized metered-dose inhaler, released in 1956, which transformed asthma treatment and is still widely used."],["Sport and asthma","Exercise can trigger asthma symptoms in some people, but with good control, people with asthma can play sports at the highest level. Many Olympic athletes and professional footballers have asthma. Warming up properly and using a reliever inhaler before exercise, if prescribed, can help."],["Asthma in the Philippines","Asthma is common among Filipino children and adults. Doctors encourage patients to use preventer inhalers regularly, avoid smoke and keep homes free of dust and mold. Health groups have worked to make inhalers more affordable and to train health workers to diagnose and manage asthma."],["Living well with asthma","With good treatment, most people with asthma have few symptoms. Key steps include taking preventer medicine as prescribed, knowing how to use inhalers correctly, avoiding triggers, getting vaccinated against flu and having regular check-ups. Schools and coaches should know which students have asthma and where their inhalers are kept."]],
sp:["A spray can for medicine","In the 1950s, people with asthma used bulky glass nebulizers with rubber bulbs to breathe in medicine. They were fragile and hard to carry. According to the company’s history, Susie Maison, the 13-year-old daughter of the president of Riker Laboratories in California, asked why her asthma medicine could not be put in a spray can like her mother’s hairspray. Engineers took up the challenge and developed a small canister that released an exact dose of medicine with each puff. Launched in 1956, the metered-dose inhaler became one of the most widely used medical devices in the world."],
g:[["Asthma","A long-term condition that narrows the airways"],["Bronchi","The main airways leading into the lungs"],["Inhaler","A device that delivers medicine into the lungs"],["Trigger","Something that sets off asthma symptoms"]],
f:["Asthma narrows the airways of the lungs.","Inhalers deliver medicine straight to the lungs.","The metered-dose inhaler was invented in 1956."],
fun:"The word asthma comes from an ancient Greek word meaning panting or short of breath."});

