/* data/tech9.js */
// Technology, more articles: AI-generated art, voice assistants, motorcycles, open-source software and fiber optics.
ADDART({id:"aiart",t:"AI-Generated Images",c:"Artificial Intelligence",i:"🖼️",w:"Artificial intelligence art",r:["ai","neural","llm","modernart","aisafety"],
p:[["Pictures from words","AI image generators create pictures from written descriptions."],["Rapid growth","Tools such as DALL-E, Midjourney and Stable Diffusion became popular in 2022."],["Big debates","They raise questions about creativity, copyright, jobs and fake images."]],
l:"AI-generated images are pictures created by artificial intelligence systems, usually from a written description called a prompt. A person might type a cat astronaut painted in the style of a Filipino jeepney, and within seconds the system produces original-looking images. These tools are trained on huge collections of images and captions from the internet, learning patterns that link words to visual features. AI image generators became widely available in 2022 with tools such as DALL-E, Midjourney and Stable Diffusion, and later video generators followed. They are used for illustration, design, advertising, games and fun, but they have also sparked intense debate about artistic creativity, copyright, the livelihoods of artists and the spread of misleading fake images.",
s:[["How they work","Most modern image generators use a method called diffusion. During training, the system learns to remove random noise from images step by step. To create a new picture, it starts from pure noise and gradually shapes it into an image that matches the prompt, guided by a model that connects words and pictures."],["Early AI art","Computer-generated art has existed since the 1960s, and the program AARON, created by the artist Harold Cohen from the 1970s, drew original pictures. In 2014 researchers invented generative adversarial networks, or GANs, which could produce realistic faces and artworks. In 2018 a GAN-made portrait sold at auction for 432,500 US dollars."],["The 2022 boom","In 2022 several powerful image generators became available to the public. Millions of people experimented with them, sharing fantastical images online. Companies added image generation to design software, phones and chat assistants, and quality improved quickly, from blurry pictures to images that can be hard to tell from photographs."],["Uses","AI images are used to brainstorm ideas, create concept art, illustrate stories and presentations, design products and make advertising. Some artists use them as one tool in their creative process, combining AI output with their own drawing, painting or photography."]],
k:[["Input","A written prompt"],["Main method","Diffusion models"],["Public boom","2022"],["Well-known tools","DALL-E, Midjourney, Stable Diffusion"],["Main debates","Copyright, artists’ jobs, deepfakes"]],
d:[["1973","Harold Cohen begins developing AARON, an art-making program"],["2014","Generative adversarial networks are invented"],["2018","An AI-generated portrait sells at Christie’s auction house"],["2021","OpenAI introduces DALL-E"],["2022","Midjourney and Stable Diffusion are released to the public"],["2023","Artists and image companies file lawsuits over AI training"]],
s2:[["Is it art?","People disagree about whether AI-generated images are art and who should be considered the artist: the person writing the prompt, the developers or the system itself. In 2022 an AI-assisted image won a prize at a state fair art competition in the United States, causing a heated debate. Supporters compare AI to earlier tools such as cameras, while critics argue it lacks human intention and skill."],["Copyright questions","AI image systems are trained on billions of images, many created by artists and photographers who did not give permission. Artists and stock photo companies have filed lawsuits arguing that this infringes their rights, while AI companies argue that learning from public images is lawful. Courts in several countries are considering these cases. Authorities in the United States have said that images generated without meaningful human creative input cannot be copyrighted."],["Artists’ livelihoods","Some illustrators, designers and concept artists report losing work as companies turn to AI images. Others have found new opportunities. Artists’ groups call for consent, credit and payment when their work is used to train AI, and some tools now let artists opt out or use systems trained only on licensed images."],["Fake images","AI can create realistic images of events that never happened, such as fake photos of politicians, disasters or celebrities. These can spread misinformation and be used to harass people. Technology companies are developing watermarks and labels to identify AI-generated content, and many governments are considering rules."],["Bias in images","Because AI learns from internet images, it can repeat stereotypes, for example showing certain jobs mainly with one gender or ethnicity, or poorly representing people and places from some countries, including Southeast Asia. Developers are working to reduce such biases, and users are encouraged to look critically at results."],["Using AI images responsibly","Experts suggest being open about when images are AI-generated, respecting the rights of artists, not creating images that deceive or harm real people, and checking whether school, workplace or competition rules allow AI-made work."]],
sp:["The portrait that sold for a fortune","In October 2018 the auction house Christie’s in New York sold a blurry portrait of a man in a dark coat, titled Edmond de Belamy. It was created by a French art group called Obvious using a generative adversarial network trained on thousands of historical portraits. Instead of a painter’s signature, the corner bore a mathematical formula from the algorithm. Expected to sell for less than 10,000 dollars, it fetched 432,500 dollars, making headlines around the world. The sale also stirred controversy, as the group had used computer code written by another young programmer who received no share of the money."],
g:[["Prompt","A written instruction given to an AI system"],["Diffusion model","An AI that creates images by gradually removing noise"],["GAN","Generative adversarial network, two AIs that compete to make realistic images"],["Watermark","A hidden or visible mark identifying the source of an image"]],
f:["AI image generators create pictures from text prompts.","An AI portrait sold for 432,500 dollars in 2018.","AI images raise copyright debates."],
fun:"Early AI image generators were notorious for drawing hands with too many or too few fingers."});

ADDART({id:"voiceassistants",t:"Voice Assistants",c:"Artificial Intelligence",i:"🗣️",w:"Virtual assistant",r:["ai","llm","smartphone","smarthome","dataprivacy"],
p:[["Talking to computers","Voice assistants let people control devices and find information by speaking."],["Siri, Alexa and more","Well-known assistants include Siri, Alexa and Google Assistant."],["Smarter conversations","New AI models allow more natural spoken conversations."]],
l:"A voice assistant is a computer program that understands spoken commands and questions and responds by speaking, carrying out tasks or finding information. Voice assistants are built into smartphones, smart speakers, cars, televisions and other devices. They use speech recognition to turn spoken words into text, artificial intelligence to understand what the person wants, and speech synthesis to reply aloud. Apple’s Siri, launched in 2011, brought voice assistants to millions of people, followed by Amazon’s Alexa, Google Assistant and others. Since the 2020s, assistants powered by large language models can hold more natural conversations. They make technology easier to use, especially for people with disabilities, but raise concerns about privacy.",
s:[["How they work","When a person says a wake word, such as hey Siri or Alexa, the device starts listening. The recorded speech is converted into text by speech recognition software, often in the cloud. Language-understanding systems work out the request, such as setting a timer or checking the weather, and a synthetic voice gives the answer."],["Early speech recognition","In 1952 Bell Laboratories built Audrey, a machine that could recognize spoken digits from a single speaker. IBM’s Shoebox, shown in 1962, understood 16 words. For decades, speech recognition was slow and error-prone, but improvements in computing and statistical methods gradually made it practical for dictation software in the 1990s."],["The smartphone era","Apple introduced Siri on the iPhone 4S in 2011. Google launched Google Now and later Google Assistant, and Microsoft introduced Cortana. Amazon’s Echo smart speaker with Alexa, launched in 2014, put voice assistants in homes, where people use them to play music, control lights and ask questions."],["What they can do","Voice assistants can make calls, send messages, set alarms and reminders, answer questions, translate languages, give directions, play music and control smart home devices. Newer assistants can summarize information, help with homework, hold conversations and describe what a phone camera sees."]],
k:[["Early speech recognizer","Audrey, Bell Labs, 1952"],["Siri launched","2011"],["Amazon Echo launched","2014"],["Key technologies","Speech recognition and speech synthesis"],["Main concern","Privacy"]],
d:[["1952","Bell Labs builds Audrey, which recognizes spoken digits"],["1962","IBM demonstrates the Shoebox speech recognizer"],["1997","Dragon NaturallySpeaking software allows continuous dictation"],["2011","Apple launches Siri"],["2014","Amazon launches the Echo smart speaker with Alexa"],["2023","Assistants powered by large language models begin to spread"]],
s2:[["Helping people with disabilities","Voice assistants can be life-changing for people who are blind or have limited movement, allowing them to send messages, read news, control lights and call for help by voice. Older people who find screens difficult also benefit. Disability advocates have pushed companies to improve support for people with speech differences."],["Accents and languages","Voice assistants have worked best for speakers of major languages with standard accents and have struggled with regional accents and smaller languages. Filipinos who mix English and Filipino in Taglish have found some assistants confused by switching languages. Companies have been adding more languages and training systems on more diverse voices."],["Privacy concerns","Because voice assistants listen for wake words, many people worry that they may record private conversations. Reports have revealed that some companies had human workers listen to recordings to improve accuracy. Companies now offer settings to delete recordings and mute microphones, and privacy laws regulate how voice data is used."],["Large language model assistants","From 2023, companies began connecting voice assistants to large language models, the technology behind AI chatbots. These assistants can understand more complex requests, answer follow-up questions and speak with more natural voices and timing. Critics warn that they can still make mistakes or invent facts."],["Voice cloning","AI can now copy a person’s voice from short recordings. This allows people who have lost their voices to illness to speak again with a synthetic version of their own voice. However, cloned voices have also been used in scams, such as fake calls pretending to be relatives in trouble, so experts advise families to agree on secret code words."],["The future of talking to machines","Researchers expect voice to become a more common way to interact with computers, cars and appliances. Assistants may become more personal, remembering preferences and helping with daily planning. How to balance convenience with privacy and accuracy remains a central question."]],
sp:["The machine called Audrey","In 1952 engineers at Bell Laboratories in the United States unveiled a machine called Audrey, short for Automatic Digit Recognizer. It filled a large rack of electronics and could recognize the numbers zero to nine when spoken by its inventor, with high accuracy, though it worked poorly for other voices. Lights flashed to show which digit it had heard. Engineers hoped such machines could one day let people dial telephones by voice. It took nearly 60 years of progress in computing before people could simply ask their phones to call home."],
g:[["Voice assistant","A program that responds to spoken commands"],["Speech recognition","Technology that converts speech into text"],["Speech synthesis","Technology that produces spoken words from text"],["Wake word","A word that activates a voice assistant"]],
f:["Siri launched in 2011.","Audrey recognized spoken digits in 1952.","Voice assistants help people with disabilities."],
fun:"The name Siri was chosen by one of its creators and is a Norwegian name meaning beautiful woman who leads you to victory."});

ADDART({id:"motorcycles",t:"Motorcycles",c:"Transport",i:"🏍️",w:"Motorcycle",r:["cars","bicycle","jeepney","megacities","gigeconomy"],
p:[["Two wheels and an engine","A motorcycle is a two-wheeled vehicle powered by an engine."],["Asia’s workhorse","Motorcycles are the main family vehicle in much of Southeast Asia."],["Safety matters","Helmets and safe riding save many lives."]],
l:"A motorcycle is a two-wheeled motor vehicle, ranging from small scooters and underbones to powerful sports bikes and heavy touring machines. The first motorcycles appeared in Germany in the 1880s, and mass production began in the 1890s. In the 20th century motorcycles became popular for sport, travel and transport, and Japanese companies such as Honda made them affordable worldwide. Today hundreds of millions of motorcycles are used around the world, especially in Asia, where they are often the main vehicle for families, workers and delivery riders. In the Philippines, motorcycles are the most common type of registered vehicle. They are cheap and efficient but riders face high risks, making helmets and road safety vital.",
s:[["Early motorcycles","In 1885 the German engineers Gottlieb Daimler and Wilhelm Maybach fitted a small gasoline engine to a wooden frame with two wheels, creating the Reitwagen, often called the first motorcycle. In 1894 the Hildebrand and Wolfmüller became the first motorcycle produced in large numbers for sale."],["How a motorcycle works","Most motorcycles have a gasoline engine that turns the rear wheel through a chain, belt or shaft. Riders control speed with a twist grip on the handlebar, brake with hand and foot levers, and in many models change gears with a foot pedal. Scooters and many small bikes have automatic transmissions. Riders balance and steer by leaning into turns."],["Kinds of motorcycles","Scooters have small wheels and a platform for the feet. Underbones, popular in Southeast Asia, combine features of scooters and motorcycles. Standard and sport bikes are built for speed and handling, cruisers for relaxed riding, touring bikes for long trips and dirt bikes for rough tracks."],["The Super Cub","In 1958 Honda launched the Super Cub, a light, reliable and easy-to-ride step-through motorcycle. It became the best-selling motor vehicle in history, with more than 100 million built by 2017. Its success helped make motorcycles everyday transport across Asia, Africa and Latin America."]],
k:[["First motorcycle","Daimler Reitwagen, 1885"],["First mass-produced motorcycle","Hildebrand and Wolfmüller, 1894"],["Best-selling motor vehicle","Honda Super Cub"],["Philippine helmet law","Republic Act 10054 (2009)"],["Most registered vehicles in the Philippines","Motorcycles and tricycles"]],
d:[["1885","Daimler and Maybach build the Reitwagen"],["1894","The first mass-produced motorcycle goes on sale"],["1903","Harley-Davidson is founded in the United States"],["1958","Honda launches the Super Cub"],["2009","The Philippines passes the Motorcycle Helmet Act"],["2019","A pilot program for motorcycle taxis begins in Metro Manila"]],
s2:[["Motorcycles in the Philippines","Motorcycles are the most popular vehicles in the Philippines, used for commuting, carrying goods, farming and family trips. They are affordable, use little fuel and can weave through heavy traffic and travel on narrow rural roads. Many families buy them through installment plans."],["Tricycles and habal-habal","The tricycle, a motorcycle with a covered sidecar, is a common form of public transport in Philippine towns and cities. In rural and mountainous areas, habal-habal, motorcycles carrying several passengers or extended with wooden planks, transport people where other vehicles cannot go."],["Motorcycle taxis and delivery","App-based motorcycle taxi services have grown in Metro Manila and other cities since a government pilot program began in 2019. Food and parcel delivery riders, many working through apps, have become a familiar sight. Supporters praise the speed and jobs these services provide, while debates continue over safety, regulation and workers’ rights."],["Road safety","Motorcycle riders are much more likely than car occupants to be killed or seriously injured in crashes. In the Philippines, motorcyclists make up a large share of road deaths. The Motorcycle Helmet Act of 2009 requires riders and passengers to wear standard helmets, and rules limit carrying young children. Training, safe speeds and visible clothing also help."],["Electric motorcycles","Electric scooters and motorcycles are spreading quickly, especially in China, Vietnam, Taiwan, India and Indonesia. They are quiet and produce no exhaust fumes, helping reduce air pollution in cities. Battery-swapping stations allow riders to exchange a flat battery for a charged one in seconds."],["Motorcycle culture","Motorcycles have inspired clubs, races and films. Events such as the Isle of Man TT and MotoGP draw huge audiences. In the Philippines, riding clubs organize long rides to provinces, charity events and safety campaigns, and motorcycle shows attract many fans."]],
sp:["The first motorcycle ride","On a November day in 1885, 17-year-old Paul Daimler climbed onto a strange machine built by his father, Gottlieb Daimler, and his partner Wilhelm Maybach. It had a wooden frame, iron-banded wooden wheels, small side wheels to help it stay upright, and a tiny gasoline engine under the seat. He rode it about 12 kilometers between two towns near Stuttgart, Germany, reaching about 12 kilometers per hour. The leather seat reportedly caught fire from the hot engine. The Reitwagen, or riding car, was really a test of the engine Daimler would soon put into automobiles, but it is remembered as the ancestor of all motorcycles."],
g:[["Motorcycle","A two-wheeled motor vehicle"],["Scooter","A motorcycle with small wheels and a platform for the feet"],["Underbone","A light motorcycle with a step-through frame, common in Asia"],["Habal-habal","A motorcycle used to carry passengers in rural Philippines"]],
f:["The first motorcycle was built in 1885.","The Honda Super Cub is the best-selling motor vehicle ever.","Philippine law requires motorcycle helmets."],
fun:"The world’s first motorcycle had a leather seat that reportedly caught fire from the engine during its first ride."});

ADDART({id:"opensource",t:"Open-Source Software",c:"Computers",i:"🐧",w:"Open-source software",r:["coding","comp","internet","cybersecurity","cloud"],
p:[["Code anyone can see","Open-source software is software whose code is freely available to read, change and share."],["Built together","Volunteers and companies around the world collaborate to build it."],["Everywhere","Open-source software runs much of the internet, phones and supercomputers."]],
l:"Open-source software is computer software whose source code, the instructions written by programmers, is published so that anyone can read it, use it, change it and share it, usually under licenses that guarantee these freedoms. It is often developed collaboratively by communities of volunteers, students and companies around the world. Famous examples include the Linux operating system, the Firefox web browser, the Android phone platform and Wikipedia’s software. Open-source code now forms the foundation of much of the internet, most smartphones and nearly all of the world’s supercomputers. Supporters value its openness, security and shared benefits, while challenges include funding the volunteers who maintain critical code.",
s:[["Source code","Programs are written by people in programming languages, producing source code. Proprietary software companies usually keep their source code secret and sell only the finished program. Open-source projects publish their code, letting others learn from it, fix problems and build new features."],["Free software","In 1983 the American programmer Richard Stallman launched the GNU Project to create a completely free operating system, and in 1985 he founded the Free Software Foundation. He argued that users should have the freedom to run, study, change and share software, and created licenses such as the GNU General Public License to protect these freedoms."],["Linux","In 1991 Linus Torvalds, a Finnish student, released the first version of the Linux kernel, the core of an operating system, and invited others to help. Thousands of programmers joined in. Combined with GNU tools, Linux became a complete system that now runs most web servers, the world’s fastest supercomputers and, through Android, billions of phones."],["The term open source","In 1998 a group of software developers introduced the term open source to describe freely shared software in a way that appealed to businesses. That year the company Netscape released the code of its web browser, which later became the basis of Firefox. Today many large technology companies both use and contribute to open-source projects."]],
k:[["GNU Project founded","1983"],["Linux first released","1991"],["Term open source coined","1998"],["Famous examples","Linux, Firefox, Android, Python"],["Common license","GNU General Public License"]],
d:[["1983","Richard Stallman announces the GNU Project"],["1985","The Free Software Foundation is founded"],["1991","Linus Torvalds releases Linux"],["1998","The term open source is introduced"],["2004","Firefox 1.0 is released"],["2008","The code-sharing platform GitHub is launched"]],
s2:[["How projects work","Open-source projects usually have maintainers who review and accept changes. Contributors anywhere in the world can suggest improvements, report bugs or write documentation. Platforms such as GitHub and GitLab host millions of projects, allowing people to collaborate across time zones and countries."],["Why companies use it","Businesses use open-source software because it saves money, avoids dependence on a single supplier and lets them customize tools. Many companies pay employees to contribute to projects they rely on. Governments, schools and nonprofit groups, including some in the Philippines, use open-source tools to cut costs."],["Security","Supporters argue that open code is more secure because many people can inspect it and find flaws. However, problems can still go unnoticed. In 2021 a serious flaw called Log4Shell was found in widely used open-source code, and in 2024 a hidden backdoor was discovered in a compression tool called xz before it could spread widely. These events highlighted the need to support maintainers."],["Who pays the maintainers?","Many important projects are maintained by small groups of volunteers in their spare time. Critics warn that the digital world depends on code that may be underfunded. Foundations, company sponsorships and donation platforms now help pay maintainers, and some governments have started funding open-source security."],["Open source beyond software","The open-source idea has spread to other fields. Wikipedia lets anyone edit its encyclopedia articles, OpenStreetMap is a free world map made by volunteers, and open hardware projects share designs for electronics and 3D printers. Scientists increasingly share data and code openly."],["Learning to code","Open-source projects give students and beginners a chance to read real code, practice programming and contribute to software used by millions. Filipino developers contribute to international projects, and local communities hold meetups and events to encourage open-source participation."]],
sp:["Just a hobby","On 25 August 1991, Linus Torvalds, a 21-year-old computer science student at the University of Helsinki, posted a short message to an online discussion group. He wrote that he was making a free operating system, just a hobby, which he said would not be big and professional. He invited others to share ideas. Within a few years, programmers around the world were contributing code, and his hobby grew into Linux, one of the most important pieces of software ever created. Its mascot, a friendly penguin named Tux, was chosen because Torvalds said he had once been bitten by a penguin at a zoo."],
g:[["Source code","The human-readable instructions of a computer program"],["Open source","Software whose code anyone can read, change and share"],["License","Legal terms that say how software may be used"],["Maintainer","A person who manages and reviews changes to a project"]],
f:["Linux was released in 1991.","Android is built on Linux.","Most web servers run open-source software."],
fun:"Linux’s penguin mascot, Tux, was chosen partly because Linus Torvalds said he had once been nipped by a penguin at a zoo."});

ADDART({id:"fiberoptics",t:"Fiber Optics",c:"Communication",i:"💡",w:"Optical fiber",r:["internet","light","laser","phone","cloud"],
p:[["Light through glass","Fiber optics send information as pulses of light through thin strands of glass."],["The internet’s backbone","Undersea fiber cables carry most international internet traffic."],["Fast and clear","Fiber connections are much faster than copper wires."]],
l:"Fiber optics is the technology of sending light through long, thin strands of very pure glass or plastic called optical fibers. Each fiber is about as thin as a human hair. Light travels along it by bouncing off its walls, a process called total internal reflection, and can carry huge amounts of information as rapid flashes of laser light. Optical fibers form the backbone of the internet and modern telephone networks, including undersea cables that link continents and carry almost all international data. They are also used in medicine to see inside the body, in lighting and in sensors. The physicist Charles Kao, who showed in 1966 that glass fibers could carry signals over long distances, received the Nobel Prize in Physics in 2009.",
s:[["How light stays inside","An optical fiber has a glass core surrounded by a layer of glass called cladding that bends light less. When light hits the boundary at a shallow angle, it reflects back into the core instead of escaping. Bouncing again and again, it can travel many kilometers along a fiber, even around gentle bends."],["Carrying information","A laser at one end switches on and off billions of times a second, representing the ones and zeros of digital data. At the other end, a detector turns the light back into electrical signals. Many colors of light can travel through the same fiber at once, each carrying its own stream of data, so a single fiber can carry enormous amounts of information."],["Better than copper","Compared with copper wires, optical fibers carry far more data over much longer distances with less signal loss. They are not affected by electrical interference, are hard to tap secretly and are lighter. This is why internet providers install fiber to homes and businesses for fast broadband."],["Undersea cables","Hundreds of fiber-optic cables lie on the ocean floor, linking countries and continents. They carry the vast majority of international internet traffic, phone calls and financial transactions. Ships lay them across the seabed, and repair ships fix them when they are damaged by anchors, fishing or earthquakes."]],
k:[["Principle","Total internal reflection"],["Key paper","Charles Kao, 1966"],["Low-loss fiber made","Corning, 1970"],["First transatlantic fiber cable","TAT-8, 1988"],["Nobel Prize","Charles Kao, 2009"]],
d:[["1842","Daniel Colladon shows light guided along a stream of water"],["1960","The first working laser is built"],["1966","Charles Kao proposes glass fibers for communication"],["1970","Corning makes low-loss optical fiber"],["1988","TAT-8, the first transatlantic fiber-optic cable, begins service"],["2009","Charles Kao receives the Nobel Prize in Physics"]],
s2:[["Guiding light","In 1842 the Swiss physicist Daniel Colladon showed that light could follow a curved stream of water pouring from a tank, staying trapped inside it. This light fountain demonstrated the principle behind optical fibers. Later inventors used bundles of glass fibers to carry images, leading to medical instruments for looking inside the body."],["Charles Kao’s insight","In the 1960s glass fibers lost too much light to be useful for long-distance communication. Charles Kao, working in England, showed in 1966 that the losses came from impurities, and that very pure glass could carry signals for kilometers. His prediction inspired researchers at Corning, who in 1970 produced the first low-loss fiber. Kao is often called the father of fiber optics."],["Fiber in the Philippines","As an archipelago, the Philippines depends on undersea cables to connect its islands and link it to the world. Several international cables land at stations on Luzon and other islands, and domestic cables connect major islands. Internet providers have expanded fiber-to-the-home service in cities, while a national broadband plan aims to bring fast internet to more provinces."],["Vulnerable cables","Because so much traffic depends on undersea cables, damage can cut off communication. In 2022 a volcanic eruption near Tonga severed its only international cable, leaving the country largely offline for weeks. Countries worry about protecting cables from accidents, earthquakes and possible sabotage."],["Fiber in medicine","Endoscopes use bundles of optical fibers to carry light into the body and send images back, allowing doctors to examine the stomach, lungs or joints and perform keyhole surgery through small cuts. Fibers also deliver laser light for eye surgery and other treatments."],["Light for the future","Researchers are developing fibers that carry even more data, hollow-core fibers in which light travels through air for greater speed, and fibers that can sense earthquakes, temperature or vibration along their length. Optical connections are also used inside large data centers that power cloud computing and artificial intelligence."]],
sp:["Repairing a cable at the bottom of the sea","When an undersea cable breaks, perhaps snagged by a ship’s anchor or shaken by an undersea earthquake, a special repair ship sails to the area. Engineers send test signals to locate the break, sometimes thousands of meters deep. The crew lowers a grapnel, a hooked tool, or a remotely operated underwater robot to catch the cable and haul it to the surface. On board, technicians carefully splice the hair-thin glass fibers together in a clean room, test the connection and lower the repaired cable back to the seabed. Repairs can take days or weeks, depending on weather and depth."],
g:[["Optical fiber","A thin strand of glass that carries light"],["Total internal reflection","When light bounces back inside a material instead of escaping"],["Cladding","The outer glass layer that keeps light in the core"],["Broadband","A fast internet connection"]],
f:["Optical fibers are about as thin as a human hair.","Undersea cables carry most international internet traffic.","Charles Kao won the 2009 Nobel Prize."],
fun:"A single modern optical fiber can carry the equivalent of millions of video calls at the same time."});

