// Arts and Culture, more articles: the kundiman, weaving and textiles, manga and ceramics.
ADDART({id:"kundiman",t:"Kundiman",c:"Music",i:"🎼",w:"Kundiman",r:["opm","zarzuela","phdance","guitar","phlit"],
p:[["The Filipino love song","The kundiman is a traditional Filipino song of love, longing and devotion."],["Love and country","Many kundiman expressed love for the nation in the language of romance."],["From folk to concert hall","Composers such as Francisco Santiago and Nicanor Abelardo turned it into a classical art song."]],
l:"The kundiman is a genre of traditional Filipino song, usually slow and lyrical, about love, longing, sorrow and devotion. It developed in the Tagalog region during the Spanish colonial period, and by the late 1800s it was sung in serenades, in which young men sang beneath the windows of the women they courted. During the Philippine Revolution and the American period, many kundiman used the language of romantic love to express love for the homeland, at times when open patriotic songs were dangerous. In the early 1900s classically trained composers such as Francisco Santiago and Nicanor Abelardo created the kundiman art song. The kundiman remains a treasured part of Filipino music and identity.",
s:[["Musical features","A typical kundiman is written in triple time, like a slow waltz, and often begins in a minor key, expressing sadness or longing, before shifting to a major key in its second part, suggesting hope or resolution. The melodies are smooth and expressive, and the lyrics are usually in Tagalog, rich in poetic imagery."],["Serenades","In the 1800s and early 1900s, the harana was a custom in which a young man, accompanied by friends playing guitars, sang kundiman and other love songs outside a young woman’s house at night. If she was interested, she might open her window or invite the singers in. The harana tradition has faded but is still remembered and sometimes revived."],["Songs of the nation","During the Revolution against Spain and later under American rule, songwriters disguised patriotic feelings as love songs. The beloved woman in the song often stood for the Philippines itself. Jocelynang Baliwag, a kundiman from about 1896, is known as the kundiman of the Revolution, and Bayan Ko, written in 1928, became one of the most famous patriotic songs."],["Art songs","Filipino composers trained in Western classical music raised the kundiman to a concert form. Francisco Santiago, called the father of the kundiman art song, composed Anak Dalita in 1917. Nicanor Abelardo wrote Nasaan Ka Irog and Mutya ng Pasig, which remain favorites of singers and audiences."]],
k:[["Origin","Tagalog region, Spanish colonial period"],["Time signature","Usually triple time"],["Typical key pattern","Minor, then major"],["Father of the kundiman art song","Francisco Santiago"],["Famous kundiman","Nasaan Ka Irog, Mutya ng Pasig, Bayan Ko"]],
d:[["1800s","The kundiman develops as a Tagalog love song"],["c. 1896","Jocelynang Baliwag is sung during the Revolution"],["1917","Francisco Santiago composes Anak Dalita"],["1924","Nicanor Abelardo composes Nasaan Ka Irog"],["1926","Abelardo composes Mutya ng Pasig"],["1928","Bayan Ko is written by José Corazón de Jesús and Constancio de Guzmán"]],
s2:[["Where the name comes from","The origin of the word kundiman is uncertain. One popular explanation is that it comes from the phrase kung hindi man, meaning if it were not so, which appeared in the lyrics of early songs. Others have suggested different origins, and scholars continue to discuss it."],["Jocelynang Baliwag","Jocelynang Baliwag is a kundiman said to have been written for a young woman from Baliwag, Bulacan. During the Revolution, revolutionaries sang it as a song about the motherland, with the woman representing the Philippines. It is often called the first kundiman of the Revolution."],["Bayan Ko","Bayan Ko, with words by the poet José Corazón de Jesús and music by Constancio de Guzmán, was written in 1928 for a play. Its lyrics compare the Philippines to a bird longing to be free. It became an anthem of protest against foreign rule and, during the martial law years and the 1986 People Power Revolution, a powerful song of resistance and hope."],["Great kundiman singers","The singer Atang de la Rama was celebrated as the Queen of the Kundiman, and later singers such as Sylvia La Torre and Ruben Tagalog helped popularize kundiman on radio and records. Opera singers and choirs continue to perform kundiman in concerts at home and abroad."],["Kundiman today","Although pop, rock and hip-hop dominate the airwaves, kundiman are still taught in schools, sung at cultural events and performed by classical singers and choirs. Contemporary artists have recorded new versions with modern arrangements, and some modern Filipino love songs, such as many in the OPM tradition, carry the kundiman’s spirit of heartfelt longing."],["Kundiman around the world","Filipino communities abroad perform kundiman to keep their heritage alive, and the songs have been featured in international concerts. Choirs from the Philippines, which have won many international competitions, often include kundiman arrangements in their programs."]],
sp:["A serenade by moonlight","Picture a quiet town in the early 1900s. Late in the evening, a group of young men gathers outside a house with capiz windows. One carries a guitar, another perhaps a banduria. The suitor clears his throat and begins to sing a kundiman, telling of a love so deep that he would give his life for it. Neighbors may peek out from their windows. Inside, the young woman listens, and if she approves, she slides open her window or her family invites the singers in for refreshments. If not, the window stays closed. The harana was a gentle and public way of courting, and kundiman were its finest songs."],
g:[["Kundiman","A traditional Filipino song of love and longing"],["Harana","A serenade sung outside a woman’s home"],["Art song","A classical song for voice and piano"],["Triple time","A rhythm with three beats in each measure"]],
f:["The kundiman is a Filipino love song.","Many kundiman expressed love for the country.","Bayan Ko was written in 1928."],
fun:"A popular explanation says the word kundiman comes from kung hindi man, meaning if it were not so, though no one is certain."});

ADDART({id:"weaving",t:"Weaving and Textiles",c:"Crafts",i:"🧵",w:"Weaving",r:["fastfashion","silk","indrev","comp","phfolk"],
p:[["Making cloth","Weaving is the craft of interlacing threads to make cloth."],["An ancient art","People have woven fabrics for thousands of years using looms."],["Philippine traditions","The Philippines has rich weaving traditions, from piña to t’nalak and inabel."]],
l:"Weaving is the craft of making cloth by interlacing two sets of threads at right angles: the warp, which runs lengthwise and is held tight on a frame called a loom, and the weft, which passes over and under the warp threads. People have woven textiles for thousands of years from fibers such as cotton, wool, silk, linen, abaca, pineapple and bamboo. Weaving traditions express the culture, history and beliefs of peoples around the world, from Chinese silk and Indian cotton to West African kente and Andean textiles. The Philippines has many distinctive weaving traditions, including piña cloth from Aklan, t’nalak of the T’boli, inabel of the Ilocos region and Yakan weaving of Basilan.",
s:[["How weaving works","On a loom, the warp threads are stretched side by side. The weaver lifts some warp threads and lowers others, creating a gap called a shed, then passes the weft thread through it, often on a shuttle. Changing which warp threads are lifted creates different patterns, from simple plain weaves to complex designs."],["Kinds of looms","The simplest looms include the backstrap loom, in which the weaver uses their own body to keep the warp tight; it is still used by many Philippine and other Indigenous weavers. Floor looms use foot pedals to lift threads, while modern factories use power looms run by computers."],["Fibers from nature","Cotton, linen, wool and silk are among the world’s most common natural fibers. In the Philippines, weavers also use abaca, a strong fiber from a relative of the banana plant, piña from pineapple leaves, and fibers from bamboo, banana and other plants. Natural dyes come from roots, bark, leaves and mud."],["Patterns with meaning","Many weaving traditions use patterns with special meanings, representing animals, plants, ancestors, stars or social status. Some designs are worn only for ceremonies or by certain people. Techniques such as ikat, in which threads are tie-dyed before weaving, create blurred, shimmering patterns."]],
k:[["Lengthwise threads","Warp"],["Crosswise threads","Weft"],["Weaving frame","Loom"],["Philippine fibers","Abaca, piña, cotton"],["Philippine fabric law","Philippine Tropical Fabrics Law (2004)"]],
d:[["c. 5000 BC","Linen is woven in ancient Egypt"],["c. 3000 BC","Silk weaving is practiced in China"],["1733","John Kay invents the flying shuttle"],["1804","Joseph Marie Jacquard introduces a loom controlled by punched cards"],["1998","T’boli weaver Lang Dulay receives the National Living Treasures Award"],["2004","The Philippines passes the Philippine Tropical Fabrics Law"]],
s2:[["Weaving and the Industrial Revolution","Weaving played a central part in the Industrial Revolution. John Kay’s flying shuttle of 1733 doubled the speed of weaving, and power looms driven by water and steam soon filled factories in Britain. This transformed cloth from a costly handmade product into a cheap one, but also put many hand weavers out of work."],["The loom that inspired computers","In 1804 the French weaver Joseph Marie Jacquard introduced a loom controlled by chains of punched cards, each card setting which threads would be lifted to create a pattern. This allowed complex designs to be woven automatically. The idea of storing instructions on punched cards later inspired early computer pioneers such as Charles Babbage and Herman Hollerith."],["Piña","Piña cloth is woven from fibers of pineapple leaves, mainly in Aklan province. It is fine, light and almost transparent, and is often hand-embroidered. Piña is used for the barong tagalog and Filipiniana dresses worn on formal occasions. In 2023 the traditional piña handloom weaving of Aklan was added to UNESCO’s list of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity."],["T’nalak, inabel and Yakan weaving","The T’boli of South Cotabato weave t’nalak from abaca fibers tie-dyed in deep red, black and white. Weavers are said to receive their designs in dreams, and the cloth is considered sacred. The Ilocano inabel is a sturdy cotton cloth with geometric patterns, while the Yakan of Basilan weave brightly colored, intricate textiles."],["Masters of the loom","The Philippines honors master weavers with the National Living Treasures Award, or Gawad sa Manlilikha ng Bayan. Recipients include Lang Dulay, a T’boli t’nalak weaver honored in 1998, and Magdalena Gamayo, an Ilocano inabel weaver honored in 2012. They have taught younger generations to keep their traditions alive."],["Weaving today","Handwoven textiles face competition from cheap machine-made fabrics, and fewer young people learn the craft. Designers, cooperatives and government programs promote Philippine textiles in fashion and home goods. The Philippine Tropical Fabrics Law of 2004 requires that uniforms of government officials and employees use locally made fabrics, supporting weavers."]],
sp:["The dream weavers of Lake Sebu","Around Lake Sebu in South Cotabato, T’boli women have woven t’nalak for generations. The process takes weeks or even months. Abaca fibers are stripped, dried and knotted together into long threads. Following a design held in memory, the weaver ties parts of the threads tightly so they resist dye, then soaks them in natural dyes made from roots and leaves. Finally she weaves the cloth on a backstrap loom, leaning back to keep the threads taut. Many T’boli weavers say the patterns come to them in dreams, gifts from the spirit of the abaca. This is why they are known as dream weavers."],
g:[["Warp","The lengthwise threads held tight on a loom"],["Weft","The crosswise threads woven over and under the warp"],["Loom","A frame for weaving cloth"],["Ikat","A technique of dyeing threads before weaving"]],
f:["Weaving uses warp and weft threads.","Jacquard’s punched-card loom inspired computers.","Piña cloth is woven from pineapple leaf fibers."],
fun:"Punched cards used to control weaving looms in 1804 later helped inspire the first computers."});

ADDART({id:"manga",t:"Manga",c:"Comics",i:"📚",w:"Manga",r:["anime","comics","japan","childlit","kdrama"],
p:[["Japanese comics","Manga are comics and graphic novels from Japan."],["Read right to left","Manga are traditionally read from right to left."],["A global phenomenon","Manga are read by millions of people around the world."]],
l:"Manga are comics and graphic novels created in Japan, or in a style that developed there. They cover almost every subject and audience, from adventure, sports and comedy to romance, horror, history, science fiction and everyday life, for children and adults alike. Manga are usually printed in black and white, published first in weekly or monthly magazines and later collected into books called tankōbon. Traditionally they are read from right to left. Modern manga were shaped after the Second World War by artists such as Osamu Tezuka. Many popular manga have been adapted into anime, films and games, and manga have become a global phenomenon, inspiring artists in the Philippines and around the world.",
s:[["Reading manga","Japanese is traditionally written from top to bottom and right to left, so manga books open from what Western readers think of as the back, and panels are read from the top right. Many translated editions keep this layout to preserve the artist’s original design."],["Styles and audiences","Manga are often grouped by their target audience. Shōnen manga are aimed at boys and teenagers and often feature action and friendship; shōjo manga for girls often focus on relationships and emotions; seinen and josei manga are written for adult men and women. Readers of all kinds enjoy every category."],["Art style","Manga often use expressive characters with large eyes, dramatic speed lines, close-ups and creative panel layouts that give a cinematic feel. Artists use symbols, such as a sweat drop for nervousness or a nosebleed for embarrassment, that readers learn to recognize."],["From magazine to book","Most manga first appear as chapters in magazines such as Weekly Shōnen Jump, which has published many famous series. Readers vote for their favorites, and popular series are collected into volumes. Some series run for decades and hundreds of chapters."]],
k:[["Country of origin","Japan"],["Reading direction","Right to left"],["God of manga","Osamu Tezuka"],["Collected volumes","Tankōbon"],["Best-selling series","One Piece"]],
d:[["1814","Hokusai publishes sketchbooks titled Hokusai Manga"],["1952","Osamu Tezuka begins Astro Boy"],["1968","Weekly Shōnen Jump magazine is launched"],["1984","Akira Toriyama begins Dragon Ball"],["1997","Eiichiro Oda begins One Piece"],["2010s","Digital manga apps and webcomics spread worldwide"]],
s2:[["Early roots","Japanese art has a long tradition of humorous and story-telling pictures, including picture scrolls from the 12th century showing animals behaving like people. The word manga, meaning whimsical pictures, became widely known through the sketchbooks of the woodblock artist Katsushika Hokusai, published from 1814."],["Osamu Tezuka","Osamu Tezuka, often called the god of manga, created hundreds of works after the Second World War, including Astro Boy, Kimba the White Lion and Black Jack. Inspired by films and animation, he used cinematic techniques that shaped modern manga and anime. He also founded an animation studio that produced the first Japanese television anime series."],["Famous series","Some of the world’s best-known manga include Dragon Ball by Akira Toriyama, One Piece by Eiichiro Oda, Naruto by Masashi Kishimoto, Sailor Moon by Naoko Takeuchi and Doraemon by Fujiko F. Fujio. One Piece has sold hundreds of millions of copies worldwide, more than any other comic series by a single author."],["Manga in the Philippines","Anime shows such as Voltes V, Dragon Ball and Slam Dunk, broadcast on Philippine television, made Japanese stories hugely popular, and manga followed through bookstores, fan translations and digital apps. Filipino artists have drawn on manga styles in their own comics, and fans gather at conventions dressed as their favorite characters in cosplay."],["Komiks and manga","The Philippines has its own comics tradition, called komiks, which flourished from the 1940s to the 1990s with characters such as Darna, created by Mars Ravelo. Some Filipino artists have worked for American and Japanese publishers, and new generations of Filipino creators mix local stories with influences from manga and Western comics."],["Manhwa, manhua and webtoons","Korea’s comics, called manhwa, and China’s, called manhua, have their own traditions. Korean webtoons, designed to be read by scrolling vertically on phones, have become globally popular, and several have been adapted into television dramas. These forms often share audiences with manga."]],
sp:["Drawing a weekly manga","Creating a weekly manga is demanding work. A manga artist, or mangaka, may produce around 20 pages every week, often for years. The artist plans the story, sketches rough layouts called name, and discusses them with an editor before drawing the final pages. Assistants help with backgrounds, shading and special effects. Deadlines are strict because the magazine prints every week, and some famous artists have described working through the night. Readers’ votes help decide whether a new series continues or ends, so artists feel great pressure to keep their stories exciting."],
g:[["Manga","Japanese comics and graphic novels"],["Mangaka","A manga artist"],["Tankōbon","A collected volume of manga chapters"],["Webtoon","A digital comic designed for vertical scrolling"]],
f:["Manga are read from right to left.","Osamu Tezuka is called the god of manga.","One Piece is one of the best-selling comics ever."],
fun:"The word manga can be translated as whimsical or random pictures."});

ADDART({id:"ceramics",t:"Pottery and Ceramics",c:"Crafts",i:"🏺",w:"Pottery",r:["sculpture","chineseart","archaeology","silk","islamicart"],
p:[["Art from clay","Pottery and ceramics are objects made from clay and hardened by heat."],["One of the oldest crafts","People have made pottery for more than 20,000 years."],["Philippine treasures","The Manunggul Jar is a famous ancient Philippine burial jar."]],
l:"Pottery and ceramics are objects made by shaping clay or similar materials and hardening them with heat in a fire or kiln. They include everyday cooking pots, plates, cups and storage jars, as well as decorative vases, sculptures, tiles and fine porcelain. Pottery is among the oldest human crafts: fragments found in a cave in China are about 20,000 years old. Different cultures developed distinctive styles and techniques, from Chinese porcelain and Japanese tea bowls to Islamic tiles and Greek painted vases. In the Philippines, ancient burial jars such as the Manunggul Jar and the Maitum jars show remarkable artistry, and traditions such as the burnay jars of Vigan continue today.",
s:[["From clay to pot","Clay is a fine earth that becomes soft and shapeable when wet. Potters shape it by hand, by coiling ropes of clay, by pressing it into molds or by throwing it on a spinning potter’s wheel. After drying, the object is fired at high temperatures, which changes the clay permanently into a hard material."],["Kinds of ceramics","Earthenware is fired at lower temperatures and remains slightly porous. Stoneware is fired hotter, making it dense and waterproof. Porcelain, made from a fine white clay called kaolin and fired at very high temperatures, is hard, white and can be thin enough to let light through."],["Glazes and decoration","Many ceramics are coated with a glaze, a glassy layer that makes them waterproof and adds color and shine. Potters decorate their work by painting, carving, stamping, adding colored slips or using special firing methods that create unique effects."],["Kilns","A kiln is an oven for firing pottery. Early pottery was fired in open bonfires or pits, but kilns allowed higher, more controlled temperatures. Chinese potters built long dragon kilns climbing up hillsides, which could fire thousands of pieces at once."]],
k:[["Oldest known pottery","About 20,000 years old, Xianrendong Cave, China"],["Main types","Earthenware, stoneware, porcelain"],["Porcelain clay","Kaolin"],["Philippine National Treasure","Manunggul Jar"],["Vigan tradition","Burnay jars"]],
d:[["c. 18,000 BC","Pottery is made at Xianrendong Cave in China"],["c. 14,000 BC","Jōmon pottery is made in Japan"],["c. 3500 BC","The potter’s wheel is used in Mesopotamia"],["c. 890–710 BC","The Manunggul Jar is made in Palawan"],["1300s","Blue-and-white porcelain is made in Jingdezhen, China"],["1964","The Manunggul Jar is discovered in Tabon Caves"]],
s2:[["Chinese porcelain","Chinese potters perfected porcelain over many centuries, and the city of Jingdezhen became famous for it. Blue-and-white porcelain, decorated with cobalt blue, was traded across Asia, the Middle East and Europe. Porcelain was so closely linked with China that in English it came to be called china."],["The Manunggul Jar","The Manunggul Jar, discovered in 1964 in a cave in the Tabon Caves complex in Palawan, is a burial jar about 2,800 years old. Its lid is topped by a boat carrying two figures, thought to represent a soul being ferried to the afterlife. It is a National Treasure of the Philippines and appears on the old thousand-peso banknote."],["The Maitum jars","In 1991 archaeologists found clay burial jars in a cave in Maitum, Sarangani, shaped like human figures with faces showing different expressions, such as joy, sadness and calm. They are about 2,000 years old and are among the most remarkable artworks of early Philippine history."],["Trade ceramics","For centuries before and during Spanish rule, Chinese, Vietnamese and Thai ceramics were traded in the Philippine islands. Archaeologists have found large amounts of these ceramics in burial sites and shipwrecks, such as the San Diego, a Spanish galleon that sank off Fortune Island, Batangas, in 1600."],["Burnay of Vigan","In Vigan, Ilocos Sur, potters make large earthenware jars called burnay, a tradition said to have been introduced by Chinese settlers. The jars are used to ferment and store basi, a sugarcane wine, and bagoong, fermented fish paste. Visitors can watch potters shaping burnay on wheels turned by a carabao or by foot."],["Ceramics today","Ceramics are used in many modern technologies, including tiles, toilets, spark plugs, electronics, heat shields on spacecraft and artificial joints. At the same time, studio potters around the world, including in the Philippines, create handmade pottery as art, and pottery classes have become popular hobbies."]],
sp:["The boat of souls","Sitting on top of the Manunggul Jar is one of the most moving sculptures of ancient Southeast Asia. A small boat carries two figures, one steering with a paddle and the other sitting with arms crossed over the chest, a position associated with burial. The boat’s prow is carved with a face. Archaeologists think the scene shows a soul traveling to the afterlife, guided by a ferryman, reflecting a belief that the spirits of the dead journeyed across water. The swirling red-painted designs on the jar’s body add to its beauty. Nearly three thousand years old, the jar connects modern Filipinos with the beliefs of their ancestors."],
g:[["Ceramics","Objects made from clay and hardened by heat"],["Kiln","An oven used to fire pottery"],["Glaze","A glassy coating on pottery"],["Porcelain","A hard, white, fine ceramic"]],
f:["Pottery is over 20,000 years old.","Porcelain is called china in English.","The Manunggul Jar is a Philippine National Treasure."],
fun:"In English, fine porcelain is called china because it was first made in China."});

;
/* data/earth6.js */
// Planet Earth, more articles: landslides, sinkholes, glaciers and plateaus.
ADDART({id:"landslides",t:"Landslides",c:"Earth Science",i:"⛰️",w:"Landslide",r:["quake","phtyph","deforestation","soil","disasterprep"],
p:[["Sliding ground","A landslide is the movement of rock, soil and debris down a slope."],["Rain and earthquakes","Heavy rain and earthquakes are the most common triggers."],["A deadly hazard","Landslides kill thousands of people worldwide every year, including in the Philippines."]],
l:"A landslide is the movement of a mass of rock, earth or debris down a slope under the pull of gravity. Landslides range from small falls of loose stones to huge flows that bury whole villages. They happen when the forces pulling material downhill become greater than the strength holding it in place, often after heavy rain soaks the ground, when earthquakes shake slopes, or when people cut into hillsides or remove trees. Landslides are a major hazard in mountainous, rainy and earthquake-prone countries such as the Philippines, where typhoons and monsoon rains frequently trigger them. Warning systems, careful land use, reforestation and evacuation plans can save lives.",
s:[["Why slopes fail","Every slope is held in place by friction and the strength of its soil and rock. Water adds weight and fills the spaces between soil particles, reducing friction and making the ground slippery. Earthquakes shake material loose, and erosion by rivers or waves can remove the support at the base of a slope."],["Kinds of landslides","Rockfalls are rocks dropping from cliffs. Slides occur when a mass of soil or rock moves along a surface. Mudflows and debris flows are fast-moving rivers of mud, rocks and water, often racing down valleys after heavy rain. Creep is very slow downhill movement, shown by tilted trees, fences and poles."],["Human causes","People can make landslides more likely by cutting roads into hillsides, building on steep slopes, mining, quarrying and clearing forests. Tree roots help bind soil, so deforested slopes are often more unstable. Leaking water pipes and poor drainage can also weaken slopes."],["Warning signs","Signs that a landslide may happen include new cracks in the ground or walls, tilting trees and poles, doors and windows that suddenly stick, bulging ground at the base of a slope, muddy water in streams and rumbling sounds. People who notice such signs, especially during heavy rain, should move to safety."]],
k:[["Main force","Gravity"],["Common triggers","Heavy rain, earthquakes"],["Fast, wet landslide","Debris flow or mudflow"],["Slowest movement","Creep"],["Philippine hazard maps","Mines and Geosciences Bureau"]],
d:[["1920","An earthquake triggers huge landslides in Haiyuan, China"],["1970","An earthquake-triggered avalanche buries the town of Yungay in Peru"],["1985","Mudflows from the Nevado del Ruiz volcano destroy Armero, Colombia"],["2006","A landslide buries the village of Guinsaugon in Southern Leyte"],["2018","Typhoon Mangkhut triggers deadly landslides in Itogon, Benguet"],["2024","Severe storm Kristine causes deadly landslides in Batangas and other provinces"]],
s2:[["The Guinsaugon tragedy","On 17 February 2006, after days of heavy rain, a mountainside collapsed above the village of Guinsaugon in Saint Bernard, Southern Leyte. Within minutes, mud and rock buried the village, including its elementary school, which was in session. More than 1,000 people were killed or went missing. The disaster led to greater attention to landslide hazard mapping in the Philippines."],["Typhoons and landslides","In the Philippines, typhoons and monsoon rains bring huge amounts of water in a short time, saturating mountain slopes. In 2018 Typhoon Mangkhut, known locally as Ompong, triggered a landslide that buried miners’ bunkhouses in Itogon, Benguet. In 2024 severe tropical storm Kristine caused landslides that killed many people in Talisay, Batangas, and other areas."],["Volcanic mudflows","Volcanic eruptions leave loose ash and rock on mountain slopes. When heavy rain falls, this material can form lahars, fast-flowing volcanic mudflows. After Mount Pinatubo erupted in 1991, lahars buried towns in Pampanga and Tarlac for years. Lahars from Mayon Volcano, triggered by typhoon rains in 2006, also caused many deaths."],["Hazard maps and warnings","The Mines and Geosciences Bureau and other agencies produce maps showing areas at risk of landslides, floods and other hazards. Local governments use them to plan where people can safely build and when to evacuate. Rain gauges, sensors and community monitoring help give early warnings."],["Making slopes safer","Engineers stabilize slopes with retaining walls, drainage channels, nets and anchors. Planting trees, grasses and bamboo helps bind soil. Avoiding construction on the steepest and most dangerous slopes, and relocating families from high-risk areas, are among the most effective ways to reduce deaths."],["Landslides around the world","Landslides occur on every continent. Earthquakes in China, Peru and Nepal have triggered huge slides, and volcanic mudflows have buried towns in Colombia. Scientists warn that heavier rainfall linked to climate change and the spread of settlements onto steep slopes may increase landslide risks in many places."]],
sp:["Listening to the mountain","In many upland communities in the Philippines, villagers have learned to watch the mountains during heavy rain. Some local governments train residents as landslide watchers, who check cracks in the ground, measure rainfall with simple gauges and listen for unusual sounds. When danger signs appear, they ring bells or use radios to warn neighbors to evacuate to safer places such as schools or chapels on stable ground. Such community-based warning systems, combined with hazard maps and government alerts, have helped save lives during storms."],
g:[["Landslide","The movement of rock, soil or debris down a slope"],["Debris flow","A fast-moving mix of mud, rocks and water"],["Lahar","A volcanic mudflow"],["Creep","Very slow downhill movement of soil"]],
f:["Heavy rain is a common landslide trigger.","Tree roots help hold soil in place.","Lahars are volcanic mudflows."],
fun:"Some landslides move so slowly that they shift only a few centimeters a year, slowly tilting trees and fences."});

ADDART({id:"sinkholes",t:"Sinkholes",c:"Earth Science",i:"🕳️",w:"Sinkhole",r:["caves","rocks","water","quake","geysers"],
p:[["Holes in the ground","A sinkhole is a hole or hollow in the ground caused by the collapse of the surface."],["Limestone lands","Most sinkholes form where water slowly dissolves rock such as limestone."],["Sudden or slow","Some form gradually, while others collapse suddenly."]],
l:"A sinkhole is a depression or hole in the ground formed when the surface layer collapses or sinks into an underground space. Most sinkholes occur in areas of karst, landscapes built on rocks such as limestone, dolomite or gypsum that slowly dissolve in slightly acidic water. Over thousands of years, water seeping underground creates caves and hollows; when their roofs become too thin, the ground above can sag or suddenly collapse. Sinkholes range from small dips to enormous pits hundreds of meters deep. Some are filled with water, forming beautiful pools. Human activities, such as pumping groundwater or leaking pipes, can also cause sinkholes, sometimes swallowing roads, cars and buildings.",
s:[["How sinkholes form","Rainwater absorbs carbon dioxide from the air and soil, becoming a weak acid. As it seeps through cracks in limestone, it slowly dissolves the rock, widening cracks into channels and caves. When the rock or soil above an empty space can no longer support its own weight, it collapses, creating a sinkhole."],["Kinds of sinkholes","Solution sinkholes form slowly as rock at the surface dissolves, creating bowl-shaped hollows. Cover-subsidence sinkholes form when sand gradually trickles into openings below. Cover-collapse sinkholes happen suddenly when a hidden cavity’s roof gives way, and they are the most dangerous."],["Karst landscapes","Karst areas often have caves, underground rivers, springs and few surface streams, because water drains underground. Famous karst landscapes include the towering limestone hills of Guilin in China, Halong Bay in Vietnam and the Chocolate Hills of Bohol in the Philippines."],["Human-caused sinkholes","Pumping large amounts of groundwater, mining, construction and broken water or sewer pipes can wash away soil and create sinkholes, even in places without limestone. Heavy rains from storms can trigger collapses in areas already weakened."]],
k:[["Common rock","Limestone"],["Landscape type","Karst"],["Cause","Dissolving rock or underground erosion"],["Deepest known sinkhole","Xiaozhai Tiankeng, China, about 660 m"],["Famous water-filled sinkhole","Great Blue Hole, Belize"]],
d:[["1971","Jacques Cousteau explores the Great Blue Hole of Belize"],["1981","A large sinkhole swallows homes and cars in Winter Park, Florida"],["1994","Explorers survey the giant Xiaozhai Tiankeng in China"],["2010","A sinkhole opens in Guatemala City after a tropical storm"],["2013","The Bohol earthquake opens many new sinkholes in the province"],["2022","Scientists explore a newly found giant sinkhole with a forest in Guangxi, China"]],
s2:[["Sinkholes in the Philippines","Large parts of the Philippines, including Bohol, Palawan, Cebu and Samar, are built on limestone. When a magnitude 7.2 earthquake struck Bohol in October 2013, it opened many new sinkholes, damaged roads and farmland and changed the landscape. Geologists mapped the sinkholes to help communities avoid dangerous areas."],["Underground rivers","Water that drains into karst can form long underground rivers. The Puerto Princesa Subterranean River in Palawan flows for about 8 kilometers through a limestone cave before reaching the sea. It is a UNESCO World Heritage Site and one of the most famous karst features in Asia."],["Giant sinkholes","In southern China, huge sinkholes called tiankeng, meaning heavenly pits, are so large that forests grow on their floors, with plants and animals isolated from the world above. The Xiaozhai Tiankeng is about 660 meters deep. Explorers continue to discover new giant sinkholes in the region."],["Blue holes and cenotes","Some sinkholes near the coast are filled with water. Blue holes, such as the Great Blue Hole off Belize, appear as dark blue circles in the sea. In Mexico’s Yucatán Peninsula, water-filled sinkholes called cenotes were sacred to the ancient Maya and are now popular for swimming and diving."],["Dangers and detection","Sudden sinkholes can damage roads, houses and pipelines and, rarely, injure people. Geologists use ground-penetrating radar, surveys and satellite measurements to find areas at risk. Signs include cracks in walls and floors, sagging ground, and pools of water forming where none existed before."],["The Dead Sea sinkholes","Around the shores of the Dead Sea, between Israel, Jordan and the West Bank, thousands of sinkholes have appeared since the 1980s. As the sea’s level drops because water is diverted from the Jordan River, fresh groundwater dissolves underground salt layers, causing the ground to collapse. The sinkholes have damaged roads, farms and resorts."]],
sp:["The forest at the bottom of the pit","In 2022 a team of Chinese cave explorers lowered themselves by rope into a newly found sinkhole in Guangxi, southern China. About 192 meters below the surface, they found a hidden forest of ancient trees, some reaching about 40 meters tall, growing toward the circle of sky far above. Thick undergrowth covered the floor. Scientists believe such isolated forests may hold plant and animal species that have never been described. The region has dozens of these giant sinkholes, and explorers continue to discover new ones, each a small lost world."],
g:[["Sinkhole","A hole formed when the ground collapses into an underground space"],["Karst","A landscape formed by dissolving rocks such as limestone"],["Limestone","A rock made mostly of calcium carbonate"],["Cenote","A natural water-filled sinkhole in Mexico"]],
f:["Most sinkholes form in limestone areas.","China has the deepest known sinkhole.","The Bohol earthquake of 2013 opened new sinkholes."],
fun:"Some giant sinkholes in China are so large that whole forests grow on their floors."});

ADDART({id:"glaciers",t:"Glaciers",c:"Earth Science",i:"🧊",w:"Glacier",r:["iceage","climchange","antarc","alps","himal","snowballearth"],
p:[["Rivers of ice","Glaciers are huge masses of ice that slowly flow over land."],["Shaping the land","Glaciers carve valleys, lakes and mountain peaks."],["Melting fast","Most glaciers are shrinking as the climate warms."]],
l:"A glacier is a large, long-lasting mass of ice that forms on land from compacted snow and moves slowly under its own weight. Glaciers form where more snow falls in winter than melts in summer, building up layers that are squeezed into dense ice over many years. They are found in high mountains on every continent except Australia, and in the vast ice sheets covering Antarctica and Greenland. Glaciers hold about two-thirds of the world’s fresh water. As they move, they carve deep valleys, sharpen mountain peaks and leave behind lakes and piles of rock. Today most glaciers are shrinking rapidly because of rising temperatures, affecting water supplies and sea levels.",
s:[["How glaciers form","In cold places, snow that does not melt piles up year after year. The weight of new snow presses the older snow into grainy ice called firn, and over decades into solid glacier ice. Air is squeezed out, making old glacier ice look blue, because dense ice absorbs red light and lets blue light pass."],["How glaciers move","Glaciers flow like very thick, slow rivers. Ice deep inside deforms under pressure, and meltwater at the base can help the glacier slide over the rock below. Most glaciers move a few centimeters to a few meters a day, but some fast glaciers in Greenland can move more than 30 meters a day."],["Kinds of glaciers","Mountain or valley glaciers flow down valleys from high peaks. Ice sheets are enormous glaciers covering huge areas of land, found today only in Antarctica and Greenland. Ice caps are smaller dome-shaped glaciers, and tidewater glaciers flow into the sea, where chunks break off as icebergs."],["Sculpting the land","As glaciers move, they scrape and pluck rock, carving U-shaped valleys, sharp ridges and pointed peaks such as the Matterhorn in the Alps. When glaciers melt, they leave behind piles of rock called moraines and basins that fill with water to form lakes. Norway’s fjords are valleys carved by glaciers and flooded by the sea."]],
k:[["Share of world’s fresh water","About two-thirds"],["Ice sheets today","Antarctica and Greenland"],["Old snow turning to ice","Firn"],["Rock piles left behind","Moraines"],["UN year","2025, International Year of Glaciers’ Preservation"]],
d:[["c. 20,000 years ago","Ice sheets reach their greatest extent in the last ice age"],["1837","Louis Agassiz proposes that great ice sheets once covered Europe"],["1991","Ötzi the Iceman is found in a melting Alpine glacier"],["2019","Iceland holds a funeral for the lost Okjökull glacier"],["2024","Venezuela is reported to have lost its last glacier"],["2025","A glacier collapse buries most of the Swiss village of Blatten"]],
s2:[["Glaciers and water supplies","In dry seasons, meltwater from glaciers feeds rivers that supply drinking water, farms and hydroelectric dams. Glaciers of the Himalayas and Tibetan Plateau help feed rivers such as the Ganges, Indus and Yangtze, on which more than a billion people depend. Shrinking glaciers threaten these supplies in the long term."],["Melting glaciers","Scientists measure glaciers using satellites, photographs and field visits. Most glaciers around the world have been losing ice for decades, and the rate has increased. Melting glaciers and ice sheets are a major cause of sea level rise. The United Nations declared 2025 the International Year of Glaciers’ Preservation to draw attention to their loss."],["Lost glaciers","Some small glaciers have already disappeared. In 2019 people in Iceland held a memorial ceremony for Okjökull, which had melted so much that it was no longer a glacier, and placed a plaque addressed to the future. Venezuela was reported in 2024 to have lost its last glacier, and tropical glaciers on Mount Kilimanjaro and in Indonesia’s Papua are also shrinking quickly."],["Glacier hazards","Glaciers can cause disasters. Lakes dammed by glacial ice or rock may burst, sending floods down valleys. Chunks of glacier can break off and fall. In May 2025, part of the Birch Glacier in Switzerland collapsed, burying most of the village of Blatten, which had been evacuated days earlier after scientists noticed warning signs."],["Secrets in the ice","Glaciers preserve objects and information from the past. In 1991 hikers found Ötzi, a man who died in the Alps about 5,300 years ago, preserved in glacier ice with his clothes and tools. Ice cores drilled from Antarctica and Greenland contain air bubbles that reveal Earth’s climate over hundreds of thousands of years."],["Glaciers near the equator","Glaciers can exist even in the tropics on very high mountains, where the air is cold. Mount Kilimanjaro in Tanzania, the Andes of Peru and Ecuador and the peaks of Puncak Jaya in Indonesian Papua have had tropical glaciers. The Philippines has no glaciers, because its mountains are not high enough, but the country is affected by the sea level rise that melting ice causes."]],
sp:["A letter to the future","In August 2019, a group of scientists, officials and local people climbed the volcano Ok in western Iceland to mark the loss of Okjökull, once a glacier covering about 16 square kilometers. By 2014 it had shrunk so much that it no longer moved and was declared dead. They installed a bronze plaque titled A letter to the future, written by the Icelandic author Andri Snær Magnason. It says that Ok was the first Icelandic glacier to lose its status as a glacier and that in the next 200 years all of the country’s glaciers are expected to follow, ending with the level of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere at the time."],
g:[["Glacier","A large mass of ice that slowly flows over land"],["Ice sheet","A huge glacier covering a large area of land"],["Moraine","A pile of rock and soil left by a glacier"],["Fjord","A long, narrow sea inlet carved by a glacier"]],
f:["Glaciers hold about two-thirds of the world’s fresh water.","Old glacier ice looks blue.","Most glaciers are shrinking."],
fun:"Glacier ice can look bright blue because dense ice absorbs red light and lets blue light pass through."});

ADDART({id:"plateaus",t:"Plateaus",c:"Geography",i:"🏞️",w:"Plateau",r:["himal","andes","tect","canyon","apo"],
p:[["High flat lands","A plateau is a large, high area of fairly flat land."],["Roof of the World","The Tibetan Plateau is the highest and largest plateau on Earth."],["Philippine plateaus","Bukidnon and Lanao in Mindanao have high plateaus."]],
l:"A plateau is a large area of high, relatively flat land that rises sharply above the surrounding area on at least one side. Plateaus are sometimes called tablelands. They form in several ways: when forces inside the Earth push up large blocks of land, when layers of lava spread out and harden, or when erosion wears away surrounding land. Plateaus cover a large part of Earth’s land surface and include some of its most dramatic landscapes, such as the Tibetan Plateau, called the Roof of the World, the Colorado Plateau with the Grand Canyon, and the Deccan Plateau of India. In the Philippines, the Bukidnon and Lanao plateaus of Mindanao are important farming regions.",
s:[["How plateaus form","Some plateaus are raised when tectonic plates collide, pushing up broad areas of land, as happened with the Tibetan Plateau. Volcanic plateaus form when huge amounts of runny lava pour out and spread over wide areas, as on the Deccan Plateau. Others are left standing when rivers and weather erode lower land around them."],["The Tibetan Plateau","The Tibetan Plateau, in western China and nearby regions, has an average height of about 4,500 meters and covers about 2.5 million square kilometers. It was raised by the collision of the Indian and Eurasian plates. Its glaciers and snowfields feed many of Asia’s great rivers, including the Yangtze, Yellow, Mekong, Indus and Brahmaputra."],["The Colorado Plateau","The Colorado Plateau in the southwestern United States is famous for its colorful layered rocks, cut into deep canyons by rivers. The Colorado River carved the Grand Canyon through it, revealing nearly two billion years of Earth’s history in its walls."],["Life on plateaus","High plateaus have thin air, strong sunlight and large temperature changes between day and night. People who have lived for generations on high plateaus, such as Tibetans and some peoples of the Andes and Ethiopia, have bodies adapted to the low oxygen. Plateaus are used for grazing animals, farming and, in some places, mining."]],
k:[["Also called","Tableland"],["Highest and largest","Tibetan Plateau"],["Average height of Tibetan Plateau","About 4,500 m"],["Volcanic plateau","Deccan Plateau, India"],["Philippine plateaus","Bukidnon and Lanao, Mindanao"]],
d:[["c. 66 million years ago","Vast lava flows build the Deccan Plateau"],["c. 50 million years ago","India begins colliding with Asia, raising the Tibetan Plateau"],["1869","John Wesley Powell leads an expedition through the Grand Canyon"],["1919","Grand Canyon National Park is established"],["2000","The Mount Kitanglad Range in Bukidnon is protected by law as a natural park"],["2006","A railway across the Tibetan Plateau to Lhasa opens"]],
s2:[["Plateaus of the Philippines","In northern Mindanao, the Bukidnon Plateau rises between mountain ranges, with a cool climate suitable for growing pineapples, corn, sugarcane, coffee and vegetables, and for raising cattle. To the west, the Lanao Plateau holds Lake Lanao, the country’s second-largest lake. Smaller upland plateaus and tablelands are found in Luzon and other islands."],["Bukidnon’s farms","Large pineapple and banana plantations, as well as small farms, cover parts of the Bukidnon Plateau. Its cooler temperatures and fertile volcanic soils make it one of the country’s food baskets. Indigenous peoples of Bukidnon, such as the Higaonon, Talaandig and Bukidnon, have lived in the region’s highlands and mountains for generations."],["Volcanic plateaus","Lava plateaus form when lava erupts from long cracks rather than cone-shaped volcanoes, spreading in layers over huge areas. The Deccan Traps in India formed about 66 million years ago, around the time the dinosaurs died out, and some scientists think the eruptions contributed to that mass extinction. The Columbia Plateau in the United States formed in a similar way."],["Tepuis","In Venezuela, Guyana and Brazil, flat-topped mountains called tepuis rise steeply from the rainforest. They are remnants of an ancient plateau worn away over millions of years. Their isolated tops have unique plants and animals and inspired Arthur Conan Doyle’s adventure novel The Lost World. Angel Falls, the world’s highest waterfall, plunges from a tepui."],["The Altiplano","The Altiplano, meaning high plain in Spanish, lies in the Andes of Peru, Bolivia, Chile and Argentina at an average height of over 3,500 meters. It holds Lake Titicaca and the Salar de Uyuni, the world’s largest salt flat. Indigenous peoples such as the Aymara and Quechua have farmed and herded llamas and alpacas there for thousands of years."],["Plateaus and climate","Large plateaus influence weather over wide regions. The Tibetan Plateau heats up in summer, helping to drive the South Asian monsoon, which brings vital rain to India and neighboring countries. Changes on the plateau, such as melting glaciers and thawing permafrost, are closely studied by climate scientists."]],
sp:["The railway to the roof of the world","In 2006 China opened the Qinghai–Tibet Railway, linking the city of Xining to Lhasa, the capital of Tibet. More than 960 kilometers of the line lie above 4,000 meters, and its highest point, the Tanggula Pass, is about 5,070 meters above sea level, making it the highest railway in the world. Engineers had to build over frozen ground called permafrost, using cooling pipes to keep it from thawing and shifting the tracks. Passenger carriages supply extra oxygen to help travelers cope with the thin air. The railway has boosted tourism and trade, while critics have raised concerns about its effects on Tibetan culture and the fragile environment."],
g:[["Plateau","A large area of high, fairly flat land"],["Tableland","Another name for a plateau"],["Tepui","A flat-topped mountain in South America"],["Permafrost","Ground that stays frozen all year"]],
f:["The Tibetan Plateau is called the Roof of the World.","The Grand Canyon cuts through the Colorado Plateau.","Lake Lanao lies on the Lanao Plateau."],
fun:"Angel Falls, the world’s highest waterfall, plunges from the top of a flat-topped mountain called a tepui."});

;
/* data/science9.js */
// Science and Numbers, more articles: quarks, polymers and germ theory.
ADDART({id:"quarks",t:"Quarks",c:"Physics",i:"🔴",w:"Quark",r:["particles","standardmodel","atom","higgs","accelerators"],
p:[["Inside protons","Quarks are tiny particles that make up protons and neutrons."],["Six kinds","There are six types, or flavors, of quark."],["Never alone","Quarks are always found bound together, never on their own."]],
l:"Quarks are elementary particles, among the smallest known building blocks of matter. They combine to form protons and neutrons, which make up the nuclei of atoms. A proton contains two up quarks and one down quark, while a neutron contains one up quark and two down quarks. There are six types, or flavors, of quark: up, down, strange, charm, bottom and top. Quarks are held together by the strong nuclear force, carried by particles called gluons, and are never found alone in nature. The idea of quarks was proposed in 1964 by Murray Gell-Mann and George Zweig, and experiments soon confirmed that protons have smaller particles inside. Quarks are a central part of the Standard Model of particle physics.",
s:[["Building protons and neutrons","Every atom has a nucleus made of protons and neutrons, and each of these is made of three quarks. Up quarks carry an electric charge of plus two-thirds and down quarks minus one-third, so two ups and a down give the proton a charge of plus one, while one up and two downs give the neutron zero charge."],["Six flavors","Up and down quarks are the lightest and make up ordinary matter. The other four, strange, charm, bottom and top, are heavier and unstable, quickly decaying into lighter particles. They are created in high-energy collisions in particle accelerators and in cosmic rays. The top quark, discovered in 1995, is the heaviest known elementary particle."],["Color charge and gluons","Quarks carry a property called color charge, which has nothing to do with ordinary color but comes in three types named red, green and blue. The strong force between colored quarks is carried by gluons, which act like a powerful glue. The theory describing this is called quantum chromodynamics."],["Confinement","The strong force behaves strangely: the farther apart two quarks are pulled, the stronger the force between them becomes. If enough energy is used to separate them, it creates new quarks, which pair up with the old ones. This is why quarks are always confined inside particles and have never been seen alone."]],
k:[["Proposed","1964"],["Proposed by","Murray Gell-Mann and George Zweig"],["Flavors","Up, down, strange, charm, bottom, top"],["Force carriers","Gluons"],["Heaviest quark","Top, discovered 1995"]],
d:[["1964","Gell-Mann and Zweig propose quarks"],["1968","Experiments at Stanford reveal point-like particles inside protons"],["1969","Murray Gell-Mann receives the Nobel Prize in Physics"],["1974","The charm quark is discovered"],["1977","The bottom quark is discovered"],["1995","The top quark is discovered at Fermilab"]],
s2:[["A name from a novel","Murray Gell-Mann took the word quark from a line in James Joyce’s novel Finnegans Wake: Three quarks for Muster Mark! He liked the sound of the word and the fact that it involved the number three, matching the three quarks inside a proton. George Zweig, who developed the idea independently, called them aces, but quark was the name that stuck."],["The particle zoo","In the 1950s and 1960s, particle accelerators produced dozens of new particles, a confusing collection physicists called the particle zoo. Gell-Mann and others noticed patterns among them, like a periodic table for particles. The quark model explained these patterns by showing that many particles were combinations of a few kinds of quarks."],["Seeing inside the proton","In 1968 experiments at the Stanford Linear Accelerator Center fired electrons at protons. Some electrons bounced back sharply, as if hitting small, hard objects inside, much as Rutherford had discovered the atomic nucleus decades earlier. These results provided strong evidence for quarks, and the leaders of the experiment received the 1990 Nobel Prize in Physics."],["Where mass comes from","Surprisingly, the quarks in a proton account for only about one percent of its mass. Most of the proton’s mass comes from the energy of the quarks’ motion and the gluons binding them, following Einstein’s famous equation linking energy and mass. In this sense, most of the mass of everyday objects comes from energy trapped inside protons and neutrons."],["Quark-gluon plasma","Just after the Big Bang, the universe was so hot that quarks and gluons moved freely in a soup called quark-gluon plasma. Scientists have recreated tiny drops of this plasma by smashing heavy atoms together at enormous energies at laboratories in the United States and at CERN. They found it behaves like an almost perfect liquid."],["Exotic combinations","Most particles made of quarks contain either three quarks or a quark and an antiquark. In the 2000s and 2010s, experiments found exotic particles with four or five quarks, called tetraquarks and pentaquarks. These discoveries help physicists test their understanding of the strong force."]],
sp:["Three quarks for Muster Mark","Physicists are not always serious when naming things. Murray Gell-Mann, a brilliant physicist with a love of languages, first imagined the sound of the word kwork for his new particles before finding the word quark in James Joyce’s playful, puzzling novel Finnegans Wake, in which a flock of seagulls cries three quarks for Muster Mark. Later physicists continued the playful tradition, naming quark flavors strange and charm and calling their color charges red, green and blue. For a time, the bottom and top quarks were also nicknamed beauty and truth."],
g:[["Quark","An elementary particle that makes up protons and neutrons"],["Flavor","A type of quark, such as up or down"],["Gluon","A particle that carries the strong force between quarks"],["Confinement","The fact that quarks are never found alone"]],
f:["A proton contains three quarks.","There are six flavors of quark.","The word quark comes from Finnegans Wake."],
fun:"The quarks in a proton make up only about one percent of its mass; the rest comes from the energy that binds them."});

ADDART({id:"polymers",t:"Polymers",c:"Chemistry",i:"🧶",w:"Polymer",r:["plastics","molecules","dna","carbonchem","reactions"],
p:[["Long chains","Polymers are huge molecules made of many small units linked in long chains."],["Natural and synthetic","They include natural materials such as silk and rubber, and synthetic ones such as nylon and plastics."],["Everywhere","Polymers are found in our bodies, our clothes, our homes and our technology."]],
l:"A polymer is a very large molecule made of many smaller repeating units, called monomers, joined together in long chains or networks, like beads on a necklace. Polymers occur naturally in living things: proteins, DNA, starch, cellulose, silk, wool and natural rubber are all polymers. Since the early 1900s chemists have also made synthetic polymers, including plastics such as polyethylene and PVC, fibers such as nylon and polyester, and materials such as synthetic rubber, Kevlar and silicone. Polymers can be soft or hard, stretchy or rigid, and are used in clothing, packaging, medicine, construction, electronics and vehicles. Their durability is useful but also causes problems, as plastic waste builds up in the environment.",
s:[["Monomers and chains","Polymers are made when small molecules, the monomers, link together in a chemical reaction called polymerization. Ethylene molecules join to form polyethylene, for example. A single polymer molecule may contain thousands or even millions of monomers. The length and arrangement of the chains help determine how a material behaves."],["Natural polymers","Life depends on polymers. Proteins are chains of amino acids, DNA is a chain of nucleotides carrying genetic information, and starch and cellulose are chains of sugar molecules. Cellulose makes up the cell walls of plants and is the main material of wood, paper and cotton. Abaca fiber from the Philippines is rich in cellulose."],["Plastics","Plastics are synthetic polymers that can be molded into shapes. Thermoplastics, such as polyethylene and PET used in bottles, soften when heated and can be reshaped and often recycled. Thermosets, such as epoxy resins, set permanently when heated and cannot be melted again."],["Fibers and rubbers","Synthetic fibers such as nylon and polyester are used in clothing, ropes and fishing nets. Elastomers, or rubbers, have chains that can stretch and spring back. Natural rubber comes from the sap of rubber trees, grown in parts of Mindanao, while synthetic rubbers are made from petroleum."]],
k:[["Building blocks","Monomers"],["Chain-forming reaction","Polymerization"],["Natural examples","Proteins, DNA, cellulose, silk, rubber"],["Synthetic examples","Nylon, polyethylene, PVC, polyester"],["Polymer pioneer","Hermann Staudinger, Nobel Prize 1953"]],
d:[["1839","Charles Goodyear discovers how to make rubber stronger by heating it with sulfur"],["1907","Leo Baekeland invents Bakelite, the first fully synthetic plastic"],["1920","Hermann Staudinger proposes that polymers are long chain molecules"],["1935","Wallace Carothers’s team at DuPont makes nylon"],["1965","Stephanie Kwolek invents Kevlar"],["2022","United Nations members agree to negotiate a treaty on plastic pollution"]],
s2:[["Staudinger’s big idea","In the early 1900s, many chemists believed that substances such as rubber and cellulose were clusters of small molecules held loosely together. In 1920 the German chemist Hermann Staudinger argued that they were actually giant molecules made of long chains held by strong chemical bonds. His idea was at first ridiculed but was later proved correct, and he received the Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 1953."],["Nylon","In 1935 a team led by the American chemist Wallace Carothers at the DuPont company created nylon, a strong, silky synthetic fiber. Nylon stockings went on sale in 1939 and 1940 and were hugely popular. During the Second World War nylon was used for parachutes, ropes and tents. It is still used in clothing, toothbrushes and many other products."],["Kevlar","In 1965 the chemist Stephanie Kwolek, working at DuPont, discovered a polymer solution that could be spun into fibers five times stronger than steel by weight. Called Kevlar, it is used in bulletproof vests, helmets, ropes, tires and sports equipment. Kwolek became one of the few women inducted into the National Inventors Hall of Fame in the United States."],["Polymers in medicine","Polymers are used in contact lenses, artificial joints, heart valves, dissolving stitches, medicine capsules and blood bags. Some special polymers release medicines slowly inside the body, while others form scaffolds on which new tissue can grow."],["The plastic problem","Because most synthetic polymers do not break down easily, plastic waste can last for hundreds of years. Much of it ends up in landfills, rivers and oceans, harming wildlife and breaking into tiny pieces called microplastics. The Philippines is among the countries where large amounts of plastic waste reach the sea. Solutions include reducing single-use plastics, better recycling and new biodegradable materials."],["Polymers from plants","Scientists are developing polymers made from plants rather than petroleum, such as polylactic acid from corn or sugarcane, and materials made from seaweed, cassava and agricultural waste. Some are biodegradable under the right conditions. Researchers in the Philippines have explored materials from local sources such as abaca, coconut and seaweed."]],
sp:["The stockings that caused riots","When nylon stockings went on sale across the United States in May 1940, women lined up outside stores and millions of pairs sold within days. During the Second World War, nylon was needed for parachutes and other military uses, and stockings became scarce. Some women painted lines on the backs of their legs to look as if they were wearing stockings. When nylon stockings returned to stores after the war, huge crowds gathered, and scuffles broke out in several cities in what newspapers called the nylon riots. A new polymer had become a symbol of modern life."],
g:[["Polymer","A very large molecule made of many repeating units"],["Monomer","A small molecule that joins with others to form a polymer"],["Polymerization","The chemical process of joining monomers into polymers"],["Microplastics","Tiny pieces of plastic less than 5 millimeters long"]],
f:["DNA and proteins are natural polymers.","Nylon was invented in 1935.","Kevlar is stronger than steel by weight."],
fun:"A single long polymer molecule can contain millions of atoms linked in a chain."});

ADDART({id:"germtheory",t:"Germ Theory of Disease",c:"History of Science",i:"🔬",w:"Germ theory of disease",r:["pasteur","vacc","antibiotics","hygiene","sanitation"],
p:[["Germs cause disease","Germ theory states that many diseases are caused by tiny living things called microorganisms."],["A medical revolution","It replaced older ideas, such as bad air, as the explanation for disease."],["Saving lives","Germ theory led to handwashing, sterilization, vaccines and clean water."]],
l:"The germ theory of disease is the scientific understanding that many diseases are caused by microorganisms, or germs, such as bacteria, viruses, fungi and parasites, which invade the body and multiply. Before germ theory was accepted in the late 1800s, many people believed that diseases were caused by miasma, poisonous bad air from rotting matter, or by imbalances in the body. The work of scientists such as Ignaz Semmelweis, John Snow, Louis Pasteur, Joseph Lister and Robert Koch showed how germs spread illness and how they could be stopped. Germ theory transformed medicine and public health, leading to antiseptic surgery, sterilization, vaccines, clean water systems and antibiotics, and saving countless lives.",
s:[["Before germ theory","For centuries, many doctors followed the theory of the four humors, believing illness came from imbalances of bodily fluids. Others blamed miasma, foul-smelling air. Although these ideas were wrong, the miasma theory sometimes encouraged useful steps, such as cleaning streets and draining swamps."],["Seeing microbes","In the 1670s the Dutch scientist Antonie van Leeuwenhoek used tiny handmade microscopes to see living creatures in pond water and scrapings from his teeth, which he called animalcules. He was among the first people to see bacteria, but no one yet connected them with disease."],["Semmelweis and handwashing","In 1847 the Hungarian doctor Ignaz Semmelweis noticed that women giving birth in a Vienna hospital ward run by doctors died of fever far more often than in a ward run by midwives. Doctors often came straight from examining dead bodies. When he required them to wash their hands in a chlorine solution, deaths dropped sharply. Many doctors rejected his ideas at the time."],["John Snow and cholera","During a cholera outbreak in London in 1854, the doctor John Snow mapped the cases and found they clustered around a water pump on Broad Street. He persuaded officials to remove the pump’s handle, and the outbreak declined. His work showed that cholera spread through contaminated water, not bad air."]],
k:[["Main idea","Microorganisms cause many diseases"],["Older theory replaced","Miasma, or bad air"],["Key scientists","Pasteur, Koch, Lister, Semmelweis, Snow"],["Accepted","Late 1800s"],["Led to","Antiseptics, vaccines, clean water, antibiotics"]],
d:[["1546","Girolamo Fracastoro suggests that diseases spread by tiny seeds"],["1676","Antonie van Leeuwenhoek describes microscopic organisms"],["1847","Ignaz Semmelweis introduces handwashing in a Vienna hospital"],["1854","John Snow traces a London cholera outbreak to a water pump"],["1867","Joseph Lister publishes his work on antiseptic surgery"],["1882","Robert Koch identifies the bacterium that causes tuberculosis"]],
s2:[["Pasteur’s experiments","In the 1860s the French chemist Louis Pasteur showed that microorganisms cause fermentation and spoiling. Using flasks with long, curved necks that let in air but trapped dust, he showed that broth stayed fresh unless germs reached it, disproving the idea that life arises spontaneously. He developed pasteurization, heating liquids to kill germs, and later vaccines against anthrax and rabies."],["Lister’s antiseptic surgery","Inspired by Pasteur, the British surgeon Joseph Lister began in 1865 to clean wounds, instruments and dressings with carbolic acid. Infections and deaths after surgery fell dramatically. His methods led to modern sterile surgery, in which instruments are sterilized and surgeons wear gloves and masks."],["Koch’s postulates","The German doctor Robert Koch proved that specific bacteria cause specific diseases. He identified the germs behind anthrax in 1876, tuberculosis in 1882 and cholera in 1883. He developed a set of rules, known as Koch’s postulates, for proving that a particular microbe causes a particular disease. He received the Nobel Prize in 1905."],["Public health revolution","Germ theory changed how cities were built and run. Governments built sewer systems and water treatment plants, inspected food, and promoted handwashing and vaccination. Deaths from diseases such as cholera, typhoid and tuberculosis fell sharply in many countries in the late 1800s and early 1900s."],["Germ theory in the Philippines","In the late 1800s and early 1900s, cholera, smallpox and other epidemics struck the Philippines. Public health campaigns introduced vaccination, water purification, quarantine and sanitation in Manila and other towns. Filipino doctors and scientists trained in modern medicine, and the University of the Philippines College of Medicine and the Bureau of Science were founded in the early 1900s."],["Beyond germs","Germ theory explains infectious diseases, but not all illnesses. Many diseases, such as heart disease, diabetes and most cancers, are caused by a mix of genes, lifestyle and environment. Scientists now also know that many microbes living in and on the body are helpful, forming the microbiome that aids digestion and protects against harmful germs."]],
sp:["The pump on Broad Street","In late August 1854, cholera swept through the Soho district of London, killing hundreds of people in a few days. Most experts blamed foul air. Dr. John Snow suspected the water. He went door to door, recording where victims lived, and marked each death on a map. The marks clustered around a public water pump on Broad Street. Interestingly, workers at a nearby brewery, who drank beer instead of water, mostly escaped the disease. Snow presented his evidence to local officials, who removed the pump’s handle. His map is now famous as one of the founding works of epidemiology, the study of how diseases spread."],
g:[["Germ","A microorganism that can cause disease"],["Miasma","An old belief that disease was caused by bad air"],["Antiseptic","A substance that kills germs on skin or wounds"],["Epidemiology","The study of how diseases spread in populations"]],
f:["Germ theory replaced the idea of bad air.","Semmelweis promoted handwashing in 1847.","John Snow traced cholera to a water pump."],
fun:"During the 1854 London cholera outbreak, workers at a brewery near the infected pump mostly escaped because they drank beer instead of water."});

