/* data/modern7.js */
// The Modern World, part 6: smart cities, overtourism, financial literacy, deepfakes, online scams and sea level rise.
ADDART({id:"smartcities",t:"Smart Cities",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🏙️",w:"Smart city",r:["megacities","smarthome","dataprivacy","powergrid","worldpop"],
p:[["Cities using data","A smart city uses digital technology and data to run services more efficiently."],["Sensors everywhere","Sensors, cameras and apps help manage traffic, energy, water and safety."],["Promise and concern","Smart cities promise convenience but raise questions about privacy and fairness."]],
l:"A smart city is an urban area that uses digital technology, sensors and data to improve public services and the quality of life of its residents. Smart city projects may use connected traffic lights to ease congestion, sensors to detect water leaks and air pollution, apps for paying fares and taxes, smart meters for electricity, and data platforms to help governments plan. Cities such as Singapore, Seoul, Barcelona and Amsterdam are often cited as leaders, and new planned cities, including New Clark City in the Philippines, are being designed with smart technology in mind. Supporters say smart cities can be cleaner, safer and more efficient, while critics warn about surveillance, data privacy, high costs and leaving poorer residents behind.",
s:[["What makes a city smart","Smart cities connect devices and systems through networks, often called the Internet of Things. Data from sensors, cameras, phones and payment systems are collected and analyzed to help officials and residents make decisions, from adjusting traffic signals to sending repair crews where they are needed."],["Smarter transport","Cities use real-time data to manage traffic lights, guide drivers to parking spaces, show when buses and trains will arrive and allow cashless fares. Some cities charge drivers to enter crowded areas at busy times, using cameras to read license plates, which reduces congestion."],["Energy and environment","Smart grids, efficient street lighting that dims when no one is around, and sensors that monitor air quality, noise and flooding help cities save energy and respond to environmental problems. Smart waste bins can signal when they are full, so collection trucks avoid unnecessary trips."],["Digital government","Many cities let residents apply for permits, pay taxes, report problems such as potholes and access public information online. Open data portals share information on budgets, transport and services, which supporters say improves transparency."]],
k:[["Key technology","Sensors and data networks"],["Often-cited leaders","Singapore, Seoul, Barcelona"],["Philippine planned city","New Clark City, Tarlac"],["Main benefits","Efficiency, convenience, sustainability"],["Main concerns","Privacy, cost, inequality"]],
d:[["2008","Technology companies begin promoting the idea of smarter cities"],["2014","Singapore launches its Smart Nation initiative"],["2015","India launches its Smart Cities Mission"],["2018","ASEAN launches the ASEAN Smart Cities Network"],["2020","A high-profile smart neighborhood plan in Toronto is canceled"],["2020s","Philippine cities expand digital services and traffic monitoring"]],
s2:[["Singapore’s Smart Nation","Singapore launched its Smart Nation initiative in 2014, using technology for transport, health care, government services and digital payments. It has sensors to monitor traffic and water, a national digital identity system and widespread cashless payments. Supporters see it as a model, while some critics raise concerns about government surveillance."],["Smart cities in the Philippines","Several Philippine cities have introduced digital services, traffic command centers, closed-circuit cameras and online payment systems. New Clark City in Tarlac, developed by the government, is planned as a green and smart city with resilient infrastructure. Cities such as Manila, Cebu and Davao have joined the ASEAN Smart Cities Network."],["Privacy and surveillance","Smart city systems collect large amounts of data about people’s movements and activities. Critics worry that cameras, facial recognition and tracking could be used for surveillance or that data could be misused or hacked. In 2020 a project by a technology company to build a smart neighborhood in Toronto was canceled after public concerns about privacy and cost."],["Who benefits?","Some critics argue that smart city projects focus on gadgets rather than basic needs such as affordable housing, clean water and public transport, and may benefit wealthier areas more than poorer ones. Supporters reply that technology can help deliver services more efficiently to everyone if planned inclusively."],["Resilient cities","For cities facing typhoons, floods and earthquakes, smart technology can provide early warnings, monitor rivers and drainage, and coordinate emergency response. In the Philippines, flood sensors, hazard maps and emergency apps help local governments prepare for disasters."],["People at the center","Many urban experts now say a truly smart city is not about technology alone but about people. They emphasize involving residents in decisions, protecting rights, designing walkable neighborhoods, providing green spaces and using technology only where it solves real problems."]],
sp:["A city built from scratch","On former farmland near Clark in Tarlac, the Philippine government has been developing New Clark City, a planned city meant to ease pressure on crowded Metro Manila. Its first major facilities were a sports complex, including an athletics stadium and aquatics center, built for the 2019 Southeast Asian Games. Planners describe a city with green spaces, flood-resilient design, renewable energy, efficient public transport and digital services, and land set aside for government offices and businesses. How quickly it grows into a full city, and whether it achieves its goals, will be watched closely by urban planners."],
g:[["Smart city","A city that uses digital technology and data to improve services"],["Internet of Things","Everyday devices connected to the internet"],["Open data","Public information shared freely online"],["Surveillance","Close monitoring of people’s activities"]],
f:["Singapore launched its Smart Nation initiative in 2014.","New Clark City is a planned city in Tarlac.","Smart cities raise privacy debates."],
fun:"Some smart street lights dim automatically when no one is around and brighten when a person or car approaches."});

ADDART({id:"overtourism",t:"Overtourism",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🧳",w:"Overtourism",r:["tourism","megacities","reef","costliving","housing"],
p:[["Too many visitors","Overtourism happens when too many tourists crowd a place, harming it and its residents."],["Boracay’s closure","The Philippines closed Boracay for six months in 2018 to rehabilitate it."],["Finding balance","Places try limits, fees and better planning to manage visitors."]],
l:"Overtourism describes a situation in which a destination receives more visitors than it can handle, damaging the environment, overloading local services and harming residents’ quality of life. Crowded streets, rising rents, pollution, traffic and damaged natural sites are common complaints. The term became widely used in the late 2010s as cheap flights, cruise ships and social media drew huge crowds to popular cities, beaches and landmarks such as Venice, Barcelona, Kyoto and Maya Bay in Thailand. In the Philippines, the island of Boracay was closed to tourists for six months in 2018 to clean up pollution. Places now try visitor limits, entry fees, reservation systems and spreading tourists to less-visited areas, balancing tourism’s economic benefits with its costs.",
s:[["Causes","International tourism grew rapidly in recent decades as incomes rose, budget airlines spread and travel became easier to book online. Social media made certain spots famous overnight, drawing crowds seeking the same photographs. Cruise ships can bring thousands of visitors to a small town at once."],["Effects on places","Too many visitors can damage coral reefs, beaches, trails and historic buildings, and produce more garbage and sewage than local systems can handle. Traffic and crowds make daily life harder for residents. In some cities, homes are converted into short-term vacation rentals, raising rents and pushing local people out."],["Benefits of tourism","Tourism also brings jobs, income and investment, supporting hotels, restaurants, guides, transport and crafts. In the Philippines, tourism employs millions of people and is an important part of the economy. The challenge is to keep these benefits while limiting harm."],["Managing tourism","Destinations use many tools, including daily visitor limits, entry fees, tourist taxes, timed tickets, bans on new hotels, restrictions on short-term rentals and cruise ships, and campaigns encouraging visitors to travel off-season or to lesser-known places."]],
k:[["Term popular since","Late 2010s"],["Boracay closure","April to October 2018"],["Famous examples","Venice, Barcelona, Kyoto, Maya Bay"],["Common tools","Visitor limits, fees, reservations"],["Related idea","Sustainable tourism"]],
d:[["2017","Residents in Barcelona and Venice protest against mass tourism"],["2018","Boracay is closed to tourists for six months"],["2018","Thailand closes Maya Bay to let its ecosystem recover"],["2022","Maya Bay reopens with visitor limits"],["2024","Venice begins charging day-trippers an entry fee"],["2024","Residents in Spain hold large protests against mass tourism"]],
s2:[["Boracay’s rehabilitation","Boracay, famous for its white sand beaches, attracted millions of visitors a year. By 2018, problems with sewage, illegal construction and water pollution had become severe, and President Rodrigo Duterte described the island as a cesspool. The government closed it to tourists from 26 April to 26 October 2018. Sewage systems were repaired, illegal structures removed and rules introduced on visitor numbers and beach use. Supporters praised the cleanup, while some workers and businesses suffered from lost income during the closure."],["Maya Bay","Maya Bay in Thailand became world-famous after appearing in the 2000 film The Beach. Thousands of visitors a day damaged its coral reefs. Thailand closed the bay in 2018, and when it reopened in 2022, boats were banned from the bay itself, swimming was prohibited and visitor numbers were limited. Reef sharks returned to the waters."],["Venice","Venice, a city of canals in Italy, has seen its resident population shrink while millions of tourists visit each year. Large cruise ships were banned from the city center in 2021, and in 2024 Venice began testing an entry fee for day visitors on busy days. Some residents welcomed the measures, while others argued they were not enough."],["Protecting natural wonders","Many natural sites limit visitors to protect them. In the Philippines, places such as Tubbataha Reefs, the Puerto Princesa Underground River and some beaches and dive sites require permits or have daily limits. Bhutan charges international visitors a daily sustainable development fee to keep tourism low-impact."],["Residents versus tourists","In cities such as Barcelona, Amsterdam and Lisbon, residents have protested against crowds, noisy party tourism and rising rents linked to holiday rentals. Some cities have restricted short-term rentals or banned new tourist accommodation in central areas. Tourism businesses argue that such measures cost jobs, showing the competing interests involved."],["Being a responsible traveler","Travelers can help by visiting in less crowded seasons, exploring lesser-known places, staying in locally owned accommodation, following local rules, respecting residents’ privacy, avoiding littering, not touching coral or wildlife and supporting local businesses and crafts."]],
sp:["Six months without tourists","When Boracay closed in April 2018, the island fell quiet for the first time in decades. Bulldozers removed buildings that had been built too close to the shore, workers dug new drainage and sewage pipes, and the main road was widened. Some families who depended on tourism struggled without income, and the government offered aid. When the island reopened in October, visitors found cleaner water, a beach without crowds of vendors, and new rules banning smoking, drinking and parties on the beach. Officials set limits on the number of tourists and hotel rooms, aiming to keep the island from becoming overcrowded again."],
g:[["Overtourism","Too many tourists harming a place and its residents"],["Sustainable tourism","Tourism that protects the environment and local communities"],["Carrying capacity","The number of visitors a place can handle without damage"],["Short-term rental","A home rented to visitors for short stays"]],
f:["Boracay was closed for six months in 2018.","Venice charges some day visitors an entry fee.","Maya Bay reopened in 2022 with visitor limits."],
fun:"After Thailand closed Maya Bay to tourists, groups of blacktip reef sharks returned to swim in its shallow waters."});

ADDART({id:"financialliteracy",t:"Financial Literacy",c:"Economics and Society",i:"💰",w:"Financial literacy",r:["money","costliving","digitalpay","onlinescams","inflation"],
p:[["Money skills","Financial literacy is the knowledge and skills needed to manage money wisely."],["Saving and budgeting","It includes budgeting, saving, borrowing carefully and avoiding scams."],["Starting young","Learning about money early helps people make better choices throughout life."]],
l:"Financial literacy is the knowledge, skills and confidence needed to make good decisions about money. It includes understanding how to earn, budget, save, borrow, invest and protect money, as well as concepts such as interest, inflation, risk and debt. Financially literate people are better able to handle emergencies, avoid costly loans and scams, plan for education, housing and retirement, and reach their goals. Surveys around the world, including in the Philippines, have found that many adults struggle with basic financial ideas. Governments, schools, banks and community groups now teach financial literacy, which has become especially important as digital banking, e-wallets and online lending grow.",
s:[["Budgeting","A budget is a plan for how to use money. It lists income and expenses, such as food, rent, transport, school costs and bills, and sets aside money for savings. Tracking spending helps people see where their money goes and avoid spending more than they earn. Many people use notebooks, envelopes or phone apps to budget."],["Saving and emergency funds","Saving means setting aside part of one’s income for future needs. Experts recommend building an emergency fund to cover several months of basic expenses, so that illness, job loss or disasters do not force people into debt. Savings kept in a bank or digital account are safer than cash at home."],["Interest and debt","When people borrow money, they usually pay interest, an extra charge for using the lender’s money. When they save in a bank, they may earn interest. Compound interest means interest is earned or charged on previous interest, so savings can grow over time but debts can also grow quickly if unpaid."],["Investing","Investing means putting money into assets such as stocks, bonds, mutual funds, businesses or property, hoping it will grow. Investments can earn more than savings accounts but carry risk, as their value can go down. Experts advise understanding any investment before putting money in and spreading money across different investments."]],
k:[["Core skills","Budgeting, saving, borrowing wisely, investing"],["Key concept","Compound interest"],["Safety net","Emergency fund"],["Philippine central bank","Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas"],["Major risk","Scams and high-interest debt"]],
d:[["1993","The Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas is established"],["2008","A global financial crisis highlights the dangers of risky borrowing"],["2012","The OECD tests financial literacy among students for the first time"],["2015","The Philippines adopts a National Strategy for Financial Inclusion"],["2020","The COVID-19 pandemic speeds up the use of e-wallets and digital banking"],["2024","The Philippines passes a law against financial account scams"]],
s2:[["Filipino savings traditions","Many Filipinos save through traditions such as the paluwagan, in which a group of friends or coworkers contributes a fixed amount regularly and each member takes turns receiving the whole pot. Others save in alkansya, coin banks often made from bamboo. Cooperatives also help members save and borrow at fair rates."],["The danger of high-interest loans","Informal lenders sometimes charge extremely high interest. The 5-6 lending system, in which a borrower receives 5 pesos and repays 6 within a short period, can trap people in debt. Online lending apps have also been criticized for high charges and for harassing borrowers. Authorities have cracked down on abusive lenders."],["Digital money","E-wallets, online banking and buy now, pay later services have made financial services easier to reach, especially for people without bank accounts. They also bring new risks, such as overspending, hidden fees and scams. Financial literacy includes understanding digital tools, protecting passwords and one-time PINs and reading terms carefully."],["Remittances and planning","Millions of Filipino families receive money from relatives working abroad. Financial advisers encourage families to budget remittances, save a portion, invest in education or small businesses and plan for when the overseas worker returns home, rather than spending everything on immediate needs."],["Teaching money skills","Financial literacy is increasingly taught in schools, through lessons on budgeting, saving and entrepreneurship. The Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas and other institutions run programs for students, teachers, overseas workers and communities. Experts say parents can teach children by giving allowances, encouraging saving and talking openly about money."],["Recognizing scams","Financially literate people are better able to spot investment scams that promise unrealistic returns, such as Ponzi and pyramid schemes, which pay early investors with money from new ones until they collapse. A common rule is that if an offer sounds too good to be true, it probably is. Authorities such as the Securities and Exchange Commission publish warnings about illegal schemes."]],
sp:["The magic of compound interest","Imagine two friends. One starts saving 1,000 pesos a month at age 20 and stops at 30. The other starts at 30 and saves the same amount every month until 60. If both earn a steady return, the friend who started earlier can end up with a similar or even larger amount, despite saving for only ten years, because their money had more time to grow. Each year, interest is earned not only on the money saved but on the interest already earned. This is the power of compound interest, and it is why financial advisers often say the best time to start saving is as early as possible."],
g:[["Budget","A plan for using money"],["Interest","A charge for borrowing money or a payment for saving it"],["Compound interest","Interest earned on both the original amount and past interest"],["Paluwagan","A Filipino rotating savings group"]],
f:["A budget helps people plan their spending.","Compound interest helps savings grow.","Paluwagan is a Filipino savings tradition."],
fun:"Albert Einstein is often quoted calling compound interest the eighth wonder of the world, though there is no evidence he actually said it."});

ADDART({id:"deepfakes",t:"Deepfakes",c:"Technology and Society",i:"🎭",w:"Deepfake",r:["misinformation","aiart","dataprivacy","socialmedia","onlinescams"],
p:[["Fake but realistic","Deepfakes are fake videos, images or audio made with artificial intelligence."],["Hard to spot","They can make it look as if real people said or did things they never did."],["Risks and responses","Deepfakes are used in scams, harassment and misinformation, and laws and tools are being developed in response."]],
l:"A deepfake is a video, image or audio recording that has been created or altered using artificial intelligence to make it appear that a real person said or did something they never did. The word combines deep learning, a type of AI, and fake. Deepfakes first spread online in 2017, and the technology has since become much easier to use and more realistic. They can be used for entertainment, satire, filmmaking and education, but also for scams, fraud, harassment, fake sexual images of real people and political misinformation. In the Philippines, a deepfake audio clip of President Ferdinand Marcos Jr. circulated in 2024. Governments, technology companies and researchers are developing laws, detection tools and labels to address the harms.",
s:[["How deepfakes are made","Deepfake systems learn from many images, videos or recordings of a person and then generate new content that mimics their face, voice and movements. Early methods used generative adversarial networks, in which two AI systems compete to make increasingly convincing fakes. Newer tools can produce realistic video or clone a voice from just a short sample."],["Harmful uses","Criminals use deepfake voices and videos to impersonate company bosses, relatives or celebrities to trick people into sending money. Deepfakes are also used to create fake intimate images of real people without consent, overwhelmingly targeting women, causing serious harm. Fake videos of politicians can spread false information, especially during elections."],["Positive uses","The same technology can be used in films to de-age actors or dub speech into other languages, in museums to bring historical figures to life, and in medicine to help people who have lost their voices speak with a synthetic version of their own voice. Satirists and artists also use it creatively, ideally with clear labels."],["Spotting deepfakes","Signs of a deepfake can include unnatural blinking, mismatched lighting or shadows, blurry edges around faces, odd mouth movements or voices with strange rhythm. However, deepfakes are improving, so experts advise checking the source, looking for reports from trusted news organizations and being cautious with shocking clips."]],
k:[["Word from","Deep learning plus fake"],["First spread","2017"],["Common harms","Scams, fake intimate images, misinformation"],["Philippine example","Fake audio of the President, 2024"],["Responses","Laws, detection tools, labels"]],
d:[["2014","Generative adversarial networks are invented"],["2017","The term deepfake appears online"],["2019","A deepfake voice is reportedly used to trick a company into transferring money"],["2024","A deepfake audio of President Marcos Jr. circulates and is flagged by the government"],["2024","The European Union’s AI Act requires labeling of deepfakes"],["2025","The United States passes a law against sharing nonconsensual intimate deepfakes"]],
s2:[["The Philippine deepfake audio","In April 2024, an audio clip circulated online in which a voice resembling President Ferdinand Marcos Jr. appeared to order military action. The Presidential Communications Office declared it a deepfake, stating that the President never made such remarks, and authorities ordered an investigation. The incident raised concern about how AI could be used to spread false information on sensitive issues."],["Elections and deepfakes","Election authorities in many countries worry that deepfakes could mislead voters, for example with fake videos of candidates or fake robocalls. Some countries require AI-generated political content to be labeled or ban deceptive deepfakes near elections. Others warn that genuine recordings might be dismissed as fake, a problem sometimes called the liar’s dividend."],["Protecting people from abuse","Many countries are passing laws against creating or sharing sexual deepfakes of real people without consent. In the Philippines, existing laws against voyeurism, cybercrime and violence against women may apply, and lawmakers have proposed specific deepfake legislation. Victims are encouraged to report abuse to platforms and authorities."],["Technology responses","Technology companies are developing systems to detect deepfakes and to add watermarks or tamper-proof labels to content showing whether it was made or edited with AI. Some cameras and apps record information about where and how a photo was taken. No method is perfect, so experts stress education and critical thinking."],["Voice cloning scams","Scammers have used cloned voices of family members claiming to be in trouble and needing money urgently. Police and banks advise people to hang up and call the person directly using a known number, and some families agree on a secret code word to confirm identity in emergencies."],["Free expression concerns","Some civil liberties groups caution that laws against deepfakes must be carefully written so they do not restrict legitimate satire, parody, art or journalism. Lawmakers try to balance protecting people from harm with protecting free speech."]],
sp:["Think before you share","Imagine scrolling through social media and seeing a video of a famous person saying something shocking. Before sharing it, fact-checkers suggest pausing to ask a few questions. Who posted it first? Is it reported by trusted news organizations? Does the person’s voice or face look slightly unnatural? Are there signs it was edited? Searching for the clip along with words like fake or fact check can reveal whether it has been debunked. Because false content often spreads faster than corrections, taking a moment to check before sharing can help stop the spread of deepfakes and other misinformation."],
g:[["Deepfake","Fake media created with artificial intelligence"],["Deep learning","A type of AI that learns patterns from large amounts of data"],["Voice cloning","Copying a person’s voice using AI"],["Liar’s dividend","Using the existence of deepfakes to deny real evidence"]],
f:["Deepfake combines deep learning and fake.","Deepfakes are used in scams and misinformation.","Check the source before sharing shocking videos."],
fun:"The word deepfake came from the username of an online account that shared AI-altered videos in 2017."});

ADDART({id:"onlinescams",t:"Online Scams",c:"Technology and Society",i:"🎣",w:"Internet fraud",r:["cybersecurity","dataprivacy","digitalpay","financialliteracy","deepfakes","consumerrights"],
p:[["Tricks for money","Online scams are tricks that use the internet, texts or calls to steal money or personal information."],["Many forms","They include phishing messages, fake online sellers, investment fraud and romance scams."],["Staying safe","Never share passwords or one-time PINs, and verify before you click or pay."]],
l:"Online scams are dishonest schemes that use the internet, text messages, phone calls, social media or apps to trick people into giving away money or personal information. Common types include phishing messages that pretend to come from banks or delivery companies, fake online shops, investment schemes promising high returns, romance scams, fake job offers and impersonation of family members or officials. Scammers often create a sense of urgency, fear or excitement to make victims act without thinking. Online scams cost people billions of dollars every year worldwide and have grown with digital payments. In the Philippines, laws such as the SIM Registration Act and the Anti-Financial Account Scamming Act aim to fight fraud, while awareness remains the best protection.",
s:[["Phishing and smishing","Phishing messages are emails, texts or chats that pretend to come from trusted organizations, such as banks, e-wallets, government agencies or couriers, and ask people to click a link, log in or share details. When sent by text message, they are called smishing. The links often lead to fake websites that steal passwords and one-time PINs."],["Fake sellers and online shopping","Scammers set up fake online shops or social media pages offering popular products at very low prices. After buyers pay, the goods never arrive. Others pose as buyers and send fake proof of payment. Using trusted platforms, checking reviews and paying through secure methods reduces risk."],["Investment and romance scams","Investment scams promise guaranteed high returns, often involving cryptocurrency or trading, and use fake testimonials. In romance scams, criminals build online relationships over weeks or months, then ask for money for emergencies or investments. Some combine both, in schemes sometimes called pig butchering."],["Warning signs","Common red flags include messages creating urgency, such as your account will be locked; requests for passwords, PINs or OTPs; offers that seem too good to be true; pressure to pay by unusual methods; poor spelling; and links with strange web addresses. Legitimate banks never ask customers to share their one-time PINs."]],
k:[["Common type","Phishing"],["Text message phishing","Smishing"],["Philippine SIM law","SIM Registration Act, 2022"],["Philippine anti-scam law","Anti-Financial Account Scamming Act, 2024"],["Golden rule","Never share your OTP or password"]],
d:[["1990s","Email scams spread with the growth of the internet"],["2012","The Philippines passes the Cybercrime Prevention Act"],["2022","The Philippines passes the SIM Registration Act"],["2023","A United Nations report warns that hundreds of thousands of people are forced to work in scam centers in Southeast Asia"],["2024","The Philippines passes the Anti-Financial Account Scamming Act"],["2024","The Philippines announces a ban on offshore gaming operators linked to scams and crime"]],
s2:[["Scams in the Philippines","Filipinos have reported large numbers of fake text messages, often containing links or offers of prizes and jobs. In response, the SIM Registration Act of 2022 required all mobile phone SIM cards to be registered with users’ identities, aiming to make scammers easier to trace. Critics questioned its effectiveness and raised privacy concerns, and scammers shifted to other methods."],["The anti-scam law","The Anti-Financial Account Scamming Act, signed in 2024, made it a crime to act as a money mule, allowing one’s bank or e-wallet account to be used to receive stolen funds, and to engage in social engineering schemes to steal account information. It also gives banks and regulators more power to freeze suspicious accounts."],["Scam compounds","Investigations have revealed large scam centers in parts of Southeast Asia, where workers, some of them trafficking victims lured by fake job offers, are forced to run online scams targeting people around the world. In the Philippines, raids on some offshore gaming operations uncovered scam activities, and in 2024 the government announced a ban on offshore gaming operators."],["Protecting yourself","Experts recommend never sharing passwords or one-time PINs, enabling two-factor authentication, typing website addresses directly instead of clicking links, verifying requests by calling official numbers, keeping apps updated and being cautious about what personal information is shared online."],["Helping family members","Older people and those new to digital technology are often targeted. Family members can help by explaining common scams, setting up security features and encouraging relatives to ask before responding to unusual messages or calls. Young people are also targeted through gaming, social media and fake job offers."],["What to do if scammed","Victims should immediately contact their bank or e-wallet provider to try to freeze transactions, change passwords, report the scam to authorities such as the cybercrime units of the police and the National Bureau of Investigation, and warn others. Reporting helps authorities track scammers, even if money cannot always be recovered."]],
sp:["Congratulations, you have won!","A text message arrives: Congratulations! Your number has won 50,000 pesos in a raffle. Click this link to claim your prize. It looks exciting, but there was no raffle. Clicking the link leads to a page asking for bank or e-wallet details, or a small processing fee, and then the one-time PIN sent to the phone. Once the scammer has these, they can empty the account within minutes. Such messages are among the most common scams in the Philippines. The safest response is simple: do not click, do not reply, and delete or report the message."],
g:[["Phishing","Tricking people into giving information by pretending to be trustworthy"],["Smishing","Phishing through text messages"],["OTP","One-time PIN, a temporary code used to confirm transactions"],["Money mule","A person whose account is used to move stolen money"]],
f:["Banks never ask for your one-time PIN.","Smishing is phishing by text message.","The Philippines passed an anti-scam law in 2024."],
fun:"The word phishing comes from fishing, because scammers throw out bait hoping someone will bite."});

ADDART({id:"sealevel",t:"Sea Level Rise",c:"Environment",i:"🌊",w:"Sea level rise",r:["climchange","glaciers","pacific","megacities","extremeheat","tuvalucountry"],
p:[["Rising oceans","Global sea levels are rising as the planet warms."],["Two main causes","Melting ice and warming seawater, which expands, both raise sea levels."],["Coasts at risk","Low-lying islands and coastal cities face flooding and erosion."]],
l:"Sea level rise is the increase in the average level of the world’s oceans. Since 1900, global sea level has risen by roughly 20 centimeters, and the rate of rise has been speeding up. The main causes are the melting of glaciers and the great ice sheets of Greenland and Antarctica, and the expansion of seawater as it warms. Rising seas increase coastal flooding, erosion, storm surges and the intrusion of salt water into farmland and drinking water. Low-lying island nations and coastal cities, including many in Southeast Asia, are especially at risk. In parts of the Philippines, such as around Manila Bay, local sea levels are rising faster than the global average, partly because the land is sinking.",
s:[["Why seas are rising","As Earth warms, glaciers and ice sheets on land melt and add water to the oceans. At the same time, seawater expands as it warms, a process called thermal expansion. Together these have caused most of the observed rise. Floating sea ice, by contrast, does not raise sea level much when it melts."],["Measuring sea level","Scientists measure sea level using tide gauges along coasts, some with records going back more than a century, and satellites that bounce radar off the ocean surface. Since satellite measurements began in 1993, the rate of global rise has roughly doubled."],["Future projections","How much seas rise this century depends mainly on how much greenhouse gas emissions are reduced. The Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change has projected a likely rise of roughly 30 centimeters to one meter by 2100, depending on emissions, with larger increases possible if ice sheets become unstable. Sea levels will continue rising for centuries."],["Effects on coasts","Higher seas make storm surges more dangerous, cause more frequent high-tide flooding, wear away beaches and push salt water into rivers, wells and rice fields. Mangroves and coral reefs, which protect coasts, can be harmed if seas rise faster than they can adapt."]],
k:[["Rise since 1900","Roughly 20 centimeters"],["Main causes","Melting ice and warming water"],["Measured by","Tide gauges and satellites"],["Projected rise by 2100","Roughly 0.3 to 1 meter, depending on emissions"],["Philippine hotspot","Manila Bay area"]],
d:[["1880","Reliable global sea level estimates begin"],["1993","Satellites begin precise measurements of sea level"],["2013","Typhoon Haiyan’s storm surge devastates Tacloban"],["2019","An IPCC report focuses on oceans and frozen regions"],["2021","An IPCC report projects continued sea level rise for centuries"],["2023","Tuvalu and Australia agree a treaty offering residency to Tuvaluans affected by climate change"]],
s2:[["Sinking land in the Philippines","In some coastal areas of Metro Manila, Bulacan and Pampanga, the land itself is sinking, a process called subsidence, mainly because large amounts of groundwater are pumped out. Combined with rising seas, this makes flooding worse. Some towns in Bulacan now flood regularly at high tide, and residents have raised floors and roads."],["Storm surges","Storm surges, walls of seawater pushed ashore by strong typhoon winds, become more dangerous as sea levels rise. In 2013 Typhoon Haiyan, known locally as Yolanda, drove a storm surge of several meters into Tacloban and other towns in Leyte and Samar, causing thousands of deaths. Higher seas mean future surges can reach farther inland."],["Island nations","Low-lying island nations such as Tuvalu, Kiribati, the Marshall Islands and the Maldives have much of their land only a few meters above sea level. Their leaders have warned that rising seas threaten their existence and have called for strong global action. In 2023 Australia and Tuvalu signed a treaty offering Tuvaluans a pathway to live in Australia."],["Big cities at risk","Many of the world’s largest cities lie on coasts or river deltas, including Manila, Jakarta, Bangkok, Ho Chi Minh City, Shanghai, Mumbai, Lagos and New York. Jakarta, which is both sinking and facing rising seas, is one reason Indonesia decided to build a new capital city on the island of Borneo."],["Adapting to rising seas","Coastal communities adapt by building sea walls and dikes, raising homes and roads, restoring mangroves and wetlands, improving drainage, limiting groundwater pumping and, in some cases, moving away from the most exposed areas. The Netherlands, much of which lies below sea level, is famous for its long history of managing water."],["Debates over solutions","Hard defenses such as sea walls can protect cities but are expensive and may damage natural coastlines. Some communities debate whether to defend, adapt or relocate. In Manila Bay, land reclamation projects have been supported for economic development and criticized by environmental groups concerned about flooding and ecosystems."]],
sp:["The village where the tide comes in","In some coastal villages of Bulacan, north of Manila, the sea now enters homes during high tides even on sunny days. Residents have raised their floors with concrete and rubble, built walkways of planks and moved belongings to upper levels. Churches and schools have been elevated, and some families travel by boat along streets that were once dry. The causes include both rising seas and land sinking because of groundwater pumping. Researchers study these communities to understand how people adapt to living with water, and what could happen in other coastal areas in the coming decades."],
g:[["Sea level rise","The increase in the average level of the oceans"],["Thermal expansion","The expansion of water as it warms"],["Subsidence","The gradual sinking of land"],["Storm surge","A rise of seawater pushed ashore by a storm"]],
f:["Global sea level has risen about 20 cm since 1900.","Warming water expands and raises sea level.","Parts of Manila Bay are sinking."],
fun:"If all the ice on Greenland melted, global sea level would rise by about 7 meters, though this would take many centuries."});

